nd coastal residents can anticipate glowing nights in advance[14]. [15]If the tool succeeds, it would mark one of the first times traditional local knowledge and satellite data have been combined this directly in marine science[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: The overlap between his observations and her data was strong enough, she says, to surprise even her most skeptical colleagues]',
    options: [
      'The overlap between his observations and her data was strong enough, she says, to shock even her most skeptical colleagues',
      'The overlap between his observations and her data was strong enough, she says, to astonish even her most skeptical colleagues',
      'NO CHANGE',
      'The overlap between his observations and her data was strong enough, she says, to startle even her most skeptical colleagues',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "surprise" is the most fitting word for describing skeptical colleagues encountering unexpectedly strong data, matching the measured, academic tone of the passage. "Astonish" is a plausible near-synonym but implies a more overwhelming reaction than the context of careful scientific skepticism supports. "Shock" carries a connotation of distress or alarm that does not fit a description of scientific surprise at supportive data. "Startle" typically describes a sudden, often physical reaction to an unexpected stimulus, which is a poor fit for colleagues reacting to a data comparison. ACT Tip: For vocabulary-in-context questions, test each near-synonym by considering its specific connotation, not just its dictionary definition, against the passage\'s tone.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-english-pow-035',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.english,
