    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Scientist 2\'s specific objection to the volcanic explanation centers on the difficulty of reconciling a rapid extinction onset with volcanic eruptions occurring over "roughly one million years"; if new evidence showed the eruptions actually occurred over a much shorter period of ten thousand years, this specific timescale objection would become substantially weaker, since a shorter eruption period is easier to reconcile with a rapid extinction, actually making this new information more favorable to Scientist 1\'s argument rather than Scientist 2\'s. The total duration of the eruptions is directly relevant to Scientist 2\'s specific timescale-based objection to Scientist 1\'s argument, so it would not have no effect. This new information would not clearly weaken Scientist 1\'s overall argument; the core mechanisms proposed, carbon dioxide warming and sulfur dioxide acid rain, do not depend specifically on a one-million-year timescale, and a shorter, more intense eruption period could still produce comparable atmospheric effects. This new information alone would not conclusively prove an asteroid impact caused the extinction; it addresses only one specific point of contention between the two arguments, the volcanic timescale, not the full body of evidence either scientist presents. ACT Tip: When evaluating how new information would affect a Conflicting Viewpoints argument, identify precisely which specific claim or objection the new information relates to, rather than assuming it broadly helps or harms an entire argument.',
