    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'The passage notes that at 65 C, the solution "appeared to be decomposing on its own even before the catalyst was added," meaning some hydrogen peroxide had already broken down or evaporated before the timed measurement began, leaving less available to react and produce measurable gas during the actual 60-second interval, combined with the possibility that extreme heat can reduce an enzyme-independent catalyst\'s structural effectiveness. The data clearly shows MnO2 continued functioning as a catalyst well above 22 C, producing increasing oxygen volumes up to 50 C, directly contradicting the claim that it stops functioning above that temperature. The passage does not discuss oxygen solubility or measurement device limitations at all; the researchers\' note specifically attributes the change to premature decomposition, not to a detection problem. The passage explicitly states the same solution volume was used as in Experiment 1 across all trials, contradicting the claim that a smaller volume was used at 65 C specifically. ACT Tip: When a passage includes a researcher\'s own explanatory note about an unexpected result, use that note as the primary basis for interpreting the data pattern rather than proposing an unrelated explanation the passage does not support.',
