  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-011',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Researchers studying urban wildlife measured the average ambient noise level (in decibels, dB) and the average minimum song frequency (in kilohertz, kHz) of a common city-dwelling songbird species at six locations with varying levels of traffic noise. Higher minimum song frequencies allow birdsong to be heard above lower-pitched traffic noise. Figure 1 presents the results as a set of paired measurements.\n\nFigure 1: Ambient Noise Level and Minimum Song Frequency by Location\n\nLocation 1 (quiet park, far from roads): Noise level 42 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.1 kHz\nLocation 2 (residential street, light traffic): Noise level 51 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.4 kHz\nLocation 3 (near a two-lane road): Noise level 58 dB, Minimum song frequency 2.9 kHz\nLocation 4 (near a four-lane road): Noise level 66 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.5 kHz\nLocation 5 (downtown commercial street): Noise level 72 dB, Minimum song frequency 3.9 kHz\nLocation 6 (highway overpass): Noise level 79 dB, Minimum song frequency 4.4 kHz\n\nThe researchers also noted that the same songbird species living in the quiet park location, when recorded during a rare nighttime road closure that temporarily reduced noise to 38 dB, sang at a minimum frequency of 2.0 kHz, close to its typical daytime frequency at that location.',
    questionText: 'The nighttime road closure data described in the passage is best interpreted as evidence that:',
    options: [
      'the songbird species cannot sing at all when noise levels fall below 40 dB',
      'the songbird species sings at a fixed minimum frequency regardless of ambient noise level',
      'nighttime and daytime noise levels are always identical at every location',
      'the songbird\'s minimum song frequency tends to decrease when ambient noise level decreases, supporting the idea that noise level influences song frequency rather than location alone',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'When noise dropped from the park\'s typical 42 dB to 38 dB during the road closure, minimum song frequency also dropped slightly, from 2.1 kHz to 2.0 kHz, suggesting the bird\'s frequency responds to actual noise level rather than being fixed by location alone, since the location itself did not change. The data shows frequency did change slightly with the noise reduction, directly contradicting the claim that frequency remains fixed regardless of noise level. The passage states the bird was recorded singing during the closure, directly contradicting the claim that it cannot sing below 40 dB. The passage describes the road closure as producing an unusually low, non-typical noise level specifically because it was rare, directly contradicting the idea that nighttime and daytime levels are always identical. ACT Tip: A natural experiment, such as a temporary noise reduction at an otherwise constant location, can help isolate whether a variable like noise level, rather than location itself, is driving an observed pattern.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's01-science-emi-004',
    setNumber: 1,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Marine biologists monitored four coral reef sites over a five-year period, recording the average sea surface temperature (in degrees Celsius) during the warmest month of each year and the percentage of coral colonies at each site showing visible signs of bleaching (a stress response in which corals expel the algae that give them color and nutrients). Table 1 shows the results.\n\nTable 1: Average Warmest-Month Sea Surface Temperature and Percent Coral Bleaching by Site and Year\n\nSite A (shallow, high wave action):\nYear 1: Temperature 28.2 C, Bleaching 4%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.5 C, Bleaching 6%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.6 C, Bleaching 22%\nYear 4: Temperature 29.1 C, Bleaching 15%\nYear 5: Temperature 30.3 C, Bleaching 41%\n\nSite B (deep, sheltered):\nYear 1: Temperature 27.9 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.1 C, Bleaching 3%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.0 C, Bleaching 9%\nYear 4: Temperature 28.7 C, Bleaching 7%\nYear 5: Temperature 29.8 C, Bleaching 19%\n\nSite C (shallow, sheltered):\nYear 1: Temperature 28.4 C, Bleaching 5%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.6 C, Bleaching 8%\nYear 3: Temperature 29.8 C, Bleaching 28%\nYear 4: Temperature 29.3 C, Bleaching 18%\nYear 5: Temperature 30.5 C, Bleaching 47%\n\nSite D (deep, high wave action):\nYear 1: Temperature 27.8 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 2: Temperature 28.0 C, Bleaching 2%\nYear 3: Temperature 28.9 C, Bleaching 8%\nYear 4: Temperature 28.6 C, Bleaching 6%\nYear 5: Temperature 29.6 C, Bleaching 17%\n\nResearchers also noted that scientists generally consider 29.5 C to be a critical threshold above which bleaching risk increases sharply at reef sites in this region.',
    questionText: 'Based on Table 1, which of the following statements is best supported by a comparison between Site A and Site B?',
    options: [
      'Wave action has no relationship to coral bleaching regardless of depth',
      'Shallow, high wave action sites experience less coral bleaching than deep, sheltered sites at similar temperatures',
      'Deep, sheltered sites experience less coral bleaching than shallow, high wave action sites at similar temperatures',
      'Sheltered sites always have higher sea surface temperatures than sites with high wave action',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'Comparing Site A (shallow, high wave action) and Site B (deep, sheltered) across years with similar temperatures, such as Year 3 (Site A: 29.6 C, 22% bleaching; Site B: 29.0 C, 9% bleaching) and Year 5 (Site A: 30.3 C, 41%; Site B: 29.8 C, 19%), Site B consistently shows lower bleaching percentages despite comparable or only slightly lower temperatures, supporting this statement. The first option reverses this relationship; Site A (shallow, high wave action) shows higher, not lower, bleaching than Site B in every comparable year. The data shows a clear pattern relating wave action and depth characteristics to differing bleaching rates, contradicting the claim that wave action has no relationship to bleaching. Site B\'s temperatures (27.9 C to 29.8 C) are, in fact, generally lower than Site A\'s temperatures (28.2 C to 30.3 C) across the five years, contradicting the claim that sheltered sites always have higher temperatures. ACT Tip: When comparing two sites, look for years where their temperatures are closest together to isolate the effect of the other variable, such as depth or wave action, being tested.',
  ),
];


