const List<ActQuestion> englishSet2 = [
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-cse-001',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'In 2020, a rural hospital network in western Nebraska launched a telemedicine program aimed at reducing the long drives many patients faced to see a specialist. [1]Before the program began, patients living more than ninety miles from the nearest hospital regularly misses appointments simply because of the distance[1]. Dr. Elena Vasquez, the network\'s chief medical officer, [2]had spent years watching patients delay care until conditions worsened into emergencies[2]. [3]The telemedicine program paired rural clinics with video equipment, allowing patients to consult specialists in real time without leaving their hometown[3]. Nurses at each rural clinic were trained to take vital signs and relay them to the specialist during the call, [4]essentially serving as the physician\'s hands while the physician served as the diagnostic mind[4]. [5]Early results were promising, missed specialist appointments dropped by nearly forty percent within the first year[5]. [6]Vasquez attributes much of the program\'s success to the trust rural patients already had in their local clinic staff[6]. Patients who might have hesitated to speak openly with an unfamiliar specialist in a distant city [7]felt comfortable describing their symptoms with a familiar nurse standing beside them[7]. [8]The program did face early obstacles, particularly around internet connectivity in the most remote clinics[8]. [9]Two clinics, located in areas without reliable broadband, initially could not maintain a stable enough connection for video consultations[9]. [10]The network eventually solved this problem by installing satellite internet equipment, an expensive but ultimately effective fix[10]. [11]Cardiologists, dermatologists, and psychiatrists have proven to be the specialists most requested through the program[11]. [12]Psychiatric consultations, in particular, have grown rapidly, reflecting a broader shortage of mental health providers across rural America[12]. [13]Vasquez notes that video consultations, while imperfect, have allowed patients to receive care they would have otherwise gone without entirely[13]. [14]Some skeptics worry that telemedicine could eventually replace in-person care too broadly, reducing the quality of the doctor-patient relationship[14]. [15]Vasquez responds that the program was never intended to replace in-person visits, only to fill gaps that distance had long left unfilled[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Before the program began, patients living more than ninety miles from the nearest hospital regularly misses appointments simply because of the distance]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'regularly missed appointments simply because of the distance',
      'regularly missing appointments simply because of the distance',
      'regularly miss appointments simply because of the distance',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The sentence describes a repeated past situation, established by "Before the program began," so the verb should be in past tense: "missed." NO CHANGE incorrectly uses "misses," a present-tense singular verb that does not match the past-tense context. "Missing" removes the finite verb and leaves the sentence without a complete predicate. "Miss," while past-tense adjacent in form, is actually present tense and plural-agreeing but still fails to match the clearly established past-tense narrative frame of the sentence. ACT Tip: When an introductory phrase like "Before the program began" establishes a past time frame, the verb in the main clause must match that same past tense.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-pow-002',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In 2020, a rural hospital network in western Nebraska launched a telemedicine program aimed at reducing the long drives many patients faced to see a specialist. [1]Before the program began, patients living more than ninety miles from the nearest hospital regularly misses appointments simply because of the distance[1]. Dr. Elena Vasquez, the network\'s chief medical officer, [2]had spent years watching patients delay care until conditions worsened into emergencies[2]. [3]The telemedicine program paired rural clinics with video equipment, allowing patients to consult specialists in real time without leaving their hometown[3]. Nurses at each rural clinic were trained to take vital signs and relay them to the specialist during the call, [4]essentially serving as the physician\'s hands while the physician served as the diagnostic mind[4]. [5]Early results were promising, missed specialist appointments dropped by nearly forty percent within the first year[5]. [6]Vasquez attributes much of the program\'s success to the trust rural patients already had in their local clinic staff[6]. Patients who might have hesitated to speak openly with an unfamiliar specialist in a distant city [7]felt comfortable describing their symptoms with a familiar nurse standing beside them[7]. [8]The program did face early obstacles, particularly around internet connectivity in the most remote clinics[8]. [9]Two clinics, located in areas without reliable broadband, initially could not maintain a stable enough connection for video consultations[9]. [10]The network eventually solved this problem by installing satellite internet equipment, an expensive but ultimately effective fix[10]. [11]Cardiologists, dermatologists, and psychiatrists have proven to be the specialists most requested through the program[11]. [12]Psychiatric consultations, in particular, have grown rapidly, reflecting a broader shortage of mental health providers across rural America[12]. [13]Vasquez notes that video consultations, while imperfect, have allowed patients to receive care they would have otherwise gone without entirely[13]. [14]Some skeptics worry that telemedicine could eventually replace in-person care too broadly, reducing the quality of the doctor-patient relationship[14]. [15]Vasquez responds that the program was never intended to replace in-person visits, only to fill gaps that distance had long left unfilled[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: had spent years watching patients delay care until conditions worsened into emergencies]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'had spent years watching patients delay care until conditions worsened into emergencies, which was frustrating for her to witness',
      'spent years watching patients delay care until conditions worsened into emergencies',
      'had spent a considerable number of years watching as patients would delay care until such time as conditions worsened into emergencies',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the past perfect "had spent" correctly signals that this pattern of observation occurred before the telemedicine program began, and the sentence is already clear and appropriately concise. The second option adds the unnecessary, redundant editorializing phrase "which was frustrating for her to witness," which is not needed to convey the sentence\'s point. Switching to simple past "spent" loses the past perfect\'s signal that this observation predates the program\'s launch described in the surrounding sentences. The fourth option is needlessly wordy, replacing "years" with "a considerable number of years" and "until" with "until such time as," adding words without adding meaning. ACT Tip: Past perfect tense signals that one past event happened before another past event already established in the passage; switching to simple past can blur that sequence.',
  ),
