    questionText: '[underlined: The telemedicine program paired rural clinics with video equipment, allowing patients to consult specialists in real time without leaving their hometown]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'The telemedicine program paired rural clinics with video equipment; allowing patients to consult specialists in real time without leaving their hometown',
      'The telemedicine program paired rural clinics with video equipment allowing patients to consult specialists in real time without leaving their hometown',
      'The telemedicine program paired rural clinics, with video equipment, allowing patients to consult specialists in real time without leaving their hometown',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the comma properly sets off the participial phrase "allowing patients to consult specialists in real time without leaving their hometown," which describes the result of pairing clinics with video equipment. Using a semicolon, as in the second option, is incorrect because "allowing patients to consult specialists in real time without leaving their hometown" is not an independent clause. Removing the comma entirely, as in the third option, fuses the modifying phrase onto the main clause without the needed pause, making the sentence harder to parse. Adding commas around "with video equipment," as in the fourth option, incorrectly treats an essential prepositional phrase as a nonessential aside. ACT Tip: A participial phrase that explains the result or purpose of the main clause\'s action is typically set off by a single comma, not a semicolon.',
