      'essentially serving as the physician\'s hands while the physician serving as the diagnostic mind',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The verb tenses within the sentence should match; since the participle "serving" describes an ongoing role in the present-tense framework the passage uses to describe the program\'s current design, the parallel clause should also use present tense: "serves." NO CHANGE incorrectly pairs the present participle "serving" with the past-tense "served," creating a tense mismatch within a single descriptive sentence. Changing both verbs to past tense, as in the third option, breaks the participial structure of the sentence, since "served" cannot function as the sentence\'s main descriptive verb without a subject and auxiliary. Changing both to "-ing" forms, as in the fourth option, removes the finite verb needed in the second clause, leaving that clause without a complete predicate. ACT Tip: When a sentence describes two parallel roles connected by "while," check that both verbs share a consistent and grammatically appropriate tense.',
