deo consultations, while imperfect, have allowed patients to receive care they would have otherwise gone without entirely[13]. [14]Some skeptics worry that telemedicine could eventually replace in-person care too broadly, reducing the quality of the doctor-patient relationship[14]. [15]Vasquez responds that the program was never intended to replace in-person visits, only to fill gaps that distance had long left unfilled[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: felt comfortable describing their symptoms with a familiar nurse standing beside them]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'felt comfortable describing their symptoms to a familiar nurse standing beside them',
      'felt comfortable describing their symptoms toward a familiar nurse standing beside them',
      'felt comfortable describing their symptoms at a familiar nurse standing beside them',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The idiomatic preposition for describing something to a listener is "to," not "with," which incorrectly suggests joint description rather than the patient speaking to the nurse. NO CHANGE uses the nonstandard "with," which does not correctly convey a patient speaking to a listener. "Toward" suggests physical direction rather than the act of communicating information to a person, an incorrect idiomatic fit. "At" suggests directing something forcefully or briefly at a target rather than the sustained act of describing symptoms to a listening person. ACT Tip: Watch for idiomatic preposition errors, where a word choice may seem plausible but does not match standard English usage in context.',
  ),
