  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-cse-016',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Five years ago, the mid-sized city of Halbrook began redesigning its downtown streets to include a connected network of protected bike lanes, separated from car traffic by physical barriers rather than painted lines alone. [1]City planner Owen Reyes, who led the redesign, believe that painted lines alone rarely convince nervous cyclists to leave their cars at home[1]. Before the redesign, Halbrook\'s bike lanes existed only in scattered, disconnected segments, [2]forcing cyclists to merge into traffic whenever a protected lane abruptly ended[2]. [3]Reyes and his team mapped every gap in the existing network, prioritizing connections between residential neighborhoods and the downtown business district[3]. [4]Construction proceeded in phases, funded partly by a state grant aimed at reducing urban traffic congestion[4]. [5]Local business owners were initially split on the project, some worried that removing parking spaces for the new lanes would hurt their sales[5]. [6]Other business owners, particularly those near university housing, supported the change immediately[6], anticipating that more cyclists would mean more casual foot traffic browsing their storefronts. [7]Three years after construction finished, the city\'s transportation department released data comparing cycling rates before and after the redesign[7]. [8]The data showed a sixty percent increase in daily bicycle trips along the newly connected corridors[8]. [9]Traffic accidents involving cyclists, meanwhile, dropped by nearly a third over the same period[9]. [10]Reyes attributes this improvement primarily to the physical separation between bike lanes and car traffic, rather than to any single other factor[10]. [11]Not every part of the redesign succeeded equally, however, one downtown intersection remained a persistent trouble spot despite the new infrastructure[11]. [12]Cyclists and drivers alike reported confusion at that particular intersection, where the bike lane crossed a busy turning lane[12]. [13]The city ultimately redesigned that single intersection twice more before settling on a configuration that satisfied both cyclists and drivers[13]. [14]Reyes describes this ongoing troubleshooting as an unavoidable part of any major infrastructure project, no design works perfectly the first time[14]. [15]He hopes Halbrook\'s experience, successes and setbacks alike, will offer useful lessons for other mid-sized cities considering similar projects[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: City planner Owen Reyes, who led the redesign, believe that painted lines alone rarely convince nervous cyclists to leave their cars at home]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'City planner Owen Reyes, who led the redesign, believes that painted lines alone rarely convince nervous cyclists to leave their cars at home',
      'City planner Owen Reyes, who led the redesign, believing that painted lines alone rarely convince nervous cyclists to leave their cars at home',
      'City planner Owen Reyes, who led the redesign, have believed that painted lines alone rarely convince nervous cyclists to leave their cars at home',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The singular subject "Owen Reyes" requires the singular verb "believes," not the plural "believe." NO CHANGE incorrectly uses a plural verb form. "Believing" removes the finite verb, creating a sentence fragment. "Have believed" is both plural, again mismatching the singular subject, and shifts unnecessarily into present perfect tense. ACT Tip: A relative clause like "who led the redesign," set off by commas, does not change the number of the subject the main verb must agree with.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-pow-017',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Five years ago, the mid-sized city of Halbrook began redesigning its downtown streets to include a connected network of protected bike lanes, separated from car traffic by physical barriers rather than painted lines alone. [1]City planner Owen Reyes, who led the redesign, believe that painted lines alone rarely convince nervous cyclists to leave their cars at home[1]. Before the redesign, Halbrook\'s bike lanes existed only in scattered, disconnected segments, [2]forcing cyclists to merge into traffic whenever a protected lane abruptly ended[2]. [3]Reyes and his team mapped every gap in the existing network, prioritizing connections between residential neighborhoods and the downtown business district[3]. [4]Construction proceeded in phases, funded partly by a state grant aimed at reducing urban traffic congestion[4]. [5]Local business owners were initially split on the project, some worried that removing parking spaces for the new lanes would hurt their sales[5]. [6]Other business owners, particularly those near university housing, supported the change immediately[6], anticipating that more cyclists would mean more casual foot traffic browsing their storefronts. [7]Three years after construction finished, the city\'s transportation department released data comparing cycling rates before and after the redesign[7]. [8]The data showed a sixty percent increase in daily bicycle trips along the newly connected corridors[8]. [9]Traffic accidents involving cyclists, meanwhile, dropped by nearly a third over the same period[9]. [10]Reyes attributes this improvement primarily to the physical separation between bike lanes and car traffic, rather than to any single other factor[10]. [11]Not every part of the redesign succeeded equally, however, one downtown intersection remained a persistent trouble spot despite the new infrastructure[11]. [12]Cyclists and drivers alike reported confusion at that particular intersection, where the bike lane crossed a busy turning lane[12]. [13]The city ultimately redesigned that single intersection twice more before settling on a configuration that satisfied both cyclists and drivers[13]. [14]Reyes describes this ongoing troubleshooting as an unavoidable part of any major infrastructure project, no design works perfectly the first time[14]. [15]He hopes Halbrook\'s experience, successes and setbacks alike, will offer useful lessons for other mid-sized cities considering similar projects[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: forcing cyclists to merge into traffic whenever a protected lane abruptly ended]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'forcing cyclists to merge into traffic whenever a protected lane would suddenly and abruptly come to an end',
      'which forced cyclists, unfortunately, to merge into traffic whenever a protected lane abruptly ended',
      'forcing cyclists to merge into traffic whenever a protected lane ended',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because it conveys the idea clearly and concisely, with "abruptly" adding a meaningful detail about the suddenness of the disruption. The second option is redundant, pairing "suddenly" and "abruptly," two words expressing nearly the same meaning. The third option adds the unnecessary editorializing word "unfortunately" and shifts to a less direct "which forced" construction. The fourth option cuts "abruptly," losing a meaningful detail that emphasizes how jarring the sudden gaps were for cyclists, which supports the passage\'s argument for a connected network. ACT Tip: A single well-chosen descriptive word, like "abruptly" here, should generally be kept if it adds specific, relevant detail rather than mere padding.',
  ),
