    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Construction proceeded in phases, financed partly by a state grant aimed at reducing urban traffic congestion',
      'Construction proceeded in phases, sponsored partly by a state grant aimed at reducing urban traffic congestion',
      'Construction proceeded in phases, subsidized partly by a state grant aimed at reducing urban traffic congestion',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "funded" is the most natural, direct, and commonly used word for describing money provided to support a public infrastructure project, matching the passage\'s plain, accessible tone. "Financed" is a plausible near-synonym but more often describes providing capital for a purchase or investment expected to generate returns, a less precise fit for a public grant. "Sponsored" typically implies an ongoing promotional or supportive relationship, such as sponsoring an event, rather than the one-time provision of construction funding described here. "Subsidized" specifically implies reducing the cost of something an ongoing consumer would otherwise pay directly, which is a less precise fit for one-time construction funding than "funded." ACT Tip: When choosing among near-synonyms describing financial support, consider whether the word implies an investment, an ongoing partnership, a cost reduction, or, most simply, direct funding, and match it to the specific situation described.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-pow-020',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Five years ago, the mid-sized city of Halbrook began redesigning its downtown streets to include a connected network of protected bike lanes, separated from car traffic by physical barriers rather than painted lines alone. [1]City planner Owen Reyes, who led the redesign, believe that painted lines alone rarely convince nervous cyclists to leave their cars at home[1]. Before the redesign, Halbrook\'s bike lanes existed only in scattered, disconnected segments, [2]forcing cyclists to merge into traffic whenever a protected lane abruptly ended[2]. [3]Reyes and his team mapped every gap in the existing network, prioritizing connections between residential neighborhoods and the downtown business district[3]. [4]Construction proceeded in phases, funded partly by a state grant aimed at reducing urban traffic congestion[4]. [5]Local business owners were initially split on the project, some worried that removing parking spaces for the new lanes would hurt their sales[5]. [6]Other business owners, particularly those near university housing, supported the change immediately[6], anticipating that more cyclists would mean more casual foot traffic browsing their storefronts. [7]Three years after construction finished, the city\'s transportation department released data comparing cycling rates before and after the redesign[7]. [8]The data showed a sixty percent increase in daily bicycle trips along the newly connected corridors[8]. [9]Traffic accidents involving cyclists, meanwhile, dropped by nearly a third over the same period[9]. [10]Reyes attributes this improvement primarily to the physical separation between bike lanes and car traffic, rather than to any single other factor[10]. [11]Not every part of the redesign succeeded equally, however, one downtown intersection remained a persistent trouble spot despite the new infrastructure[11]. [12]Cyclists and drivers alike reported confusion at that particular intersection, where the bike lane crossed a busy turning lane[12]. [13]The city ultimately redesigned that single intersection twice more before settling on a configuration that satisfied both cyclists and drivers[13]. [14]Reyes describes this ongoing troubleshooting as an unavoidable part of any major infrastructure project, no design works perfectly the first time[14]. [15]He hopes Halbrook\'s experience, successes and setbacks alike, will offer useful lessons for other mid-sized cities considering similar projects[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Local business owners were initially split on the project, some worried that removing parking spaces for the new lanes would hurt their sales]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Local business owners were initially split on the project: some worried that removing parking spaces for the new lanes would hurt their sales',
      'Local business owners were initially split on the project some worried that removing parking spaces for the new lanes would hurt their sales',
      'Local business owners were initially split on the project, and some worried, that removing parking spaces for the new lanes would hurt their sales',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'A colon correctly introduces the specific explanation of what being "split" meant, connecting the general claim to the specific concern that follows. NO CHANGE creates a comma splice by joining two independent clauses with only a comma. Removing all punctuation, as in the third option, creates a run-on sentence. The fourth option fixes the splice with "and" but adds an unnecessary comma after "worried," incorrectly separating the verb from its necessary object clause. ACT Tip: When a general claim, such as being "split" on an issue, is immediately followed by a specific example illustrating that split, a colon is often the most precise connector.',
  ),
