ing lane[12]. [13]The city ultimately redesigned that single intersection twice more before settling on a configuration that satisfied both cyclists and drivers[13]. [14]Reyes describes this ongoing troubleshooting as an unavoidable part of any major infrastructure project, no design works perfectly the first time[14]. [15]He hopes Halbrook\'s experience, successes and setbacks alike, will offer useful lessons for other mid-sized cities considering similar projects[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: The data showed a sixty percent increase in daily bicycle trips along the newly connected corridors]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'The data shown a sixty percent increase in daily bicycle trips along the newly connected corridors',
      'The data showing a sixty percent increase in daily bicycle trips along the newly connected corridors',
      'The data have showed a sixty percent increase in daily bicycle trips along the newly connected corridors',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "showed" is the correct simple past-tense form of "show," matching the passage\'s past-tense narration of the released data. "Shown" is the past participle form and cannot function as a standalone main verb without an auxiliary verb like "had" or "has." "Showing" removes the finite verb entirely, creating a sentence fragment. "Have showed" incorrectly uses "showed" instead of the correct past participle "shown" after the auxiliary "have," making this construction doubly incorrect for standard present perfect tense. ACT Tip: Remember that irregular verbs like "show" often have distinct simple past ("showed") and past participle ("shown") forms, and only the past participle can follow an auxiliary verb like "have" or "had."',
  ),
