      'Not every part of the redesign succeeded equally, however one downtown intersection remained a persistent trouble spot despite the new infrastructure',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'A semicolon before the conjunctive adverb "however" and a comma after it correctly join the two independent clauses. NO CHANGE creates a comma splice by using only a comma before "however." Removing all punctuation, as in the third option, creates a run-on sentence. Keeping only a comma before "however" without the semicolon, as in the fourth option, repeats the same comma splice error while also failing to set off "however" with a following comma. ACT Tip: Conjunctive adverbs like "however," "therefore," and "nevertheless" require a semicolon before them and a comma after when joining two independent clauses.',
