    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Five years ago, the mid-sized city of Halbrook began redesigning its downtown streets to include a connected network of protected bike lanes, separated from car traffic by physical barriers rather than painted lines alone. [1]City planner Owen Reyes, who led the redesign, believe that painted lines alone rarely convince nervous cyclists to leave their cars at home[1]. Before the redesign, Halbrook\'s bike lanes existed only in scattered, disconnected segments, [2]forcing cyclists to merge into traffic whenever a protected lane abruptly ended[2]. [3]Reyes and his team mapped every gap in the existing network, prioritizing connections between residential neighborhoods and the downtown business district[3]. [4]Construction proceeded in phases, funded partly by a state grant aimed at reducing urban traffic congestion[4]. [5]Local business owners were initially split on the project, some worried that removing parking spaces for the new lanes would hurt their sales[5]. [6]Other business owners, particularly those near university housing, supported the change immediately[6], anticipating that more cyclists would mean more casual foot traffic browsing their storefronts. [7]Three years after construction finished, the city\'s transportation department released data comparing cycling rates before and after the redesign[7]. [8]The data showed a sixty percent increase in daily bicycle trips along the newly connected corridors[8]. [9]Traffic accidents involving cyclists, meanwhile, dropped by nearly a third over the same period[9]. [10]Reyes attributes this improvement primarily to the physical separation between bike lanes and car traffic, rather than to any single other factor[10]. [11]Not every part of the redesign succeeded equally, however, one downtown intersection remained a persistent trouble spot despite the new infrastructure[11]. [12]Cyclists and drivers alike reported confusion at that particular intersection, where the bike lane crossed a busy turning lane[12]. [13]The city ultimately redesigned that single intersection twice more before settling on a configuration that satisfied both cyclists and drivers[13]. [14]Reyes describes this ongoing troubleshooting as an unavoidable part of any major infrastructure project, no design works perfectly the first time[14]. [15]He hopes Halbrook\'s experience, successes and setbacks alike, will offer useful lessons for other mid-sized cities considering similar projects[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: He hopes Halbrook\'s experience, successes and setbacks alike, will offer useful lessons for other mid-sized cities considering similar projects]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'He hopes Halbrook\'s experience will offer useful lessons for other mid-sized cities considering similar projects',
      'He hopes Halbrook\'s experience, both the good parts and the bad parts, will offer useful lessons for other mid-sized cities considering similar projects',
      'He is hopeful, in general, that Halbrook\'s experience, successes and setbacks alike, will offer useful lessons for other mid-sized cities considering similar projects',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the phrase "successes and setbacks alike" concisely and precisely reinforces the passage\'s closing theme, acknowledging both the project\'s achievements and its troubleshooting struggles as valuable lessons. Cutting this phrase removes a meaningful callback to the passage\'s earlier discussion of both the redesign\'s successes and its persistent intersection problem. The third option replaces the precise, slightly more formal phrase with the vaguer, more casual "the good parts and the bad parts," a less effective fit for the passage\'s closing, reflective tone. The fourth option adds the unnecessary and awkward filler phrase "in general," which adds no meaningful content and disrupts the sentence\'s flow. ACT Tip: A passage\'s closing sentence often works best when it explicitly ties back to both major threads developed earlier, such as both successes and setbacks here, rather than being simplified or padded with vague filler.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-cse-031',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past eight seasons, archaeologist Dr. Amara Okonkwo has led excavations at a small inland settlement site believed to have served as a waypoint along an ancient overland trade route connecting coastal ports to interior mining regions. [1]The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, have yielded artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation[1]. [2]Okonkwo\'s team has focused particularly on a cluster of storage pits near the settlement\'s center, where preserved organic material offers unusually detailed evidence of what goods passed through the site[2]. [3]Pottery fragments recovered from the pits include styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions, suggesting the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas[3]. [4]Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season, when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights, standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods[4]. [5]These weights, Okonkwo argues, indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size[5]. [6]Not all researchers agree with this interpretation, some argue the weights could have served ceremonial rather than strictly commercial purposes[6]. [7]Okonkwo has responded to this criticism by pointing to wear patterns on the weights themselves, patterns consistent with repeated handling in a way ceremonial objects typically do not show[7]. [8]Isotopic analysis of animal bones found at the site further supports the trade route hypothesis, revealing that many animals were raised in regions far from the settlement itself[8]. [9]This finding suggests livestock, not just goods carried by traders, moved along the route as well[9]. [10]Okonkwo\'s team has also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation, with later layers showing increasing stylistic blending between the previously distinct regional traditions[10]. [11]This blending, she suggests, may reflect longer-term cultural exchange between communities that initially interacted only through trade[11]. [12]Radiocarbon dating of charcoal samples has allowed the team to construct a detailed timeline of the settlement\'s growth and eventual decline[12]. [13]The settlement appears to have been abandoned relatively abruptly, a pattern Okonkwo\'s team is still working to explain[13]. [14]One hypothesis under active investigation involves a shift in regional trade patterns, possibly linked to the rise of a competing coastal route documented in nearby excavations[14]. [15]Okonkwo cautions that this hypothesis remains provisional, noting that archaeological evidence for the cause of a settlement\'s abandonment is often far harder to establish conclusively than evidence for its earlier growth[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, have yielded artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, has yielded artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation',
      'The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, having yielded artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation',
      'The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, yield artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The singular subject "site" requires the singular verb "has," not the plural "have." NO CHANGE incorrectly pairs a singular subject with a plural verb form. "Having yielded" removes the finite verb, creating a sentence fragment. "Yield," without the necessary auxiliary and correct form, fails to agree with the singular subject and does not fit the present perfect construction the sentence requires. ACT Tip: A parenthetical phrase describing the subject, such as "first identified through aerial surveys," does not change whether that subject is singular or plural for verb agreement purposes.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-pow-032',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past eight seasons, archaeologist Dr. Amara Okonkwo has led excavations at a small inland settlement site believed to have served as a waypoint along an ancient overland trade route connecting coastal ports to interior mining regions. [1]The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, have yielded artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation[1]. [2]Okonkwo\'s team has focused particularly on a cluster of storage pits near the settlement\'s center, where preserved organic material offers unusually detailed evidence of what goods passed through the site[2]. [3]Pottery fragments recovered from the pits include styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions, suggesting the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas[3]. [4]Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season, when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights, standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods[4]. [5]These weights, Okonkwo argues, indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size[5]. [6]Not all researchers agree with this interpretation, some argue the weights could have served ceremonial rather than strictly commercial purposes[6]. [7]Okonkwo has responded to this criticism by pointing to wear patterns on the weights themselves, patterns consistent with repeated handling in a way ceremonial objects typically do not show[7]. [8]Isotopic analysis of animal bones found at the site further supports the trade route hypothesis, revealing that many animals were raised in regions far from the settlement itself[8]. [9]This finding suggests livestock, not just goods carried by traders, moved along the route as well[9]. [10]Okonkwo\'s team has also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation, with later layers showing increasing stylistic blending between the previously distinct regional traditions[10]. [11]This blending, she suggests, may reflect longer-term cultural exchange between communities that initially interacted only through trade[11]. [12]Radiocarbon dating of charcoal samples has allowed the team to construct a detailed timeline of the settlement\'s growth and eventual decline[12]. [13]The settlement appears to have been abandoned relatively abruptly, a pattern Okonkwo\'s team is still working to explain[13]. [14]One hypothesis under active investigation involves a shift in regional trade patterns, possibly linked to the rise of a competing coastal route documented in nearby excavations[14]. [15]Okonkwo cautions that this hypothesis remains provisional, noting that archaeological evidence for the cause of a settlement\'s abandonment is often far harder to establish conclusively than evidence for its earlier growth[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Okonkwo\'s team has focused particularly on a cluster of storage pits near the settlement\'s center, where preserved organic material offers unusually detailed evidence of what goods passed through the site]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Okonkwo\'s team has focused particularly on a cluster of storage pits near the settlement\'s center',
      'Okonkwo\'s team has focused on some storage pits, which were interesting, near the settlement\'s center, where preserved organic material offers unusually detailed evidence of what goods passed through the site',
      'Okonkwo\'s team has focused particularly on a cluster of storage pits near the settlement\'s center, where preserved organic material offers unusually detailed evidence of what goods passed through the site, which is useful',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the full sentence explains both the location of the team\'s focus and specifically why that location matters, preserved organic material offering detailed evidence, which supports the passage\'s subsequent discussion of specific findings from the pits. The second option cuts the explanatory clause about why the pits are significant, weakening the connection to the detailed findings discussed afterward. The third option adds the vague, unnecessary filler "which were interesting," which provides no specific information. The fourth option adds the redundant, vague closing phrase "which is useful," restating something the sentence already implies. ACT Tip: When a sentence explains why a particular research focus matters, cutting that explanation typically weakens the passage\'s connection to the more detailed findings that follow.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-cse-033',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'For the past eight seasons, archaeologist Dr. Amara Okonkwo has led excavations at a small inland settlement site believed to have served as a waypoint along an ancient overland trade route connecting coastal ports to interior mining regions. [1]The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, have yielded artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation[1]. [2]Okonkwo\'s team has focused particularly on a cluster of storage pits near the settlement\'s center, where preserved organic material offers unusually detailed evidence of what goods passed through the site[2]. [3]Pottery fragments recovered from the pits include styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions, suggesting the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas[3]. [4]Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season, when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights, standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods[4]. [5]These weights, Okonkwo argues, indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size[5]. [6]Not all researchers agree with this interpretation, some argue the weights could have served ceremonial rather than strictly commercial purposes[6]. [7]Okonkwo has responded to this criticism by pointing to wear patterns on the weights themselves, patterns consistent with repeated handling in a way ceremonial objects typically do not show[7]. [8]Isotopic analysis of animal bones found at the site further supports the trade route hypothesis, revealing that many animals were raised in regions far from the settlement itself[8]. [9]This finding suggests livestock, not just goods carried by traders, moved along the route as well[9]. [10]Okonkwo\'s team has also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation, with later layers showing increasing stylistic blending between the previously distinct regional traditions[10]. [11]This blending, she suggests, may reflect longer-term cultural exchange between communities that initially interacted only through trade[11]. [12]Radiocarbon dating of charcoal samples has allowed the team to construct a detailed timeline of the settlement\'s growth and eventual decline[12]. [13]The settlement appears to have been abandoned relatively abruptly, a pattern Okonkwo\'s team is still working to explain[13]. [14]One hypothesis under active investigation involves a shift in regional trade patterns, possibly linked to the rise of a competing coastal route documented in nearby excavations[14]. [15]Okonkwo cautions that this hypothesis remains provisional, noting that archaeological evidence for the cause of a settlement\'s abandonment is often far harder to establish conclusively than evidence for its earlier growth[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Pottery fragments recovered from the pits include styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions, suggesting the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Pottery fragments recovered from the pits includes styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions, suggesting the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas',
      'Pottery fragments recovered from the pits include styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions; suggesting the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas',
      'Pottery fragments recovered from the pits include styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions, suggesting, the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the plural subject "fragments" correctly takes the plural verb "include," and the comma before the participial phrase "suggesting the settlement hosted traders..." is correctly placed. The second option incorrectly pairs the plural subject "fragments" with the singular verb "includes." Using a semicolon before "suggesting," as in the third option, is incorrect because the participial phrase is not an independent clause. Adding a comma after "suggesting," as in the fourth option, incorrectly separates the participle from its own clause. ACT Tip: Trace a sentence\'s true grammatical subject back through any intervening prepositional phrases, such as "from the pits" here, before checking subject-verb agreement.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-kol-034',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Knowledge of Language',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'For the past eight seasons, archaeologist Dr. Amara Okonkwo has led excavations at a small inland settlement site believed to have served as a waypoint along an ancient overland trade route connecting coastal ports to interior mining regions. [1]The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, have yielded artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation[1]. [2]Okonkwo\'s team has focused particularly on a cluster of storage pits near the settlement\'s center, where preserved organic material offers unusually detailed evidence of what goods passed through the site[2]. [3]Pottery fragments recovered from the pits include styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions, suggesting the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas[3]. [4]Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season, when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights, standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods[4]. [5]These weights, Okonkwo argues, indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size[5]. [6]Not all researchers agree with this interpretation, some argue the weights could have served ceremonial rather than strictly commercial purposes[6]. [7]Okonkwo has responded to this criticism by pointing to wear patterns on the weights themselves, patterns consistent with repeated handling in a way ceremonial objects typically do not show[7]. [8]Isotopic analysis of animal bones found at the site further supports the trade route hypothesis, revealing that many animals were raised in regions far from the settlement itself[8]. [9]This finding suggests livestock, not just goods carried by traders, moved along the route as well[9]. [10]Okonkwo\'s team has also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation, with later layers showing increasing stylistic blending between the previously distinct regional traditions[10]. [11]This blending, she suggests, may reflect longer-term cultural exchange between communities that initially interacted only through trade[11]. [12]Radiocarbon dating of charcoal samples has allowed the team to construct a detailed timeline of the settlement\'s growth and eventual decline[12]. [13]The settlement appears to have been abandoned relatively abruptly, a pattern Okonkwo\'s team is still working to explain[13]. [14]One hypothesis under active investigation involves a shift in regional trade patterns, possibly linked to the rise of a competing coastal route documented in nearby excavations[14]. [15]Okonkwo cautions that this hypothesis remains provisional, noting that archaeological evidence for the cause of a settlement\'s abandonment is often far harder to establish conclusively than evidence for its earlier growth[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season, when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights, standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season, when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods',
      'Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season, when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights; standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods',
      'Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights, standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Removing the comma before "standardized in size" correctly attaches this descriptive phrase directly to "stone weights" as an essential modifier without an unnecessary pause, since the description is closely tied to identifying the specific weights being discussed. NO CHANGE inserts an unnecessary comma between "weights" and its closely connected descriptive phrase, creating an awkward, unneeded pause. Using a semicolon before "standardized," as in the third option, is incorrect because the phrase is not an independent clause. Removing the comma after "sixth season," as in the fourth option, incorrectly fuses the introductory clause with the relative clause that follows, since "when the team uncovered..." needs to be set off from the main clause. ACT Tip: A descriptive phrase closely and essentially connected to the noun immediately before it often reads more smoothly without an intervening comma, even when a comma might seem plausible.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-pow-035',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past eight seasons, archaeologist Dr. Amara Okonkwo has led excavations at a small inland settlement site believed to have served as a waypoint along an ancient overland trade route connecting coastal ports to interior mining regions. [1]The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, have yielded artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation[1]. [2]Okonkwo\'s team has focused particularly on a cluster of storage pits near the settlement\'s center, where preserved organic material offers unusually detailed evidence of what goods passed through the site[2]. [3]Pottery fragments recovered from the pits include styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions, suggesting the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas[3]. [4]Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season, when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights, standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods[4]. [5]These weights, Okonkwo argues, indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size[5]. [6]Not all researchers agree with this interpretation, some argue the weights could have served ceremonial rather than strictly commercial purposes[6]. [7]Okonkwo has responded to this criticism by pointing to wear patterns on the weights themselves, patterns consistent with repeated handling in a way ceremonial objects typically do not show[7]. [8]Isotopic analysis of animal bones found at the site further supports the trade route hypothesis, revealing that many animals were raised in regions far from the settlement itself[8]. [9]This finding suggests livestock, not just goods carried by traders, moved along the route as well[9]. [10]Okonkwo\'s team has also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation, with later layers showing increasing stylistic blending between the previously distinct regional traditions[10]. [11]This blending, she suggests, may reflect longer-term cultural exchange between communities that initially interacted only through trade[11]. [12]Radiocarbon dating of charcoal samples has allowed the team to construct a detailed timeline of the settlement\'s growth and eventual decline[12]. [13]The settlement appears to have been abandoned relatively abruptly, a pattern Okonkwo\'s team is still working to explain[13]. [14]One hypothesis under active investigation involves a shift in regional trade patterns, possibly linked to the rise of a competing coastal route documented in nearby excavations[14]. [15]Okonkwo cautions that this hypothesis remains provisional, noting that archaeological evidence for the cause of a settlement\'s abandonment is often far harder to establish conclusively than evidence for its earlier growth[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: These weights, Okonkwo argues, indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'These weights, Okonkwo argues, indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size, which is a strong claim',
      'These weights indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size',
      'These weights, according to what Okonkwo has argued in her research, indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because attributing the claim directly to Okonkwo, "Okonkwo argues," appropriately frames this as her specific interpretation, which becomes important when the passage later introduces researchers who disagree with this interpretation. The second option adds the vague, unnecessary editorial tag "which is a strong claim," which adds no specific information. The third option removes the attribution to Okonkwo entirely, presenting her interpretation as an established fact rather than a specific scholarly claim, which weakens the passage\'s setup for the disagreement discussed in the following sentences. The fourth option is needlessly wordy, replacing the concise "Okonkwo argues" with the longer "according to what Okonkwo has argued in her research." ACT Tip: When a passage later introduces a disagreement with a specific claim, check whether that claim was clearly and specifically attributed to the researcher who made it, since removing that attribution can weaken the passage\'s later argumentative structure.',
  ),
