    questionText: '[underlined: Okonkwo\'s team has also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Okonkwo\'s teams has also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation',
      'Okonkwo\'s team have also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation',
      'Okonkwos team has also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "Okonkwo\'s" correctly shows singular possession, and "team," treated as a singular collective noun here, correctly takes the singular verb "has." "Teams," in the second option, incorrectly pluralizes the noun, which does not match the singular verb "has" that follows or the passage\'s consistent references to a single research team. "Have," in the third option, incorrectly pairs a plural verb with the singular collective noun "team" as used consistently elsewhere in the passage. "Okonkwos," in the fourth option, omits the necessary apostrophe, failing to show possession. ACT Tip: Collective nouns like "team," "family," or "committee" are typically treated as singular in standard American English and take singular verbs, even though they refer to multiple people.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-pow-041',
    setNumber: 2,
