  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-cse-042',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past eight seasons, archaeologist Dr. Amara Okonkwo has led excavations at a small inland settlement site believed to have served as a waypoint along an ancient overland trade route connecting coastal ports to interior mining regions. [1]The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, have yielded artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation[1]. [2]Okonkwo\'s team has focused particularly on a cluster of storage pits near the settlement\'s center, where preserved organic material offers unusually detailed evidence of what goods passed through the site[2]. [3]Pottery fragments recovered from the pits include styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions, suggesting the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas[3]. [4]Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season, when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights, standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods[4]. [5]These weights, Okonkwo argues, indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size[5]. [6]Not all researchers agree with this interpretation, some argue the weights could have served ceremonial rather than strictly commercial purposes[6]. [7]Okonkwo has responded to this criticism by pointing to wear patterns on the weights themselves, patterns consistent with repeated handling in a way ceremonial objects typically do not show[7]. [8]Isotopic analysis of animal bones found at the site further supports the trade route hypothesis, revealing that many animals were raised in regions far from the settlement itself[8]. [9]This finding suggests livestock, not just goods carried by traders, moved along the route as well[9]. [10]Okonkwo\'s team has also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation, with later layers showing increasing stylistic blending between the previously distinct regional traditions[10]. [11]This blending, she suggests, may reflect longer-term cultural exchange between communities that initially interacted only through trade[11]. [12]Radiocarbon dating of charcoal samples has allowed the team to construct a detailed timeline of the settlement\'s growth and eventual decline[12]. [13]The settlement appears to have been abandoned relatively abruptly, a pattern Okonkwo\'s team is still working to explain[13]. [14]One hypothesis under active investigation involves a shift in regional trade patterns, possibly linked to the rise of a competing coastal route documented in nearby excavations[14]. [15]Okonkwo cautions that this hypothesis remains provisional, noting that archaeological evidence for the cause of a settlement\'s abandonment is often far harder to establish conclusively than evidence for its earlier growth[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Radiocarbon dating of charcoal samples has allowed the team to construct a detailed timeline of the settlement\'s growth and eventual decline]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Radiocarbon dating of charcoal samples have allowed the team to construct a detailed timeline of the settlement\'s growth and eventual decline',
      'Radiocarbon dating of charcoal samples has allowed the team to construct a detailed timeline of the settlement\'s growth, and eventual decline',
      'Radiocarbon dating of charcoal samples having allowed the team to construct a detailed timeline of the settlement\'s growth and eventual decline',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the singular subject "dating" correctly takes the singular verb "has," and no comma is needed between "growth" and "and eventual decline" since these are only two items joined by "and," not a longer list requiring a comma. "Have," in the second option, incorrectly pairs a plural verb with the singular subject "dating." Adding a comma before "and eventual decline," as in the third option, is unnecessary and incorrect punctuation for a simple two-item list joined by "and." "Having allowed," in the fourth option, removes the finite verb, creating a sentence fragment. ACT Tip: A comma is generally not needed before "and" when it joins only two items in a simple list, as opposed to three or more items.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-kol-043',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Knowledge of Language',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past eight seasons, archaeologist Dr. Amara Okonkwo has led excavations at a small inland settlement site believed to have served as a waypoint along an ancient overland trade route connecting coastal ports to interior mining regions. [1]The site, first identified through aerial surveys in the early 2000s, have yielded artifacts spanning nearly four centuries of continuous occupation[1]. [2]Okonkwo\'s team has focused particularly on a cluster of storage pits near the settlement\'s center, where preserved organic material offers unusually detailed evidence of what goods passed through the site[2]. [3]Pottery fragments recovered from the pits include styles clearly originating from at least three distinct regional traditions, suggesting the settlement hosted traders from multiple, culturally distinct areas[3]. [4]Perhaps the most significant find came in the excavation\'s sixth season, when the team uncovered a cache of small stone weights, standardized in size and clearly used for measuring traded goods[4]. [5]These weights, Okonkwo argues, indicate a level of commercial organization more sophisticated than earlier scholars had assumed existed at inland waypoint sites of this size[5]. [6]Not all researchers agree with this interpretation, some argue the weights could have served ceremonial rather than strictly commercial purposes[6]. [7]Okonkwo has responded to this criticism by pointing to wear patterns on the weights themselves, patterns consistent with repeated handling in a way ceremonial objects typically do not show[7]. [8]Isotopic analysis of animal bones found at the site further supports the trade route hypothesis, revealing that many animals were raised in regions far from the settlement itself[8]. [9]This finding suggests livestock, not just goods carried by traders, moved along the route as well[9]. [10]Okonkwo\'s team has also documented a gradual shift in pottery styles over the site\'s four centuries of occupation, with later