      'Ferrer\'s workshop has instructed twelve apprentices over the past two decades, several of whom have since opened their own workshops elsewhere in Mexico',
      'Ferrer\'s workshop has schooled twelve apprentices over the past two decades, several of whom have since opened their own workshops elsewhere in Mexico',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "trained" is the most natural, precise word for describing hands-on, practical skill development in a craft apprenticeship, matching the passage\'s focus on physical technique. "Educated" is a plausible near-synonym but carries a broader connotation often associated with formal or academic learning, a less precise fit for hands-on craft instruction. "Instructed" is also plausible but suggests a more formal, structured teaching relationship than the traditional, hands-on apprenticeship model the passage describes throughout. "Schooled" carries a similar formal, classroom-oriented connotation that does not precisely match the informal, hands-on nature of a traditional craft apprenticeship. ACT Tip: When a word choice question involves teaching or learning, consider whether the context describes formal, classroom-based instruction or informal, hands-on skill development, since near-synonyms often carry different connotations along this distinction.',
