    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In a small workshop outside Oaxaca, luthier Ignacio Ferrer builds guitars using techniques passed down through four generations of his family, methods that predate the electric power tools most modern instrument makers rely on. [1]Ferrer, along with two apprentices, still shape each guitar\'s top and back panels entirely by hand, using hand planes rather than machine sanders[1]. [2]This slower process, Ferrer explains, allows him to feel subtle variations in the wood\'s density that a machine-driven process would smooth over without detection[2]. [3]Each guitar begins with wood selected specifically for its acoustic properties, spruce for the soundboard and rosewood for the back and sides, a pairing Ferrer\'s grandfather favored decades ago[3]. [4]Ferrer estimates that a single guitar, from initial wood selection to final finishing, requires roughly one hundred and twenty hours of labor[4]. [5]Younger luthiers sometimes ask him why he doesn\'t adopt faster, more modern techniques that other workshops have embraced[5]. [6]His answer, consistent across the many times he has been asked, emphasizes that speed was never the point of the craft as his family practiced it[6]. [7]Ferrer\'s workshop has trained twelve apprentices over the past two decades, several of whom have since opened their own workshops elsewhere in Mexico[7]. [8]Not every apprentice stays in the traditional craft, however, some ultimately gravitate toward faster, factory-style production once they leave Ferrer\'s workshop[8]. [9]Ferrer says he does not resent this choice, acknowledging that factory production serves a real market that his own small workshop could never fully supply[9]. [10]What concerns him more is the possibility that the specific hand techniques he learned from his father, and his father learned from his own father before him, could eventually disappear entirely if too few apprentices choose to preserve them[10]. [11]To address this concern, Ferrer began documenting