  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-kol-058',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Knowledge of Language',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In a small workshop outside Oaxaca, luthier Ignacio Ferrer builds guitars using techniques passed down through four generations of his family, methods that predate the electric power tools most modern instrument makers rely on. [1]Ferrer, along with two apprentices, still shape each guitar\'s top and back panels entirely by hand, using hand planes rather than machine sanders[1]. [2]This slower process, Ferrer explains, allows him to feel subtle variations in the wood\'s density that a machine-driven process would smooth over without detection[2]. [3]Each guitar begins with wood selected specifically for its acoustic properties, spruce for the soundboard and rosewood for the back and sides, a pairing Ferrer\'s grandfather favored decades ago[3]. [4]Ferrer estimates that a single guitar, from initial wood selection to final finishing, requires roughly one hundred and twenty hours of labor[4]. [5]Younger luthiers sometimes ask him why he doesn\'t adopt faster, more modern techniques that other workshops have embraced[5]. [6]His answer, consistent across the many times he has been asked, emphasizes that speed was never the point of the craft as his family practiced it[6]. [7]Ferrer\'s workshop has trained twelve apprentices over the past two decades, several of whom have since opened their own workshops elsewhere in Mexico[7]. [8]Not every apprentice stays in the traditional craft, however, some ultimately gravitate toward faster, factory-style production once they leave Ferrer\'s workshop[8]. [9]Ferrer says he does not resent this choice, acknowledging that factory production serves a real market that his own small workshop could never fully supply[9]. [10]What concerns him more is the possibility that the specific hand techniques he learned from his father, and his father learned from his own father before him, could eventually disappear entirely if too few apprentices choose to preserve them[10]. [11]To address this concern, Ferrer began documenting his techniques on video several years ago, creating an archive intended to outlast any single workshop or teacher[11]. [12]The videos, now numbering over two hundred hours of footage, cover everything from selecting raw timber to applying the final coat of varnish[12]. [13]A university ethnomusicology department recently reached out to Ferrer, hoping to formally archive and digitize his growing video collection[13]. [14]Ferrer agreed, on the condition that the archive remain freely accessible rather than locked behind an academic paywall[14]. [15]He hopes that whatever ultimately happens to his own workshop, the techniques themselves will continue passing from hand to hand for generations still to come[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: A university ethnomusicology department recently reached out to Ferrer, hoping to formally archive and digitize his growing video collection]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'A university ethnomusicology department recently contacted out to Ferrer, hoping to formally archive and digitize his growing video collection',
      'A university ethnomusicology department recently reached toward Ferrer, hoping to formally archive and digitize his growing video collection',
      'A university ethnomusicology department recently reached over to Ferrer, hoping to formally archive and digitize his growing video collection',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "reached out to" is the standard idiomatic phrasal verb for initiating contact with someone, correctly conveying the department\'s outreach to Ferrer. "Contacted out to" is not a standard English phrase; "contacted" does not idiomatically pair with "out to." "Reached toward" suggests physical or directional movement rather than the idiomatic sense of initiating communication. "Reached over to" similarly suggests physical movement across a space rather than the idiomatic sense of professional outreach intended here. ACT Tip: Idiomatic phrasal verbs like "reach out to" have fixed forms in standard English; altering the preposition, even slightly, often creates a nonstandard or incorrect construction.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-english-pow-059',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In a small workshop outside Oaxaca, luthier Ignacio Ferrer builds guitars using techniques passed down through four generations of his family, methods that predate the electric power tools most modern instrument makers rely on. [1]Ferrer, along with two apprentices, still shape each guitar\'s top and back panels entirely by hand, using hand planes rather than machine sanders[1]. [2]This slower process, Ferrer explains, allows him to feel subtle variations in the wood\'s density that a machine-driven process would smooth over without detection[2]. [3]Each guitar begins with wood selected specifically for its acoustic properties, spruce for the soundboard and rosewood for the back and sides, a pairing Ferrer\'s grandfather favored decades ago[3]. [4]Ferrer estimates that a single guitar, from initial wood selection to final finishing, requires roughly one hundred and twenty hours of labor[4]. [5]Younger luthiers sometimes ask him why he doesn\'t adopt faster, more modern techniques that other workshops have embraced[5]. [6]His answer, consistent across the many times he has been asked, emphasizes that speed was never the point of the craft as his family practiced it[6]. [7]Ferrer\'s workshop has trained twelve apprentices over the past two decades, several of whom have since opened their own workshops elsewhere in Mexico[7]. [8]Not every apprentice stays in the traditional craft, however, some ultimately gravitate toward faster, factory-style production once they leave Ferrer\'s workshop[8]. [9]Ferrer says he does not resent this choice, acknowledging that factory production serves a real market that his own small workshop could never fully supply[9]. [10]What concerns him more is the possibility that the specific hand techniques he learned from his father, and his father learned from his own father before him, could eventually disappear entirely if too few apprentices choose to preserve them[10]. [11]To address this concern, Ferrer began documenting his techniques on video several years ago, creating an archive intended to outlast any single workshop or teacher[11]. [12]The videos, now numbering over two hundred hours of footage, cover everything from selecting raw timber to applying the final coat of varnish[12]. [13]A university ethnomusicology department recently reached out to Ferrer, hoping to formally archive and digitize his growing video collection[13]. [14]Ferrer agreed, on the condition that the archive remain freely accessible rather than locked behind an academic paywall[14]. [15]He hopes that whatever ultimately happens to his own workshop, the techniques themselves will continue passing from hand to hand for generations still to come[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Ferrer agreed, on the condition that the archive remain freely accessible rather than locked behind an academic paywall]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Ferrer agreed',
      'Ferrer agreed, on the condition that the archive remain freely accessible rather than locked behind an academic paywall, which seems fair',
      'Ferrer, who cares deeply about accessibility, agreed, on the condition that the archive remain freely accessible rather than locked behind an academic paywall',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the full sentence specifies the exact condition Ferrer set for the archive, which directly connects to the passage\'s closing theme about wanting the techniques to remain accessible and to continue passing between people freely. Cutting the condition, as in the second option, removes this meaningful specific detail entirely, leaving only a vague statement of agreement. The third option adds the vague, unnecessary editorial phrase "which seems fair," inserting an unsupported judgment. The fourth option adds an unsupported biographical claim, "who cares deeply about accessibility," that is not stated elsewhere in the passage, even though it may seem consistent with the sentence\'s content, it is not something the passage has established directly. ACT Tip: A specific condition or requirement stated by a character often connects directly to a passage\'s broader theme; cutting it removes an important, thematically relevant detail rather than merely trimming excess wording.',
  ),
