    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Every Tuesday evening, a small legal aid clinic operating out of a converted storefront in a mid-sized city offers free consultations to tenants facing eviction, most of whom would otherwise appear in housing court without any legal representation at all. [1]Attorney Priscilla Nwosu, who founded the clinic six years ago, believe that the imbalance between represented landlords and unrepresented tenants shapes outcomes long before a case ever reaches a judge[1]. [2]National data supports her concern, studies consistently show that tenants with legal representation are far more likely to avoid eviction than those who appear in court alone[2]. [3]Nwosu\'s clinic operates with a small staff of two full-time attorneys, supplemented by law students who volunteer under close supervision[3]. [4]Each Tuesday session typically handles between eight and twelve cases, ranging from straightforward rent disputes to more complicated situations involving alleged lease violations[4]. [5]Volunteers begin each session by reviewing a tenant\'s lease and payment history, looking for procedural errors landlords sometimes make that can delay or dismiss a case entirely[5]. [6]One common error involves improper notice, landlords are legally required to provide tenants with a specific, legally defined amount of advance warning before filing for eviction[6]. [7]Nwosu estimates that improper notice alone accounts for roughly a quarter of the cases her clinic successfully challenges[7]. [8]Beyond procedural issues, the clinic also negotiates directly with landlords, often securing payment plans that allow tenants to remain in their homes[8]. [9]These negotiated agreements, Nwosu notes, frequently benefit landlords as well, since evicting and replacing a tenant is often more costly than working out a repayment arrangement[9]. [10]Not every case ends favorably, despite the clinic\'s efforts, some tenants are ultimately evicted regardless of the legal assistance provided[10]. [11]Nwosu is candid about this reality, emphasizing that legal representation improves outcomes on average without guaranteeing any individual result[11]. [12]The clinic has expanded twice since its founding, moving from a single volunteer attorney working out of her own home to the current storefront location with paid staff[12]. [13]Funding remains a persistent challenge, drawn primarily from a combination of local foundation grants and individual donations rather than any stable government source[13]. [14]Nwosu has begun exploring a partnership with the city\'s public defender\'s office, hoping to secure more consistent funding going forward[14]. [15]Whatever form that partnership eventually takes, Nwosu says her clinic\'s core mission, ensuring no tenant faces housing court entirely alone, will remain unchanged[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: National data supports her concern, studies consistently show that tenants with legal representation are far more likely to avoid eviction than those who appear in court alone]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'National data supports her concern: studies consistently show that tenants with legal representation are far more likely to avoid eviction than those who appear in court alone',
      'National data supports her concern studies consistently show that tenants with legal representation are far more likely to avoid eviction than those who appear in court alone',
      'National data supports her concern, and studies consistently show, that tenants with legal representation are far more likely to avoid eviction than those who appear in court alone',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'A colon correctly introduces the specific evidence explaining why the data supports her concern, connecting the general claim to the specific studies that follow. NO CHANGE creates a comma splice by joining two independent clauses with only a comma. Removing all punctuation, as in the third option, creates a run-on sentence. The fourth option fixes the splice with "and" but adds an unnecessary comma after "show," incorrectly separating the verb from its necessary object clause. ACT Tip: A colon is often the most precise punctuation choice when a general claim is immediately followed by the specific evidence supporting it.',
  ),
