  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-reading-kid-003',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Key Ideas and Details',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'The bakery had belonged to my aunt Rosalind for thirty-two years before she handed me the keys on a Tuesday morning in March, saying only that her hands had finally decided they were done kneading dough at four in the morning. I was twenty-six, freshly unemployed from a marketing job I had never much liked, and utterly unprepared to run a business that opened before dawn and closed only when the last loaf sold.\n\nThe first month nearly broke me. I burned three batches of the sourdough that had made the shop\'s reputation before I finally understood what Rosalind had tried, unsuccessfully, to explain in words: that the dough told you what it needed, if you paid attention to how it felt under your palms rather than watching the clock. I called her twice a day, sometimes three times, describing textures and asking questions that must have sounded absurd to anyone who hadn\'t spent three decades with flour under their fingernails. She never once told me to figure it out myself, though I suspect she wanted to. Instead she talked me through it, patiently, the same way she must have once talked herself through it, alone, in the years before I was born.\n\nMrs. Ferreira came in every morning at six-fifteen, before the shop technically opened, because Rosalind had always unlocked the door early for her. I didn\'t know this until the first week, when I found her rattling the locked door at six-twenty, visibly confused that the ritual had changed without warning. I let her in, apologized, and made a note to unlock earlier from then on. She bought the same thing every day: one plain roll, still warm, and a black coffee she drank standing at the counter rather than sitting at any of the three small tables by the window. She rarely spoke beyond thanking me, but after the third week, she began commenting on the bread itself, this one\'s better than yesterday\'s, or, you\'re getting closer to her sourdough now, comments that felt like small, exacting report cards I hadn\'t asked for but found myself anxiously awaiting each morning.\n\nBy midsummer, the burned batches had mostly stopped, though I still occasionally over-proofed a dough on the days I let myself get distracted by the accounting, which remained, if anything, more mysterious to me than the baking ever had. Rosalind had kept records in a system only she fully understood, a mix of handwritten ledgers and abbreviations that seemed to shift meaning depending on the season. I spent an entire Sunday afternoon on the phone with her, both of us laughing more than either of us managed to actually explain, as she tried to remember why a particular column was labeled simply "W" for reasons neither of us could reconstruct.\n\nWhat surprised me most, over that first year, wasn\'t the bread itself, though I did eventually produce a sourdough that Mrs. Ferreira pronounced, with what I chose to interpret as genuine approval, nearly indistinguishable from Rosalind\'s. What surprised me was how much of running the shop had nothing to do with baking at all. It was remembering that Mr. Castellano\'s grandson had a piano recital and asking about it three separate times, once when I first heard, once the week before, and once the morning after. It was noticing when a regular hadn\'t come in for several days and mentioning it, gently, the next time they appeared, letting them tell me as much or as little as they wanted about wherever they had been. It was learning that the shop\'s real product, more than any individual loaf, was the fifteen minutes of someone\'s morning that they trusted us with, day after day, whether or not they ever said so directly.\n\nRosalind visited in October, ostensibly to check on the ovens, though we both understood the ovens were an excuse. She stood behind the counter for a while, watching me work, saying almost nothing until Mrs. Ferreira came in for her plain roll and black coffee. Rosalind greeted her by name, and Mrs. Ferreira\'s face changed in a way I hadn\'t seen before, something between surprise and genuine pleasure, as she asked after Rosalind\'s hands and whether retirement suited her.\n\nAfter Mrs. Ferreira left, Rosalind turned to me and said that watching me with the regulars, she could finally stop worrying about the shop, not because the bread had gotten good enough, though it had, but because I had apparently learned, without her ever quite teaching me directly, the part of the job she had never known how to put into words in the first place.',
    questionText: 'According to the passage, what did Mrs. Ferreira order every morning?',
    options: [
      'A croissant and a latte',
      'A plain roll and a black coffee',
      'A sourdough loaf and tea',
      'A pastry and an espresso',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The passage states directly that Mrs. Ferreira "bought the same thing every day: one plain roll, still warm, and a black coffee she drank standing at the counter." The other combinations of items are not mentioned anywhere in the passage. ACT Tip: For direct factual questions about a specific character\'s routine, locate the exact sentence describing that routine rather than relying on a general impression of the character.',
  ),
