    questionText: 'As it is used in paragraph 3, the word "exacting" most nearly means:',
    options: [
      'careless',
      'demanding precise standards',
      'generous and forgiving',
      'unrelated to quality',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'In context, Mrs. Ferreira\'s brief comments about the bread function as "small, exacting report cards," meaning they held the narrator to precise, high standards of comparison against Rosalind\'s original recipe, consistent with the narrator anxiously awaiting this feedback each morning. "Careless" is essentially the opposite of the intended meaning, since the sentence describes precise, standard-holding feedback, not indifferent or sloppy judgment. "Generous and forgiving" does not fit the context of a report card that holds someone to a standard rather than simply offering unconditional praise. "Unrelated to quality" directly contradicts the sentence\'s meaning, since the comments explicitly evaluate the quality of the bread compared to Rosalind\'s. ACT Tip: When a vocabulary word appears within a metaphor, such as "report cards" here, use the metaphor\'s real-world meaning to help confirm the word\'s intended sense.',
  ),
