    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Integration of Knowledge and Ideas',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'In the early 2010s, a series of studies on judicial decision-making produced a finding that surprised both psychologists and legal scholars: the likelihood of a parole board granting a favorable ruling appeared to depend, in part, on how much time had elapsed since the judges\' last food break. Researchers analyzing over a thousand parole hearings found that favorable rulings were significantly more common at the start of each session and immediately after a scheduled break, dropping steadily as the session wore on, before spiking again once judges returned from eating.\n\nThe finding became one of the most frequently cited pieces of evidence for a psychological phenomenon researchers call decision fatigue, the idea that the quality and consistency of a person\'s decision-making deteriorates after a period of sustained choice-making, regardless of how important or trivial those earlier choices were. The underlying theory suggests that self-control and deliberate reasoning draw on a limited psychological resource, sometimes compared, though imperfectly, to physical muscle fatigue, that becomes depleted with repeated use and is at least partially replenished by rest or, notably, by food intake.\n\nDr. Simone Achebe, a cognitive psychologist who has studied decision fatigue extensively, cautions against interpreting the parole board findings too simply. "It would be a mistake," she has written, "to conclude that judges are simply becoming lazier or less careful as the day progresses. The more likely explanation is that sustained decision-making pushes people toward whatever option requires less additional deliberation, and in the context of parole hearings, denying parole, which maintains the status quo and requires less complex justification, may simply be the easier default when mental resources run low." This distinction matters considerably for how the finding should be interpreted and applied elsewhere; it is not that tired decision-makers become