    questionText: 'The passage\'s discussion of the pre-existing European misconception about hieroglyphs in paragraph 2 primarily serves to:',
    options: [
      'prove that European scholars before the stone\'s discovery had no interest in ancient Egyptian civilization',
      'explain a key obstacle that made early decipherment efforts unproductive, setting up the passage\'s later discussion of how Young and Champollion overcame this assumption',
      'argue that hieroglyphs are not actually a written language at all',
      'criticize modern historians for failing to properly credit European scholars',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The paragraph explicitly states this assumption "sent generations of would-be decipherers down largely unproductive paths," directly setting up the passage\'s subsequent discussion of how Young first proposed a phonetic component and how Champollion ultimately overturned this misconception entirely, establishing a clear cause-and-obstacle relationship that the rest of the passage resolves. The passage does not address European scholars\' general interest in Egyptian civilization, only their specific misconception about how hieroglyphic writing functioned. The passage\'s entire argument centers on hieroglyphs being a genuine, phonetically-based writing system, directly contradicting any suggestion that hieroglyphs are not a written language. The passage does not criticize modern historians in this paragraph; it describes a historical misconception held by scholars of an earlier era, not a criticism of contemporary historiography. ACT Tip: When a passage explains a historical misconception, expect the passage to later describe how that misconception was overcome, and treat that overcoming as the misconception\'s primary narrative purpose.',
  ),
