    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'When French soldiers rebuilding a fort near the Egyptian town of Rosetta in 1799 unearthed a slab of dark granodiorite covered in unfamiliar carved text, few of them could have anticipated that the stone would eventually unlock a writing system that had baffled European scholars for over a thousand years. The slab, which came to be known as the Rosetta Stone, bore the same decree inscribed three times, in ancient Egyptian hieroglyphs, in a simplified Egyptian script called Demotic, and in ancient Greek, a language scholars of the era could already read fluently.\n\nThe presence of the Greek text offered an obvious starting point, but translating a known language into an unknown one, especially one as visually intricate and symbolically dense as hieroglyphic writing, proved far more difficult than early scholars initially assumed. For centuries before the stone\'s discovery, most European scholars had approached hieroglyphs under a fundamental misconception, believing the symbols to be primarily pictorial and symbolic, each glyph representing an abstract idea or concept directly, rather than functioning as a system tied to the sounds of a spoken language. This assumption, though intuitive given the hieroglyphs\' undeniably pictorial appearance, sent generations of would-be decipherers down largely unproductive paths.\n\nThe English polymath Thomas Young was among the first to make genuine progress, correctly proposing that certain hieroglyphs enclosed within oval loops, later termed cartouches, likely represented the names of rulers, and that at least some hieroglyphic signs functioned phonetically, representing sounds rather than pure abstract concepts. Young successfully matched several hieroglyphic signs within a cartouche to the sounds of the name Ptolemy, a significant breakthrough, but he remained committed to the broader assumption that most hieroglyphic writing was fundamentally symbolic rather than phonetic, a position that limited how far his analysis could ultimately progress.\n\nIt was the French scholar Jean-François Champollion who made the decisive breakthrough, building on Young\'s initial insights but reaching a far more radical and ultimately correct conclusion: that hieroglyphic writing was fundamentally a phonetic system, closely related to the Coptic language still used liturgically by Egyptian Christians in Champollion\'s own era, a language he had studied intensively for years before ever encountering the Rosetta Stone\'s cartouches in earnest. Champollion\'s crucial insight came while comparing the cartouche believed to spell "Ptolemy" with another cartouche, from a different inscription entirely, believed to spell the name "Cleopatra." By comparing which hieroglyphic signs appeared in both names, and cross-referencing these signs against his deep knowledge of Coptic phonetics, Champollion was able to confirm that specific hieroglyphic signs corresponded to specific sounds consistently across different inscriptions, not merely within a single royal name considered in isolation.\n\nWhat made Champollion\'s breakthrough particularly significant, beyond simply cracking a single decree, was his recognition that this phonetic principle extended well beyond royal names written in cartouches, applying to ordinary Egyptian words throughout hieroglyphic texts more broadly. Earlier scholars, including Young, had generally assumed that phonetic spelling, to whatever limited extent it existed at all, was a special exception reserved specifically for foreign names that lacked any pre-existing symbolic hieroglyph of their own. Champollion\'s more radical claim, that phonetic elements were woven pervasively throughout ordinary hieroglyphic Egyptian text generally, not confined to a narrow, exceptional case involving foreign rulers\' names, proved to be the correct account, though it took several more years of careful scholarly work, following his initial 1822 announcement, to fully vindicate this broader claim against considerable and persistent skepticism from some of his academic contemporaries.\n\nThe rivalry, and at times open animosity, between Young and Champollion has become a somewhat famous footnote within the broader history of the decipherment, with some historians portraying Young as an unfairly overlooked pioneer whose foundational contributions were subsequently overshadowed by Champollion\'s later, more complete breakthrough, while others emphasize that Young\'s own persistent theoretical commitments genuinely prevented him from reaching Champollion\'s more accurate and comprehensive final conclusion regarding the pervasively phonetic nature of the script. Most contemporary historians of the decipherment now favor a more balanced view, one that credits Young\'s foundational insights, particularly regarding cartouches, as a genuinely necessary precondition for Champollion\'s subsequent breakthrough, while still crediting Champollion himself as the scholar who correctly and definitively identified the true, comprehensive phonetic nature of the hieroglyphic writing system as a whole.\n\nThe broader decipherment that followed from these initial breakthroughs opened an entire lost civilization\'s own words, previously accessible to modern scholars only indirectly, through the often distorted and unreliable accounts of ancient Greek and Roman writers, to direct and increasingly nuanced examination for the very first time. Religious texts, administrative records, personal letters, and literary works, once entirely illegible to modern eyes despite surviving in remarkably intact physical condition across millennia of preservation in Egypt\'s notably dry climate, gradually became readable once more, transforming the modern scholarly understanding of ancient Egyptian civilization from a distant, largely speculative curiosity into a subject of increasingly detailed, primary-source-based historical and cultural study.',
    questionText: 'According to the passage, what language did Champollion study intensively before working on the cartouches, and which proved useful to his breakthrough?',
    options: [
      'Ancient Greek',
      'Demotic',
      'Coptic',
      'Latin',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'The passage states directly that Champollion had studied Coptic, "a language he had studied intensively for years before ever encountering the Rosetta Stone\'s cartouches in earnest," and that he cross-referenced hieroglyphic signs "against his deep knowledge of Coptic phonetics." Ancient Greek is described as the language used in one of the stone\'s three inscriptions, providing a known reference text, not the specific language Champollion had studied intensively that directly informed his phonetic analysis. Demotic is described as one of the stone\'s three scripts, not a language Champollion is described as having specially studied beforehand. Latin is not mentioned anywhere in the passage. ACT Tip: When a passage names several languages in different contexts, carefully distinguish between a language used in the original inscription and a separate language a scholar specifically studied that informed their analysis.',
  ),
