der claim against considerable and persistent skepticism from some of his academic contemporaries.\n\nThe rivalry, and at times open animosity, between Young and Champollion has become a somewhat famous footnote within the broader history of the decipherment, with some historians portraying Young as an unfairly overlooked pioneer whose foundational contributions were subsequently overshadowed by Champollion\'s later, more complete breakthrough, while others emphasize that Young\'s own persistent theoretical commitments genuinely prevented him from reaching Champollion\'s more accurate and comprehensive final conclusion regarding the pervasively phonetic nature of the script. Most contemporary historians of the decipherment now favor a more balanced view, one that credits Young\'s foundational insights, particularly regarding cartouches, as a genuinely necessary precondition for Champollion\'s subsequent breakthrough, while still crediting Champollion himself as the scholar who correctly and definitively identified the true, comprehensive phonetic nature of the hieroglyphic writing system as a whole.\n\nThe broader decipherment that followed from these initial breakthroughs opened an entire lost civilization\'s own words, previously accessible to modern scholars only indirectly, through the often distorted and unreliable accounts of ancient Greek and Roman writers, to direct and increasingly nuanced examination for the very first time. Religious texts, administrative records, personal letters, and literary works, once entirely illegible to modern eyes despite surviving in remarkably intact physical condition across millennia of preservation in Egypt\'s notably dry climate, gradually became readable once more, transforming the modern scholarly understanding of ancient Egyptian civilization from a distant, largely speculative curiosity into a subject of increasingly detailed, primary-source-based historical and cultural study.',
    questionText: 'The passage\'s discussion of the rivalry between Young and Champollion in paragraph 6 primarily functions to:',
    options: [
      'argue definitively that Young, not Champollion, deserves full credit for the decipherment',
      'present a balanced historical assessment that credits Young\'s foundational contributions while still recognizing Champollion\'s more complete and accurate breakthrough',
      'dismiss Champollion\'s contributions as entirely derivative of Young\'s earlier work',
      'suggest that the rivalry between the two scholars prevented the decipherment from ever being completed',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The passage explicitly states that "most contemporary historians of the decipherment now favor a more balanced view," one crediting Young\'s cartouche insights as "a genuinely necessary precondition" while still crediting Champollion as the scholar who "correctly and definitively identified" the comprehensive phonetic nature of the script, directly matching this balanced characterization. The passage does not argue that Young alone deserves full credit; it explicitly credits Champollion with the more complete and correct final breakthrough. The passage does not dismiss Champollion\'s contributions as derivative; it explicitly distinguishes his more radical and ultimately correct conclusion from Young\'s more limited insights. The passage does not state that the decipherment was ever prevented from being completed; it describes the decipherment as ultimately successful, despite scholarly disagreement about how credit should be distributed. ACT Tip: When a passage discusses a historical rivalry between two contributors to a shared achievement, look for whether the passage ultimately presents a balanced assessment crediting both, rather than assuming it favors one figure entirely over the other.',
  ),
