  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-reading-kid-031',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Key Ideas and Details',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Earthquake early warning systems, now operating in several seismically active regions around the world, do not predict earthquakes before they occur; instead, they exploit a brief window of physical opportunity that opens only after an earthquake has already begun, detecting the initial, less destructive seismic waves and issuing an alert before the more damaging waves arrive at a given location. Understanding how this works requires understanding that earthquakes actually generate two distinct types of body waves that travel through the Earth\'s interior at different speeds, along with slower surface waves that typically cause the most severe shaking and structural damage.\n\nThe faster of the two body wave types, called primary waves or P-waves, travel by compressing and expanding the material they pass through, similar to the way sound moves through air, and typically travel at speeds between about 5 and 8 kilometers per second through the Earth\'s crust. Secondary waves, or S-waves, travel more slowly, typically at roughly half to sixty percent of the P-wave speed, moving the ground perpendicular to the wave\'s direction of travel rather than compressing it, a type of motion that generally causes significantly more damage to structures than the gentler compressional motion of P-waves. Because P-waves consistently arrive first at any given location, detecting them provides a brief but genuinely useful warning window before the more destructive S-waves and subsequent surface waves arrive.\n\nModern early warning systems rely on dense networks of seismic sensors positioned throughout an earthquake-prone region, continuously monitoring ground motion and feeding data to a central processing system in real time. When several sensors near an earthquake\'s origin point, called the epicenter, detect the characteristic signature of P-wave arrival within a very short window of each other, the system\'s algorithms rapidly estimate the earthquake\'s location, depth, and likely magnitude, then calculate how much advance warning time different, more distant locations will receive before the more damaging S-waves and surface waves reach them.\n\nThe amount of warning time any particular location receives depends heavily on its distance from the earthquake\'s epicenter. Locations very close to the epicenter may receive only a few seconds of warning, sometimes not even enough time for a meaningful protective action to be completed before shaking begins, while locations tens or even over a hundred kilometers away may receive anywhere from ten seconds to as much as a full minute or more of advance warning, since the S-waves and surface waves take proportionally longer to reach these more distant locations after the near-instantaneous detection of the initial P-wave signature. This geographic variation creates what seismologists sometimes describe as a warning "blind zone" immediately surrounding the epicenter, where the warning time is too short to be practically useful for most protective actions, even though the underlying detection and alert system technically still functions correctly within that zone.\n\nDespite these inherent physical limitations, even a brief warning window of just several seconds can enable meaningful protective actions when properly integrated into infrastructure and public alert systems. High-speed trains can begin automatically braking before severe shaking arrives, reducing the risk of derailment. Surgeons performing delicate operations can pause at a safer moment rather than being caught mid-procedure. Elevators can be programmed to stop at the nearest floor and open their doors automatically, preventing passengers from becoming trapped between floors during shaking. Individual members of the public, alerted through smartphone notifications or public address systems, can take the several seconds available to move away from windows, get under sturdy furniture, or simply brace themselves before the more dangerous shaking begins.\n\nSeismologist Dr. Hana Fujimori, who has worked extensively on early warning system development, cautions against overstating what these systems can currently achieve. "People sometimes assume early warning means the same thing as earthquake prediction," she has noted, "and that confusion can lead to serious disappointment or even distrust when the system does exactly what it was actually designed to do: provide a short warning after an earthquake has already started, not advance notice before one begins." Fujimori emphasizes that public education about this fundamental distinction, prediction versus rapid post-onset detection and warning, remains just as important to a system\'s overall effectiveness as the underlying seismic sensor technology itself, since a public that misunderstands what kind of warning to expect may respond poorly even when the system performs exactly as its designers intended.\n\nOngoing research continues to focus on reducing the processing delay between initial P-wave detection and alert issuance, since even fractions of a second matter considerably for locations closest to an earthquake\'s epicenter, where the available warning window is already at its physical minimum. Some researchers are also exploring whether analyzing the very earliest, most subtle characteristics of a detected P-wave signature might eventually allow systems to estimate an earthquake\'s ultimate magnitude more quickly and more accurately than current methods generally allow, potentially extending the practically useful warning window at least somewhat further, even though a fundamental blind zone immediately surrounding any earthquake\'s epicenter is likely to remain an unavoidable physical limitation of this general early warning approach for the foreseeable future.',
    questionText: 'According to the passage, what do earthquake early warning systems actually do?',
    options: [
      'They predict earthquakes before they occur',
      'They detect initial, less destructive seismic waves and issue an alert before more damaging waves arrive',
      'They prevent earthquakes from causing any structural damage whatsoever',
      'They only function for earthquakes above a certain magnitude threshold',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The passage states directly that these systems "do not predict earthquakes before they occur" but instead "detect the initial, less destructive seismic waves and issue an alert before the more damaging waves arrive." The passage explicitly denies that these systems predict earthquakes before they occur, directly contradicting the first option. The passage does not claim these systems prevent structural damage entirely; it describes them as enabling protective actions that can reduce, not eliminate, damage and risk. The passage does not mention any magnitude threshold limiting when these systems function. ACT Tip: When a passage opens by explicitly correcting a common misconception, that corrected explanation is often the passage\'s central factual claim and a likely source for direct comprehension questions.',
  ),
