    passageText: 'Agricultural researchers studied the relationship between soil moisture content (as a percentage of soil weight) and corn crop yield (in bushels per acre) across five test plots over a single growing season, with each plot receiving a different, controlled irrigation schedule. Table 1 shows the average soil moisture and resulting yield for each plot.\n\nTable 1: Soil Moisture Content and Corn Yield by Plot\n\nPlot 1: Soil moisture 12%, Yield 98 bushels/acre\nPlot 2: Soil moisture 18%, Yield 142 bushels/acre\nPlot 3: Soil moisture 24%, Yield 176 bushels/acre\nPlot 4: Soil moisture 30%, Yield 168 bushels/acre\nPlot 5: Soil moisture 36%, Yield 121 bushels/acre\n\nResearchers noted that soil moisture above approximately 32% is generally associated with reduced oxygen availability to corn roots, a condition sometimes called waterlogging, which can begin to suppress yield despite abundant water availability.',
