    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Agricultural researchers studied the relationship between soil moisture content (as a percentage of soil weight) and corn crop yield (in bushels per acre) across five test plots over a single growing season, with each plot receiving a different, controlled irrigation schedule. Table 1 shows the average soil moisture and resulting yield for each plot.\n\nTable 1: Soil Moisture Content and Corn Yield by Plot\n\nPlot 1: Soil moisture 12%, Yield 98 bushels/acre\nPlot 2: Soil moisture 18%, Yield 142 bushels/acre\nPlot 3: Soil moisture 24%, Yield 176 bushels/acre\nPlot 4: Soil moisture 30%, Yield 168 bushels/acre\nPlot 5: Soil moisture 36%, Yield 121 bushels/acre\n\nResearchers noted that soil moisture above approximately 32% is generally associated with reduced oxygen availability to corn roots, a condition sometimes called waterlogging, which can begin to suppress yield despite abundant water availability.',
    questionText: 'According to Table 1, which plot had a soil moisture content closest to 20%?',
    options: [
      'Plot 1',
      'Plot 2',
      'Plot 3',
      'Plot 4',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Plot 2 has a soil moisture of 18%, which is closer to 20% than any other plot\'s value. Plot 1\'s moisture of 12% is 8 percentage points from 20%, farther than Plot 2\'s 2 percentage point difference. Plot 3\'s moisture of 24% is 4 percentage points from 20%, farther than Plot 2\'s 2 percentage point difference. Plot 4\'s moisture of 30% is 10 percentage points from 20%, considerably farther than Plot 2\'s difference. ACT Tip: When asked to find the value closest to a target number, calculate the absolute difference between the target and each candidate value before selecting the smallest difference.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-emi-006',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Agricultural researchers studied the relationship between soil moisture content (as a percentage of soil weight) and corn crop yield (in bushels per acre) across five test plots over a single growing season, with each plot receiving a different, controlled irrigation schedule. Table 1 shows the average soil moisture and resulting yield for each plot.\n\nTable 1: Soil Moisture Content and Corn Yield by Plot\n\nPlot 1: Soil moisture 12%, Yield 98 bushels/acre\nPlot 2: Soil moisture 18%, Yield 142 bushels/acre\nPlot 3: Soil moisture 24%, Yield 176 bushels/acre\nPlot 4: Soil moisture 30%, Yield 168 bushels/acre\nPlot 5: Soil moisture 36%, Yield 121 bushels/acre\n\nResearchers noted that soil moisture above approximately 32% is generally associated with reduced oxygen availability to corn roots, a condition sometimes called waterlogging, which can begin to suppress yield despite abundant water availability.',
    questionText: 'A researcher hypothesizes that corn yield increases continuously and indefinitely as soil moisture increases, with no upper limit. Do the results in Table 1 support this hypothesis?',
    options: [
      'Yes, because yield increased from Plot 1 through Plot 5 without exception',
      'No, because yield increased from Plot 1 through Plot 3 but then decreased in Plot 4 and Plot 5, indicating an optimal moisture range rather than a continuously increasing relationship',
      'Yes, because Plot 5, with the highest moisture, also had the highest yield',
      'No, because soil moisture had no measurable relationship to yield in any of the five plots',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The data shows yield rising from 98 to 142 to 176 bushels/acre across Plots 1 through 3, but then falling to 168 and 121 bushels/acre in Plots 4 and 5, directly contradicting a claim of continuous, unlimited increase and instead showing an optimal moisture range around Plot 3\'s level. Yield did not increase without exception across all five plots; it clearly decreased between Plot 3 and Plot 5, making the first option factually incorrect. Plot 5, despite having the highest moisture, had one of the lowest yields in the data set, directly contradicting the claim that it also had the highest yield. The data shows a clear, substantial relationship between moisture and yield across the five plots, directly contradicting a claim of no measurable relationship. ACT Tip: To evaluate a claim of a continuously increasing or indefinite trend, check the data across its full range, including the highest values, rather than assuming an early increasing pattern continues unchanged.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-iod-007',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Agricultural researchers studied the relationship between soil moisture content (as a percentage of soil weight) and corn crop yield (in bushels per acre) across five test plots over a single growing season, with each plot receiving a different, controlled irrigation schedule. Table 1 shows the average soil moisture and resulting yield for each plot.\n\nTable 1: Soil Moisture Content and Corn Yield by Plot\n\nPlot 1: Soil moisture 12%, Yield 98 bushels/acre\nPlot 2: Soil moisture 18%, Yield 142 bushels/acre\nPlot 3: Soil moisture 24%, Yield 176 bushels/acre\nPlot 4: Soil moisture 30%, Yield 168 bushels/acre\nPlot 5: Soil moisture 36%, Yield 121 bushels/acre\n\nResearchers noted that soil moisture above approximately 32% is generally associated with reduced oxygen availability to corn roots, a condition sometimes called waterlogging, which can begin to suppress yield despite abundant water availability.',
    questionText: 'Based on the pattern shown in Table 1, if a sixth plot had a soil moisture content of approximately 21%, its yield would most likely be closest to which of the following?',
    options: [
      '145 bushels/acre',
      '159 bushels/acre',
      '168 bushels/acre',
      '176 bushels/acre',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'A soil moisture of 21% falls roughly halfway between Plot 2\'s 18% (142 bushels/acre) and Plot 3\'s 24% (176 bushels/acre). Interpolating roughly halfway between these two yields gives an estimate close to (142 + 176) / 2 = 159 bushels/acre. A value of 145 bushels/acre is too close to Plot 2\'s actual yield and does not adequately account for the increase toward Plot 3\'s higher yield. A value of 168 bushels/acre matches Plot 4\'s actual yield at a higher, non-comparable moisture level, overshooting the expected interpolated value for 21% moisture. A value of 176 bushels/acre matches Plot 3\'s yield exactly, which would be inappropriate for a moisture level that falls before reaching Plot 3\'s 24% level. ACT Tip: When estimating a value between two known data points, interpolate roughly proportionally to how far the target value falls between the two nearest known values.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-emi-008',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Agricultural researchers studied the relationship between soil moisture content (as a percentage of soil weight) and corn crop yield (in bushels per acre) across five test plots over a single growing season, with each plot receiving a different, controlled irrigation schedule. Table 1 shows the average soil moisture and resulting yield for each plot.\n\nTable 1: Soil Moisture Content and Corn Yield by Plot\n\nPlot 1: Soil moisture 12%, Yield 98 bushels/acre\nPlot 2: Soil moisture 18%, Yield 142 bushels/acre\nPlot 3: Soil moisture 24%, Yield 176 bushels/acre\nPlot 4: Soil moisture 30%, Yield 168 bushels/acre\nPlot 5: Soil moisture 36%, Yield 121 bushels/acre\n\nResearchers noted that soil moisture above approximately 32% is generally associated with reduced oxygen availability to corn roots, a condition sometimes called waterlogging, which can begin to suppress yield despite abundant water availability.',
    questionText: 'Based on Table 1, a farmer aiming to maximize corn yield should target a soil moisture level closest to which value?',
    options: [
      '12%',
      '18%',
      '24%',
      '36%',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'Plot 3, with a soil moisture of 24%, produced the highest yield of all five plots, 176 bushels/acre, making this the moisture level a farmer should target to maximize yield based on the data provided. A moisture level of 12% corresponds to Plot 1, which had the lowest yield among all plots. A moisture level of 18% corresponds to Plot 2, which had a lower yield than Plot 3. A moisture level of 36% corresponds to Plot 5, which had a substantially lower yield than Plot 3, consistent with the waterlogging effect described in the passage. ACT Tip: When asked to identify an optimal value for maximizing an outcome, locate the specific data point corresponding to the highest measured result rather than assuming higher input values always produce better outcomes.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-iod-009',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Engineers testing a new lithium-ion battery design measured how the battery\'s maximum charge capacity (as a percentage of its original, brand-new capacity) declined as the battery underwent repeated charge-discharge cycles. Figure 1 presents the results at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Battery Capacity Retention by Number of Charge Cycles\n\n0 cycles: 100% capacity\n200 cycles: 96% capacity\n400 cycles: 91% capacity\n600 cycles: 84% capacity\n800 cycles: 74% capacity\n1000 cycles: 61% capacity\n\nEngineers noted that most manufacturers consider a battery to have reached the end of its useful service life once capacity drops below 70% of its original value, since performance and reliability tend to decline sharply beyond that point.',
    questionText: 'According to Figure 1, what was the battery\'s capacity retention at 600 cycles?',
    options: [
      '84%',
      '91%',
      '74%',
      '61%',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Figure 1 lists the capacity retention at 600 cycles as 84%. The value 91% corresponds to 400 cycles, not 600 cycles. The value 74% corresponds to 800 cycles, not 600 cycles. The value 61% corresponds to 1000 cycles, not 600 cycles. ACT Tip: When several data points have closely spaced values, trace the exact cycle count stated in the question to the matching data point before selecting an answer.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-iod-010',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Engineers testing a new lithium-ion battery design measured how the battery\'s maximum charge capacity (as a percentage of its original, brand-new capacity) declined as the battery underwent repeated charge-discharge cycles. Figure 1 presents the results at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Battery Capacity Retention by Number of Charge Cycles\n\n0 cycles: 100% capacity\n200 cycles: 96% capacity\n400 cycles: 91% capacity\n600 cycles: 84% capacity\n800 cycles: 74% capacity\n1000 cycles: 61% capacity\n\nEngineers noted that most manufacturers consider a battery to have reached the end of its useful service life once capacity drops below 70% of its original value, since performance and reliability tend to decline sharply beyond that point.',
    questionText: 'According to Figure 1, between which two consecutive measurement points did the battery experience the greatest decline in capacity?',
    options: [
      '0 to 200 cycles',
      '400 to 600 cycles',
      '600 to 800 cycles',
      '800 to 1000 cycles',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'The decline from 800 to 1000 cycles is 74% - 61% = 13 percentage points, the largest single-interval decline among the five intervals. The decline from 0 to 200 cycles is only 100% - 96% = 4 percentage points. The decline from 400 to 600 cycles is 91% - 84% = 7 percentage points, smaller than the 800-to-1000 interval. The decline from 600 to 800 cycles is 84% - 74% = 10 percentage points, still smaller than the 800-to-1000 interval\'s 13-point decline. ACT Tip: To find the interval with the greatest change, calculate the difference for every consecutive pair of data points rather than assuming the largest change occurs at either the beginning or the end.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-emi-011',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Engineers testing a new lithium-ion battery design measured how the battery\'s maximum charge capacity (as a percentage of its original, brand-new capacity) declined as the battery underwent repeated charge-discharge cycles. Figure 1 presents the results at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Battery Capacity Retention by Number of Charge Cycles\n\n0 cycles: 100% capacity\n200 cycles: 96% capacity\n400 cycles: 91% capacity\n600 cycles: 84% capacity\n800 cycles: 74% capacity\n1000 cycles: 61% capacity\n\nEngineers noted that most manufacturers consider a battery to have reached the end of its useful service life once capacity drops below 70% of its original value, since performance and reliability tend to decline sharply beyond that point.',
    questionText: 'Based on Figure 1 and the passage\'s note about end-of-service-life capacity, at approximately how many charge cycles would this battery be expected to reach the end of its useful service life?',
    options: [
      'Between 600 and 800 cycles',
      'Between 800 and 1000 cycles',
      'Exactly at 1000 cycles',
      'The battery never reaches end-of-service-life capacity within the tested range',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The passage defines end-of-service-life as capacity dropping below 70%. At 800 cycles, capacity is 74%, still above this threshold, while at 1000 cycles, capacity has dropped to 61%, below the threshold, meaning the battery crosses the 70% threshold sometime between these two measurement points. Between 600 and 800 cycles, capacity remains at 84% and 74% respectively, both still above the 70% threshold, so the battery has not yet reached end-of-service-life in this interval. At exactly 1000 cycles, capacity is already well below 70%, meaning the threshold was crossed at some point before this measurement, not exactly at it. The data clearly shows capacity falling below 70% by the 1000-cycle measurement, directly contradicting the claim that the battery never reaches end-of-service-life capacity within the tested range. ACT Tip: To determine when a value crosses a specific threshold, identify the two consecutive measurements between which the threshold value falls, rather than selecting the measurement point closest to the threshold.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-iod-012',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Engineers testing a new lithium-ion battery design measured how the battery\'s maximum charge capacity (as a percentage of its original, brand-new capacity) declined as the battery underwent repeated charge-discharge cycles. Figure 1 presents the results at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Battery Capacity Retention by Number of Charge Cycles\n\n0 cycles: 100% capacity\n200 cycles: 96% capacity\n400 cycles: 91% capacity\n600 cycles: 84% capacity\n800 cycles: 74% capacity\n1000 cycles: 61% capacity\n\nEngineers noted that most manufacturers consider a battery to have reached the end of its useful service life once capacity drops below 70% of its original value, since performance and reliability tend to decline sharply beyond that point.',
    questionText: 'According to Figure 1, what was the total decline in capacity from 0 cycles to 800 cycles?',
    options: [
      '16 percentage points',
      '20 percentage points',
      '26 percentage points',
      '30 percentage points',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'At 0 cycles, capacity was 100%, and at 800 cycles, capacity was 74%. The difference is 100 - 74 = 26 percentage points. ACT Tip: When calculating a total change across multiple intervals, subtract the final value from the initial value directly rather than summing each individual interval\'s change, which risks arithmetic errors.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-emi-013',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Engineers testing a new lithium-ion battery design measured how the battery\'s maximum charge capacity (as a percentage of its original, brand-new capacity) declined as the battery underwent repeated charge-discharge cycles. Figure 1 presents the results at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Battery Capacity Retention by Number of Charge Cycles\n\n0 cycles: 100% capacity\n200 cycles: 96% capacity\n400 cycles: 91% capacity\n600 cycles: 84% capacity\n800 cycles: 74% capacity\n1000 cycles: 61% capacity\n\nEngineers noted that most manufacturers consider a battery to have reached the end of its useful service life once capacity drops below 70% of its original value, since performance and reliability tend to decline sharply beyond that point.',
    questionText: 'Based on Figure 1, which of the following best describes the overall pattern of capacity decline as the number of charge cycles increases?',
    options: [
      'Capacity declines at a constant, unchanging rate throughout the entire tested range',
      'Capacity declines slowly at first and then at an increasingly faster rate as the number of cycles increases',
      'Capacity declines rapidly at first and then levels off completely with no further decline',
      'Capacity remains completely constant regardless of the number of charge cycles',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The interval declines are 4, 5, 7, 10, and 13 percentage points across the five consecutive 200-cycle intervals, showing a pattern of accelerating decline rather than a constant rate. The declines are not constant; they clearly grow larger with each successive interval, directly contradicting a claim of a constant, unchanging rate. The data shows the smallest declines occurring early and the largest declines occurring late, directly contradicting a claim of rapid initial decline followed by leveling off. The data shows substantial, changing declines throughout the tested range, directly contradicting a claim that capacity remains completely constant. ACT Tip: To characterize an overall trend as accelerating, constant, or decelerating, calculate the change between each consecutive pair of data points and compare how those changes themselves change over the data set.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-iod-014',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Engineers testing a new lithium-ion battery design measured how the battery\'s maximum charge capacity (as a percentage of its original, brand-new capacity) declined as the battery underwent repeated charge-discharge cycles. Figure 1 presents the results at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Battery Capacity Retention by Number of Charge Cycles\n\n0 cycles: 100% capacity\n200 cycles: 96% capacity\n400 cycles: 91% capacity\n600 cycles: 84% capacity\n800 cycles: 74% capacity\n1000 cycles: 61% capacity\n\nEngineers noted that most manufacturers consider a battery to have reached the end of its useful service life once capacity drops below 70% of its original value, since performance and reliability tend to decline sharply beyond that point.',
    questionText: 'According to Figure 1, at how many cycles did the battery first drop below 90% capacity?',
    options: [
      '200 cycles',
      '400 cycles',
      '600 cycles',
      '800 cycles',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'At 400 cycles, capacity was 91%, still above 90%. At 600 cycles, capacity dropped to 84%, the first measurement below 90%. At 200 cycles, capacity was 96%, well above 90%. At 800 cycles, capacity was 74%, also below 90%, but this is not the first measurement point at which capacity dropped below 90%, since that occurred earlier at 600 cycles. ACT Tip: When asked for the first time a threshold is crossed, check each measurement point in sequence rather than selecting a later point where the value is also below the threshold.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-emi-015',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Engineers testing a new lithium-ion battery design measured how the battery\'s maximum charge capacity (as a percentage of its original, brand-new capacity) declined as the battery underwent repeated charge-discharge cycles. Figure 1 presents the results at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Battery Capacity Retention by Number of Charge Cycles\n\n0 cycles: 100% capacity\n200 cycles: 96% capacity\n400 cycles: 91% capacity\n600 cycles: 84% capacity\n800 cycles: 74% capacity\n1000 cycles: 61% capacity\n\nEngineers noted that most manufacturers consider a battery to have reached the end of its useful service life once capacity drops below 70% of its original value, since performance and reliability tend to decline sharply beyond that point.',
    questionText: 'An engineer proposes that this battery design could safely reach 1200 charge cycles while still exceeding 70% capacity, based on extrapolating a linear trend from the 0-to-400-cycle data alone. Is this proposal well supported by the full data set in Figure 1?',
    options: [
      'Yes, because the decline from 0 to 400 cycles is roughly linear and can reliably be extended to 1200 cycles',
      'No, because the rate of capacity decline clearly accelerates in later intervals, meaning a linear extrapolation based only on early, slower-declining data would significantly overestimate capacity at 1200 cycles',
      'Yes, because capacity at 1000 cycles remains above 70%, supporting the extrapolation to 1200 cycles',
      'No, because the battery\'s capacity actually increased between 800 and 1000 cycles',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Since the data shows accelerating decline, with later intervals losing significantly more capacity than earlier ones, a linear extrapolation based only on the slower, early decline rate from 0 to 400 cycles would substantially overestimate capacity at 1200 cycles, making the engineer\'s proposal poorly supported by the full data set. The decline from 0 to 400 cycles, while relatively slow, is not representative of the accelerating pattern shown across the full data set, making an extrapolation based only on this early segment unreliable for predicting later values. Capacity at 1000 cycles is 61%, which is below 70%, not above it, directly contradicting the claim that capacity at 1000 cycles supports the extrapolation. Capacity clearly decreased, not increased, between 800 and 1000 cycles, falling from 74% to 61%, making this stated reason factually incorrect even though the conclusion that the proposal is unsupported happens to be correct for a different reason. ACT Tip: When evaluating a proposed extrapolation, check whether the trend used for extrapolation is representative of the data\'s full behavior, since a trend based on an early, unrepresentative segment can significantly mislead predictions about later values.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-sci-016',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Horticulture students conducted three experiments to study how different fertilizer types affect the growth of tomato plants grown in identical greenhouse conditions, with all plants receiving the same amount of water, sunlight (14 hours per day), and starting soil composition.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents grew tomato plants under four fertilizer conditions: a nitrogen-heavy fertilizer, a phosphorus-heavy fertilizer, a balanced fertilizer containing equal proportions of nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium, and a no-fertilizer control. After 30 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nNitrogen-heavy: 58 cm\nPhosphorus-heavy: 39 cm\nBalanced: 52 cm\nNo fertilizer (control): 31 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four fertilizer conditions, researchers measured the average number of flower buds per plant after 30 days.\nNitrogen-heavy: 6\nPhosphorus-heavy: 14\nBalanced: 11\nNo fertilizer (control): 4\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of plants using only the balanced fertilizer, varying the frequency of application to determine an optimal schedule. They measured average plant height after 30 days.\nApplied once per week: 44 cm\nApplied twice per week: 52 cm\nApplied three times per week: 55 cm\nApplied daily: 47 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, plants were randomly assigned to conditions, and each condition included 20 plants whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 1, which fertilizer condition produced the greatest average plant height after 30 days?',
    options: [
      'Nitrogen-heavy',
      'Phosphorus-heavy',
      'Balanced',
      'No fertilizer (control)',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Experiment 1 reports the nitrogen-heavy condition produced an average height of 58 cm, the highest value among the four conditions (58, 39, 52, and 31 cm). Phosphorus-heavy produced a lower height of 39 cm. Balanced produced 52 cm, higher than phosphorus-heavy but still lower than nitrogen-heavy. The no-fertilizer control produced the lowest height of all four conditions, 31 cm. ACT Tip: When a question asks for the greatest or least value among several listed conditions, scan all values in the relevant experiment before selecting an answer.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-sci-017',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Horticulture students conducted three experiments to study how different fertilizer types affect the growth of tomato plants grown in identical greenhouse conditions, with all plants receiving the same amount of water, sunlight (14 hours per day), and starting soil composition.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents grew tomato plants under four fertilizer conditions: a nitrogen-heavy fertilizer, a phosphorus-heavy fertilizer, a balanced fertilizer containing equal proportions of nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium, and a no-fertilizer control. After 30 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nNitrogen-heavy: 58 cm\nPhosphorus-heavy: 39 cm\nBalanced: 52 cm\nNo fertilizer (control): 31 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four fertilizer conditions, researchers measured the average number of flower buds per plant after 30 days.\nNitrogen-heavy: 6\nPhosphorus-heavy: 14\nBalanced: 11\nNo fertilizer (control): 4\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of plants using only the balanced fertilizer, varying the frequency of application to determine an optimal schedule. They measured average plant height after 30 days.\nApplied once per week: 44 cm\nApplied twice per week: 52 cm\nApplied three times per week: 55 cm\nApplied daily: 47 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, plants were randomly assigned to conditions, and each condition included 20 plants whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'What was the purpose of including a no-fertilizer control condition in Experiments 1 and 2?',
    options: [
      'To provide a baseline representing growth without any added fertilizer, against which the effects of different fertilizer types could be compared',
      'To determine the maximum possible height tomato plants could reach under any condition',
      'To test whether tomato plants could survive without any water at all',
      'To measure the effect of sunlight duration on plant growth independent of fertilizer',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The no-fertilizer control, producing the lowest height and bud counts in both experiments, establishes a baseline against which the effects of nitrogen-heavy, phosphorus-heavy, and balanced fertilizers can be meaningfully measured as improvements or changes relative to unfertilized growth. The control condition did not produce the maximum height or bud count in either experiment; it produced the lowest values, showing the control was not intended to represent an upper limit. All conditions in the passage involve identical water levels held constant across all plants, meaning the control was not designed to test survival without water. Sunlight duration is explicitly described as held constant at 14 hours per day across all conditions, meaning the control was not designed to isolate the effect of varying sunlight. ACT Tip: A control condition in a treatment-comparison experiment typically establishes a baseline, not necessarily the best or most extreme outcome, and its purpose is to isolate the effect of the variable being tested.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-sci-018',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Horticulture students conducted three experiments to study how different fertilizer types affect the growth of tomato plants grown in identical greenhouse conditions, with all plants receiving the same amount of water, sunlight (14 hours per day), and starting soil composition.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents grew tomato plants under four fertilizer conditions: a nitrogen-heavy fertilizer, a phosphorus-heavy fertilizer, a balanced fertilizer containing equal proportions of nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium, and a no-fertilizer control. After 30 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nNitrogen-heavy: 58 cm\nPhosphorus-heavy: 39 cm\nBalanced: 52 cm\nNo fertilizer (control): 31 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four fertilizer conditions, researchers measured the average number of flower buds per plant after 30 days.\nNitrogen-heavy: 6\nPhosphorus-heavy: 14\nBalanced: 11\nNo fertilizer (control): 4\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of plants using only the balanced fertilizer, varying the frequency of application to determine an optimal schedule. They measured average plant height after 30 days.\nApplied once per week: 44 cm\nApplied twice per week: 52 cm\nApplied three times per week: 55 cm\nApplied daily: 47 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, plants were randomly assigned to conditions, and each condition included 20 plants whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 2, which fertilizer condition produced the greatest average number of flower buds per plant?',
    options: [
      'Nitrogen-heavy',
      'Phosphorus-heavy',
      'Balanced',
      'No fertilizer (control)',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Experiment 2 reports the phosphorus-heavy condition produced an average of 14 flower buds per plant, the highest value among the four conditions (6, 14, 11, and 4). Nitrogen-heavy produced only 6 buds, notably lower than phosphorus-heavy. Balanced produced 11 buds, higher than nitrogen-heavy but still lower than phosphorus-heavy. The no-fertilizer control produced the fewest buds of all four conditions, 4. ACT Tip: Even when two experiments measure different variables, such as height and flower bud count, check each experiment\'s data independently rather than assuming the same condition will rank highest in both.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
