    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Horticulture students conducted three experiments to study how different fertilizer types affect the growth of tomato plants grown in identical greenhouse conditions, with all plants receiving the same amount of water, sunlight (14 hours per day), and starting soil composition.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents grew tomato plants under four fertilizer conditions: a nitrogen-heavy fertilizer, a phosphorus-heavy fertilizer, a balanced fertilizer containing equal proportions of nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium, and a no-fertilizer control. After 30 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nNitrogen-heavy: 58 cm\nPhosphorus-heavy: 39 cm\nBalanced: 52 cm\nNo fertilizer (control): 31 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four fertilizer conditions, researchers measured the average number of flower buds per plant after 30 days.\nNitrogen-heavy: 6\nPhosphorus-heavy: 14\nBalanced: 11\nNo fertilizer (control): 4\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of plants using only the balanced fertilizer, varying the frequency of application to determine an optimal schedule. They measured average plant height after 30 days.\nApplied once per week: 44 cm\nApplied twice per week: 52 cm\nApplied three times per week: 55 cm\nApplied daily: 47 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, plants were randomly assigned to conditions, and each condition included 20 plants whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiments 1 and 2 together, which of the following statements is best supported by the data?',
    options: [
      'Nitrogen-heavy fertilizer promotes greater height growth but fewer flower buds compared to phosphorus-heavy fertilizer',
      'Phosphorus-heavy fertilizer promotes both the greatest height growth and the greatest number of flower buds',
      'The no-fertilizer control produced the best results in both height and flower bud count',
      'Balanced fertilizer produced the lowest results in both height and flower bud count',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Nitrogen-heavy fertilizer produced the greatest height (58 cm) in Experiment 1 but only 6 flower buds in Experiment 2, while phosphorus-heavy fertilizer produced a shorter height (39 cm) but the greatest number of flower buds (14), directly supporting this statement\'s description of a trade-off between the two fertilizer types. Phosphorus-heavy fertilizer produced the greatest number of flower buds but not the greatest height, which nitrogen-heavy fertilizer achieved instead, directly contradicting this option\'s claim that phosphorus-heavy achieved both. The no-fertilizer control produced the lowest values in both experiments, directly contradicting a claim that it produced the best results in either measure. Balanced fertilizer produced the second-highest values in both experiments, not the lowest, directly contradicting this option. ACT Tip: When comparing results across two experiments, check whether a single condition dominates both measures or whether different conditions excel at different measures, revealing a trade-off.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-sci-020',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Horticulture students conducted three experiments to study how different fertilizer types affect the growth of tomato plants grown in identical greenhouse conditions, with all plants receiving the same amount of water, sunlight (14 hours per day), and starting soil composition.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents grew tomato plants under four fertilizer conditions: a nitrogen-heavy fertilizer, a phosphorus-heavy fertilizer, a balanced fertilizer containing equal proportions of nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium, and a no-fertilizer control. After 30 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nNitrogen-heavy: 58 cm\nPhosphorus-heavy: 39 cm\nBalanced: 52 cm\nNo fertilizer (control): 31 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four fertilizer conditions, researchers measured the average number of flower buds per plant after 30 days.\nNitrogen-heavy: 6\nPhosphorus-heavy: 14\nBalanced: 11\nNo fertilizer (control): 4\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of plants using only the balanced fertilizer, varying the frequency of application to determine an optimal schedule. They measured average plant height after 30 days.\nApplied once per week: 44 cm\nApplied twice per week: 52 cm\nApplied three times per week: 55 cm\nApplied daily: 47 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, plants were randomly assigned to conditions, and each condition included 20 plants whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 3, what was the optimal fertilizer application frequency for maximizing average plant height?',
    options: [
      'Once per week',
      'Twice per week',
      'Three times per week',
      'Daily',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'Among the four application frequencies tested in Experiment 3, applying the balanced fertilizer three times per week produced the greatest average height, 55 cm, higher than 44 cm, 52 cm, and 47 cm for the other three frequencies. Applying once per week produced a lower height of 44 cm. Applying twice per week produced 52 cm, higher than once per week but still lower than three times per week. Applying daily produced 47 cm, lower than three times per week, suggesting excessive application frequency may reduce effectiveness. ACT Tip: When an experiment tests several frequencies or amounts of a variable, identify the single value that produces the highest (or lowest) outcome rather than assuming more frequent application always produces better results.',
  ),
