  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-emi-024',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Horticulture students conducted three experiments to study how different fertilizer types affect the growth of tomato plants grown in identical greenhouse conditions, with all plants receiving the same amount of water, sunlight (14 hours per day), and starting soil composition.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents grew tomato plants under four fertilizer conditions: a nitrogen-heavy fertilizer, a phosphorus-heavy fertilizer, a balanced fertilizer containing equal proportions of nitrogen, phosphorus, and potassium, and a no-fertilizer control. After 30 days, researchers measured average plant height in centimeters.\nNitrogen-heavy: 58 cm\nPhosphorus-heavy: 39 cm\nBalanced: 52 cm\nNo fertilizer (control): 31 cm\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four fertilizer conditions, researchers measured the average number of flower buds per plant after 30 days.\nNitrogen-heavy: 6\nPhosphorus-heavy: 14\nBalanced: 11\nNo fertilizer (control): 4\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers grew a new set of plants using only the balanced fertilizer, varying the frequency of application to determine an optimal schedule. They measured average plant height after 30 days.\nApplied once per week: 44 cm\nApplied twice per week: 52 cm\nApplied three times per week: 55 cm\nApplied daily: 47 cm\n\nResearchers noted that in all three experiments, plants were randomly assigned to conditions, and each condition included 20 plants whose measurements were averaged to produce the reported values.',
    questionText: 'A gardener wants to choose a fertilizer application schedule that balances strong height growth with practical convenience, avoiding excessive daily labor. Based on Experiment 3, which schedule would best achieve this goal?',
    options: [
      'Once per week, since it requires the least labor regardless of results',
      'Twice per week, since it provides a strong height result while requiring less frequent application than the schedule that produced the single highest height',
      'Daily, since more frequent application should always produce the best results',
      'Three times per week is the only schedule that produced any measurable plant growth at all',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Twice per week produced a height of 52 cm, only 3 cm less than the maximum height of 55 cm achieved by the three-times-per-week schedule, while requiring meaningfully less frequent application, making it a reasonable practical compromise between strong results and convenience. Once per week produced the lowest height among all four schedules, 44 cm, making it a poor choice for a gardener who wants strong height results even if convenience is also valued. The data explicitly shows daily application produced a lower height, 47 cm, than the three-times-per-week schedule, directly contradicting the assumption that more frequent application always produces better results. All four schedules produced substantial, measurable height growth well above the no-fertilizer control\'s height from Experiment 1, directly contradicting the claim that only the three-times-per-week schedule produced measurable growth. ACT Tip: When a question asks about balancing a strong result with practical considerations like convenience, look for an option that achieves a result close to the maximum while requiring meaningfully less effort, rather than assuming the single best-performing option is always the most practical choice.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-sci-025',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Materials science students conducted two experiments to study how different solutions affect the corrosion rate of iron nails, measuring corrosion as the percent mass loss of each nail after a fixed 14-day exposure period at room temperature (21 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents submerged identical iron nails in four different solutions: plain tap water, salt water (5% sodium chloride solution), vinegar (5% acetic acid solution), and a control condition of dry air with no liquid exposure. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\nTap water: 2.1%\nSalt water: 8.4%\nVinegar: 6.7%\nDry air (control): 0.3%\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only salt water solution, students varied the salt concentration to determine its effect on corrosion rate. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\n1% salt concentration: 4.2%\n3% salt concentration: 6.8%\n5% salt concentration: 8.4%\n8% salt concentration: 9.1%\n12% salt concentration: 8.7%\n\nStudents noted that at the highest tested concentration, 12%, the solution\'s density became noticeably higher, and some students observed salt crystals beginning to form at the bottom of the container, suggesting the solution may have approached its saturation point at room temperature.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 1, which condition produced the greatest percent mass loss after 14 days?',
    options: [
      'Tap water',
      'Salt water',
      'Vinegar',
      'Dry air (control)',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Experiment 1 reports salt water produced a mass loss of 8.4%, the highest value among the four conditions (2.1%, 8.4%, 6.7%, and 0.3%). Tap water produced a lower mass loss of 2.1%. Vinegar produced 6.7%, higher than tap water but still lower than salt water. The dry air control produced the lowest mass loss of all four conditions, 0.3%. ACT Tip: When comparing multiple conditions in a single experiment, scan the complete list of results before identifying the highest or lowest value.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-sci-026',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Materials science students conducted two experiments to study how different solutions affect the corrosion rate of iron nails, measuring corrosion as the percent mass loss of each nail after a fixed 14-day exposure period at room temperature (21 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents submerged identical iron nails in four different solutions: plain tap water, salt water (5% sodium chloride solution), vinegar (5% acetic acid solution), and a control condition of dry air with no liquid exposure. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\nTap water: 2.1%\nSalt water: 8.4%\nVinegar: 6.7%\nDry air (control): 0.3%\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only salt water solution, students varied the salt concentration to determine its effect on corrosion rate. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\n1% salt concentration: 4.2%\n3% salt concentration: 6.8%\n5% salt concentration: 8.4%\n8% salt concentration: 9.1%\n12% salt concentration: 8.7%\n\nStudents noted that at the highest tested concentration, 12%, the solution\'s density became noticeably higher, and some students observed salt crystals beginning to form at the bottom of the container, suggesting the solution may have approached its saturation point at room temperature.',
    questionText: 'What was the purpose of the dry air control condition in Experiment 1?',
    options: [
      'To establish the baseline rate of mass loss without liquid exposure, allowing comparison to the liquid-exposed conditions',
      'To determine the maximum possible corrosion rate achievable under any condition',
      'To test whether iron nails could rust in outer space',
      'To measure the effect of temperature on corrosion rate',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The dry air control, producing only 0.3% mass loss without any liquid exposure, provides a baseline against which the effects of tap water, salt water, and vinegar can be meaningfully compared, showing how much each liquid accelerated corrosion beyond the minimal baseline rate. The control condition produced the lowest, not the highest, mass loss, so it does not represent a maximum achievable rate under any condition. The experiment does not address corrosion in outer space or any related extreme environment; it is a standard laboratory comparison. Temperature is explicitly held constant at 21 C across all conditions in the passage, meaning the control was not designed to test the effect of varying temperature. ACT Tip: A no-liquid-exposure control condition in a corrosion or treatment-comparison experiment typically establishes a baseline rate, allowing the treatment conditions\' effects to be measured as a change relative to that baseline.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-sci-027',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Materials science students conducted two experiments to study how different solutions affect the corrosion rate of iron nails, measuring corrosion as the percent mass loss of each nail after a fixed 14-day exposure period at room temperature (21 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents submerged identical iron nails in four different solutions: plain tap water, salt water (5% sodium chloride solution), vinegar (5% acetic acid solution), and a control condition of dry air with no liquid exposure. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\nTap water: 2.1%\nSalt water: 8.4%\nVinegar: 6.7%\nDry air (control): 0.3%\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only salt water solution, students varied the salt concentration to determine its effect on corrosion rate. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\n1% salt concentration: 4.2%\n3% salt concentration: 6.8%\n5% salt concentration: 8.4%\n8% salt concentration: 9.1%\n12% salt concentration: 8.7%\n\nStudents noted that at the highest tested concentration, 12%, the solution\'s density became noticeably higher, and some students observed salt crystals beginning to form at the bottom of the container, suggesting the solution may have approached its saturation point at room temperature.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 2, at which salt concentration was the greatest percent mass loss recorded?',
    options: [
      '1%',
      '3%',
      '8%',
      '12%',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'Experiment 2 reports 9.1% mass loss at 8% salt concentration, the highest value among the five concentrations tested (4.2%, 6.8%, 8.4%, 9.1%, and 8.7%). At 1% concentration, 4.2% mass loss was recorded, lower than at 8%. At 3% concentration, 6.8% mass loss was recorded, higher than at 1% but still lower than at 8%. At 12% concentration, 8.7% mass loss was recorded, notably lower than the peak at 8%. ACT Tip: When a data set shows values rising and then falling across a range, such as concentration here, identify the single peak value rather than assuming the highest value occurs at the highest or lowest tested point.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-emi-028',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Materials science students conducted two experiments to study how different solutions affect the corrosion rate of iron nails, measuring corrosion as the percent mass loss of each nail after a fixed 14-day exposure period at room temperature (21 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents submerged identical iron nails in four different solutions: plain tap water, salt water (5% sodium chloride solution), vinegar (5% acetic acid solution), and a control condition of dry air with no liquid exposure. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\nTap water: 2.1%\nSalt water: 8.4%\nVinegar: 6.7%\nDry air (control): 0.3%\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only salt water solution, students varied the salt concentration to determine its effect on corrosion rate. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\n1% salt concentration: 4.2%\n3% salt concentration: 6.8%\n5% salt concentration: 8.4%\n8% salt concentration: 9.1%\n12% salt concentration: 8.7%\n\nStudents noted that at the highest tested concentration, 12%, the solution\'s density became noticeably higher, and some students observed salt crystals beginning to form at the bottom of the container, suggesting the solution may have approached its saturation point at room temperature.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiment 2 and the students\' note about salt crystal formation at 12% concentration, which of the following best explains why mass loss decreased between 8% and 12% concentration?',
    options: [
      'Iron nails stop corroding entirely at any concentration above 8%',
      'The solution may have approached its saturation point at 12%, with some dissolved salt beginning to crystallize out, potentially reducing the effective concentration of dissolved ions available to accelerate corrosion',
      'The measurement device could no longer detect mass loss at higher concentrations',
      'The 12% trial used a smaller nail than the other trials',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The passage notes that at 12% concentration, "salt crystals beginning to form at the bottom of the container, suggesting the solution may have approached its saturation point," meaning some salt was no longer fully dissolved, potentially reducing the effective concentration of dissolved ions actively available to accelerate the corrosion reaction, consistent with the observed decrease in mass loss at this concentration. The data clearly shows corrosion continuing to occur at 12% concentration, with a substantial 8.7% mass loss still recorded, directly contradicting the claim that corrosion stops entirely above 8%. The passage does not discuss any measurement device limitations; the students\' note specifically attributes the change to salt saturation and crystal formation, not to a detection problem. The passage does not mention nail size varying across trials; it describes identical iron nails being used throughout Experiment 1, with no indication this changed in Experiment 2. ACT Tip: When a passage includes a researcher\'s own explanatory observation about an unexpected result, use that observation as the primary basis for interpreting the data pattern rather than proposing an unrelated explanation the passage does not support.',
  ),
