    passageText: 'Materials science students conducted two experiments to study how different solutions affect the corrosion rate of iron nails, measuring corrosion as the percent mass loss of each nail after a fixed 14-day exposure period at room temperature (21 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents submerged identical iron nails in four different solutions: plain tap water, salt water (5% sodium chloride solution), vinegar (5% acetic acid solution), and a control condition of dry air with no liquid exposure. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\nTap water: 2.1%\nSalt water: 8.4%\nVinegar: 6.7%\nDry air (control): 0.3%\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only salt water solution, students varied the salt concentration to determine its effect on corrosion rate. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\n1% salt concentration: 4.2%\n3% salt concentration: 6.8%\n5% salt concentration: 8.4%\n8% salt concentration: 9.1%\n12% salt concentration: 8.7%\n\nStudents noted that at the highest tested concentration, 12%, the solution\'s density became noticeably higher, and some students observed salt crystals beginning to form at the bottom of the container, suggesting the solution may have approached its saturation point at room temperature.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiments 1 and 2 together, which of the following statements is best supported by the data?',
    options: [
      'Vinegar causes more corrosion than any concentration of salt water tested in the passage',
