    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Experiment 1 shows 5% salt water (8.4% mass loss) causing more corrosion than tap water (2.1%), and Experiment 2 shows mass loss increasing from 1% to 8% concentration before declining at 12%, directly supporting this combined statement about salt water\'s effect and its concentration-dependent pattern. Experiment 1 shows vinegar (6.7%) causing less corrosion than 5% salt water (8.4%), directly contradicting the claim that vinegar causes more corrosion than any tested salt water concentration, since several salt concentrations exceeded vinegar\'s mass loss. Experiment 1 shows the dry air control produced the lowest mass loss of all conditions, 0.3%, directly contradicting a claim that it caused more corrosion than any liquid solution. Both experiments show substantial, measurable differences in mass loss depending on solution type and concentration, directly contradicting a claim that corrosion rate was completely unaffected by solution type. ACT Tip: When a question asks about a claim supported by two experiments together, verify that each part of the combined statement is separately supported by its corresponding experiment\'s specific data.',
