    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Materials science students conducted two experiments to study how different solutions affect the corrosion rate of iron nails, measuring corrosion as the percent mass loss of each nail after a fixed 14-day exposure period at room temperature (21 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents submerged identical iron nails in four different solutions: plain tap water, salt water (5% sodium chloride solution), vinegar (5% acetic acid solution), and a control condition of dry air with no liquid exposure. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\nTap water: 2.1%\nSalt water: 8.4%\nVinegar: 6.7%\nDry air (control): 0.3%\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only salt water solution, students varied the salt concentration to determine its effect on corrosion rate. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\n1% salt concentration: 4.2%\n3% salt concentration: 6.8%\n5% salt concentration: 8.4%\n8% salt concentration: 9.1%\n12% salt concentration: 8.7%\n\nStudents noted that at the highest tested concentration, 12%, the solution\'s density became noticeably higher, and some students observed salt crystals beginning to form at the bottom of the container, suggesting the solution may have approached its saturation point at room temperature.',
    questionText: 'Why did the students use identical iron nails and hold the exposure duration constant at 14 days across all trials in both experiments?',
    options: [
      'To isolate the effects of solution type and concentration on corrosion without the added variables of differing nail size or exposure time',
      'Because 14 days is the maximum amount of time iron can corrode before fully dissolving',
      'Because iron nails of other sizes were not available for purchase',
      'To ensure the nails would rust exactly the same amount in every trial regardless of solution',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'By keeping nail size and exposure duration constant across all trials, the students ensured that any differences in mass loss could be attributed specifically to the variable being tested, solution type in Experiment 1 or salt concentration in Experiment 2, rather than to differences in the nails themselves or how long they were exposed. The passage does not state or imply that 14 days represents any kind of maximum corrosion timeframe before an iron nail would fully dissolve; this is an unsupported claim about the chemistry involved. The passage gives no information about the availability of nails in other sizes for purchase. The data explicitly shows differing amounts of mass loss across trials, directly contradicting the claim that holding these variables constant was meant to ensure identical rusting regardless of solution. ACT Tip: When a variable is described as being held constant across an experiment\'s trials, its purpose is almost always to isolate the effect of the variable that is deliberately changed, not to force identical results.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-emi-033',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Materials science students conducted two experiments to study how different solutions affect the corrosion rate of iron nails, measuring corrosion as the percent mass loss of each nail after a fixed 14-day exposure period at room temperature (21 C).\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents submerged identical iron nails in four different solutions: plain tap water, salt water (5% sodium chloride solution), vinegar (5% acetic acid solution), and a control condition of dry air with no liquid exposure. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\nTap water: 2.1%\nSalt water: 8.4%\nVinegar: 6.7%\nDry air (control): 0.3%\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only salt water solution, students varied the salt concentration to determine its effect on corrosion rate. Percent mass loss after 14 days:\n1% salt concentration: 4.2%\n3% salt concentration: 6.8%\n5% salt concentration: 8.4%\n8% salt concentration: 9.1%\n12% salt concentration: 8.7%\n\nStudents noted that at the highest tested concentration, 12%, the solution\'s density became noticeably higher, and some students observed salt crystals beginning to form at the bottom of the container, suggesting the solution may have approached its saturation point at room temperature.',
    questionText: 'A student proposes that increasing salt concentration always increases corrosion rate, with no upper limit. Do the results of Experiment 2 support this proposal?',
    options: [
      'Yes, because mass loss increased at every single concentration tested from 1% to 12%',
      'No, because mass loss increased from 1% up to 8% but then decreased at 12%, indicating a limit rather than an indefinite increase',
      'Yes, because the highest concentration tested, 12%, also produced the highest mass loss',
      'No, because salt concentration was not actually varied in Experiment 2',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The data in Experiment 2 shows mass loss rising from 4.2% at 1% concentration to a peak of 9.1% at 8% concentration, but then falling to 8.7% at 12% concentration, directly contradicting a claim of indefinite increase and instead showing a clear upper limit or optimal point. Mass loss did not increase at every concentration tested; it decreased between 8% and 12%, making the first option factually incorrect based on the data. The highest concentration tested, 12%, produced 8.7% mass loss, which is lower than the 9.1% produced at 8%, directly contradicting the claim that the highest concentration produced the highest mass loss. Salt concentration was explicitly varied across five distinct values in Experiment 2, directly contradicting the claim that it was not varied. ACT Tip: To evaluate a claim of an indefinite or unlimited trend, check the data across its full range, including the highest values tested, rather than assuming the pattern from early values continues unchanged.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's02-science-emi-034',
    setNumber: 2,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Earth\'s oceans contain a vast quantity of water, yet the young Earth, formed roughly 4.5 billion years ago in the hot, inner region of the solar system\'s protoplanetary disk, is generally thought to have formed too hot and too close to the sun for large amounts of water to have condensed directly from the material that built the planet itself. Two scientists offer differing explanations for how Earth acquired its surface water.\n\nScientist 1: Cometary Delivery\nEarth\'s water was delivered primarily by comets, icy bodies originating in the outer solar system, that collided with the young Earth during a period of intense bombardment in the solar system\'s early history. Comets are known to contain substantial amounts of water ice, and computer models of early solar system dynamics show that gravitational interactions among the giant outer planets could have flung large numbers of comets from the outer solar system toward the inner planets, including Earth, providing a plausible delivery mechanism. This explanation gained support from early spacecraft measurements of comet water, which found that the ratio of deuterium, a heavier isotope of hydrogen, to ordinary hydrogen in cometary water closely matched the ratio found in Earth\'s ocean water, a specific chemical fingerprint that would be an unlikely coincidence if the two water sources were unrelated. Comets, having formed far from the sun in a colder region of the protoplanetary disk, would naturally retain substantial water ice that a body forming closer to the sun, like early Earth, could not have retained through direct condensation alone.\n\nScientist 2: Asteroid Delivery\nWhile comets certainly deliver some water to planets, they cannot account for the majority of Earth\'s ocean water, given how relatively rare large cometary impacts appear to have been compared to the sheer volume of water present in Earth\'s oceans. The primary source was instead water-rich asteroids, specifically a class of carbon-rich asteroids known as carbonaceous chondrites, which originate from the outer regions of the asteroid belt, a region cooler and more distant from the sun than where Earth itself formed, and which are now known, based on analysis of meteorite samples that have fallen to Earth, to contain water bound within their mineral structure in amounts far exceeding what typical comets contain per unit of impacting mass. Critically, when the deuterium-to-hydrogen ratio of these carbonaceous chondrite meteorites is measured directly, it matches Earth\'s ocean water considerably more closely and consistently than the ratio found in the relatively small number of comets that have actually been directly measured by spacecraft to date, several of which have shown deuterium ratios notably higher than Earth\'s, casting doubt on comets as the dominant source despite the intriguing matches found in a few individual cometary measurements. Given that asteroids vastly outnumber comets in the inner solar system and would have delivered far greater total mass to the young Earth during the same early bombardment period, water-rich asteroids offer a more statistically plausible primary source for the overwhelming majority of Earth\'s ocean water.',
    questionText: 'According to Scientist 1, what specific chemical fingerprint supports the cometary delivery hypothesis?',
    options: [
      'The ratio of oxygen to hydrogen in cometary water',
      'The ratio of deuterium to ordinary hydrogen in cometary water matching Earth\'s ocean water',
      'The presence of carbon in cometary ice',
      'The temperature at which comets formed in the outer solar system',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Scientist 1 explicitly cites "the ratio of deuterium, a heavier isotope of hydrogen, to ordinary hydrogen in cometary water closely matched the ratio found in Earth\'s ocean water" as the key supporting chemical fingerprint. The ratio of oxygen to hydrogen is not mentioned anywhere in the passage as evidence used by either scientist. Carbon content is discussed in relation to Scientist 2\'s carbonaceous chondrite asteroids, not as evidence Scientist 1 uses to support the cometary hypothesis. The temperature of comet formation is mentioned as context for why comets retain water ice, but it is not itself described as the specific chemical fingerprint evidence cited. ACT Tip: In a Conflicting Viewpoints passage, carefully attribute each specific piece of evidence to the correct scientist and identify precisely which detail the passage identifies as the key supporting evidence, rather than a related but distinct detail.',
  ),
