    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Two years ago, the public library system serving the city of Brightwater announced it would replace its proprietary catalog software with an open-source alternative, a shift that library director Marcus Delacroix had championed since taking the position. [1]Delacroix, along with the system\'s small IT department, spend nearly a year evaluating open-source platforms before settling on one[1]. [2]The proprietary software the library had used for over a decade came with an annual licensing fee that consumed nearly eight percent of the entire technology budget[2]. [3]Open-source software, by contrast, is typically free to use, though it often requires more in-house technical expertise to maintain and customize[3]. [4]Delacroix argued that the upfront investment in staff training would pay for itself within three years through eliminated licensing costs[4]. [5]Not every librarian on staff supported the transition enthusiastically, some worried the new system would be less reliable than the proprietary software it replaced[5]. [6]These concerns were not unfounded, the open-source platform experienced several stability issues during its first three months of full deployment[6]. [7]IT staff worked long hours during this period, patching bugs and adjusting configurations that had not surfaced during the shorter testing phase[7]. [8]By the sixth month, most of the major stability issues had been resolved, and staff reported growing comfortable with the new system[8]. [9]Patrons, for their part, mostly noticed only cosmetic differences, a redesigned search interface and slightly different due-date notification emails[9]. [10]Delacroix says the switch has already saved the library system over forty thousand dollars in licensing fees[10], money that has been redirected toward expanding the library\'s collection of digital audiobooks. [11]He acknowledges that not every library system has the technical staff capacity to manage a similar transition successfully[11]. [12]Smaller library systems, in particular, may lack the in-house expertise that made Brightwater\'s transition ultimately manageable despite its early difficulties[12]. [13]Delacroix has begun consulting informally with several neighboring library directors considering similar transitions[13]. [14]He is careful to emphasize that open-source software is not automatically the right choice for every library, only that it worked well for Brightwater\'s particular circumstances[14]. [15]Whether other library systems follow Brightwater\'s example will likely depend as much on available technical staffing as on the software\'s underlying cost advantages[15].',
