]. [12]Smaller library systems, in particular, may lack the in-house expertise that made Brightwater\'s transition ultimately manageable despite its early difficulties[12]. [13]Delacroix has begun consulting informally with several neighboring library directors considering similar transitions[13]. [14]He is careful to emphasize that open-source software is not automatically the right choice for every library, only that it worked well for Brightwater\'s particular circumstances[14]. [15]Whether other library systems follow Brightwater\'s example will likely depend as much on available technical staffing as on the software\'s underlying cost advantages[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: By the sixth month, most of the major stability issues had been resolved, and staff reported growing comfortable with the new system]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'By the sixth month, most of the major stability issues have been resolved, and staff reported growing comfortable with the new system',
      'By the sixth month, most of the major stability issues had been resolved and staff reported growing comfortable with the new system',
      'By the sixth month, most of the major stability issues had being resolved, and staff reported growing comfortable with the new system',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the past perfect "had been resolved" correctly signals a completed action before the point in time being described, and the comma before "and staff reported" correctly separates two independent clauses joined by a coordinating conjunction. "Have been resolved," in the second option, incorrectly shifts to present perfect tense, which does not match the past-tense narrative established by "By the sixth month." Removing the comma before "and," as in the third option, incorrectly joins two independent clauses without the comma a coordinating conjunction requires in that position. "Had being resolved," in the fourth option, is grammatically incorrect; the correct passive past perfect construction requires "been," not "being." ACT Tip: When a coordinating conjunction like "and" joins two independent clauses, a comma belongs directly before the conjunction.',
  ),
