  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-cse-016',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In 1887, a machinist named Eleanor Whitfield filed a patent for an improved bobbin-winding mechanism that would later become standard equipment in textile mills across New England, though her name would remain largely absent from the histories written about that transformation. [1]Whitfield had worked in a Providence textile mill since the age of sixteen, gradually earning a reputation among floor supervisors for solving mechanical problems that stumped more senior workers[1]. [2]Her bobbin-winding design reduced thread breakage by nearly forty percent, a improvement that mill owners across the region quickly recognized as commercially significant[2]. [3]Within two years of filing her patent, Whitfield had licensed the design to three separate mill equipment manufacturers[3]. [4]A fourth manufacturer, rather than licensing the design legitimately, began producing a nearly identical mechanism without Whitfield\'s permission[4]. [5]Whitfield sued for patent infringement in 1891, a decision that required her to travel to Boston repeatedly over the following two years for court proceedings[5]. [6]The case dragged on considerably longer than Whitfield had anticipated, she later wrote to a cousin that the legal costs nearly exceeded what she had earned from her legitimate licensing agreements[6]. [7]Court records from the case, rediscovered by a historian researching women inventors in the 1990s, reveal that the presiding judge initially expressed skepticism that a woman without formal engineering training could have designed the mechanism herself[7]. [8]Whitfield\'s testimony, preserved in the surviving court transcripts, describes in careful technical detail exactly how she had arrived at her design through years of hands-on observation[8]. [9]She ultimately won her infringement case in 1893, receiving a settlement that, while smaller than she had hoped, still validated her original claim[9]. [10]Despite this legal victory, Whitfield\'s name largely disappeared from subsequent accounts of the bobbin-winding mechanism\'s development, which tended to credit the manufacturers who had licensed and mass-produced her design[10]. [11]The historian who rediscovered her court records has since argued that Whitfield\'s case reflects a broader, common pattern, women inventors of the era winning legal recognition while still losing lasting historical credit[11]. [12]Whitfield herself continued working as a machinist until her retirement in 1911, filing two additional, less commercially significant patents during that time[12]. [13]Neither of these later patents generated the kind of income or recognition her original bobbin-winding design had[13]. [14]Her granddaughter, interviewed decades later for a local historical society project, recalled Whitfield rarely discussing the patent lawsuit directly, mentioning it mostly through brief, matter-of-fact asides[14]. [15]The historical society\'s project, along with the rediscovered court records, has since prompted renewed interest in properly documenting Whitfield\'s actual contribution to an invention that quietly transformed an entire industry[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Whitfield had worked in a Providence textile mill since the age of sixteen, gradually earning a reputation among floor supervisors for solving mechanical problems that stumped more senior workers]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Whitfield had worked in a Providence textile mill since the age of sixteen; gradually earning a reputation among floor supervisors for solving mechanical problems that stumped more senior workers',
      'Whitfield had worked in a Providence textile mill since the age of sixteen gradually earning a reputation among floor supervisors for solving mechanical problems that stumped more senior workers',
      'Whitfield had worked in a Providence textile mill since the age of sixteen, gradually earning, a reputation among floor supervisors for solving mechanical problems that stumped more senior workers',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the comma correctly sets off the participial phrase "gradually earning a reputation...," which describes an ongoing consequence of Whitfield\'s years working at the mill. Using a semicolon, as in the second option, is incorrect because the participial phrase is not an independent clause. Removing the comma entirely, as in the third option, fuses the modifying phrase onto the main clause without the needed pause. Adding a comma after "earning," as in the fourth option, incorrectly separates the participle from its own object, "a reputation." ACT Tip: A participial phrase describing an ongoing consequence of the main clause\'s action is typically set off from the main clause by a single comma, not a semicolon.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
