    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "arrived at" is the standard idiomatic phrase for describing how someone reaches a conclusion or design through a process of reasoning or observation. "Reached at" is not a standard idiomatic construction; "reached" is typically used without "at" in this context, as in "reached her design." "Come at" is not the standard idiom for describing reaching a conclusion through reasoning; it more commonly suggests approaching or attacking something directly. "Gotten at" is overly informal and imprecise for this context, and does not carry the standard idiomatic sense of reaching a considered conclusion through careful process. ACT Tip: Idiomatic phrasal verbs like "arrive at" have fixed forms in standard English; substituting a similar verb with a different preposition often creates a nonstandard or less precise construction.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-pow-024',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In 1887, a machinist named Eleanor Whitfield filed a patent for an improved bobbin-winding mechanism that would later become standard equipment in textile mills across New England, though her name would remain largely absent from the histories written about that transformation. [1]Whitfield had worked in a Providence textile mill since the age of sixteen, gradually earning a reputation among floor supervisors for solving mechanical problems that stumped more senior workers[1]. [2]Her bobbin-winding design reduced thread breakage by nearly forty percent, a improvement that mill owners across the region quickly recognized as commercially significant[2]. [3]Within two years of filing her patent, Whitfield had licensed the design to three separate mill equipment manufacturers[3]. [4]A fourth manufacturer, rather than licensing the design legitimately, began producing a nearly identical mechanism without Whitfield\'s permission[4]. [5]Whitfield sued for patent infringement in 1891, a decision that required her to travel to Boston repeatedly over the following two years for court proceedings[5]. [6]The case dragged on considerably longer than Whitfield had anticipated, she later wrote to a cousin that the legal costs nearly exceeded what she had earned from her legitimate licensing agreements[6]. [7]Court records from the case, rediscovered by a historian researching women inventors in the 1990s, reveal that the presiding judge initially expressed skepticism that a woman without formal engineering training could have designed the mechanism herself[7]. [8]Whitfield\'s testimony, preserved in the surviving court transcripts, describes in careful technical detail exactly how she had arrived at her design through years of hands-on observation[8]. [9]She ultimately won her infringement case in 1893, receiving a settlement that, while smaller than she had hoped, still validated her original claim[9]. [10]Despite this legal victory, Whitfield\'s name largely disappeared from subsequent accounts of the bobbin-winding mechanism\'s development, which tended to credit the manufacturers who had licensed and mass-produced her design[10]. [11]The historian who rediscovered her court records has since argued that Whitfield\'s case reflects a broader, common pattern, women inventors of the era winning legal recognition while still losing lasting historical credit[11]. [12]Whitfield herself continued working as a machinist until her retirement in 1911, filing two additional, less commercially significant patents during that time[12]. [13]Neither of these later patents generated the kind of income or recognition her original bobbin-winding design had[13]. [14]Her granddaughter, interviewed decades later for a local historical society project, recalled Whitfield rarely discussing the patent lawsuit directly, mentioning it mostly through brief, matter-of-fact asides[14]. [15]The historical society\'s project, along with the rediscovered court records, has since prompted renewed interest in properly documenting Whitfield\'s actual contribution to an invention that quietly transformed an entire industry[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: She ultimately won her infringement case in 1893, receiving a settlement that, while smaller than she had hoped, still validated her original claim]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'She ultimately won her infringement case in 1893',
      'She ultimately won her infringement case in 1893, receiving a settlement, which was nice for her',
      'She ultimately won her infringement case in 1893, receiving a settlement that, while smaller than she had hoped, still validated her original claim, an outcome that mattered',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the full sentence acknowledges both the practical limitation of the settlement\'s size and its meaningful symbolic validation, a nuanced characterization that fits the passage\'s balanced treatment of Whitfield\'s partial, complicated victory. The second option cuts this nuanced characterization entirely, losing the sentence\'s balanced acknowledgment of both limitation and validation. The third option replaces the specific, meaningful characterization with the vague, dismissive phrase "which was nice for her," losing the sentence\'s careful nuance. The fourth option adds the redundant closing phrase "an outcome that mattered," restating something the sentence\'s own description of validation already conveys. ACT Tip: When a sentence offers a nuanced, balanced characterization of an outcome, cutting or oversimplifying that nuance typically weakens the passage\'s careful, credible tone.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-cse-025',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'In 1887, a machinist named Eleanor Whitfield filed a patent for an improved bobbin-winding mechanism that would later become standard equipment in textile mills across New England, though her name would remain largely absent from the histories written about that transformation. [1]Whitfield had worked in a Providence textile mill since the age of sixteen, gradually earning a reputation among floor supervisors for solving mechanical problems that stumped more senior workers[1]. [2]Her bobbin-winding design reduced thread breakage by nearly forty percent, a improvement that mill owners across the region quickly recognized as commercially significant[2]. [3]Within two years of filing her patent, Whitfield had licensed the design to three separate mill equipment manufacturers[3]. [4]A fourth manufacturer, rather than licensing the design legitimately, began producing a nearly identical mechanism without Whitfield\'s permission[4]. [5]Whitfield sued for patent infringement in 1891, a decision that required her to travel to Boston repeatedly over the following two years for court proceedings[5]. [6]The case dragged on considerably longer than Whitfield had anticipated, she later wrote to a cousin that the legal costs nearly exceeded what she had earned from her legitimate licensing agreements[6]. [7]Court records from the case, rediscovered by a historian researching women inventors in the 1990s, reveal that the presiding judge initially expressed skepticism that a woman without formal engineering training could have designed the mechanism herself[7]. [8]Whitfield\'s testimony, preserved in the surviving court transcripts, describes in careful technical detail exactly how she had arrived at her design through years of hands-on observation[8]. [9]She ultimately won her infringement case in 1893, receiving a settlement that, while smaller than she had hoped, still validated her original claim[9]. [10]Despite this legal victory, Whitfield\'s name largely disappeared from subsequent accounts of the bobbin-winding mechanism\'s development, which tended to credit the manufacturers who had licensed and mass-produced her design[10]. [11]The historian who rediscovered her court records has since argued that Whitfield\'s case reflects a broader, common pattern, women inventors of the era winning legal recognition while still losing lasting historical credit[11]. [12]Whitfield herself continued working as a machinist until her retirement in 1911, filing two additional, less commercially significant patents during that time[12]. [13]Neither of these later patents generated the kind of income or recognition her original bobbin-winding design had[13]. [14]Her granddaughter, interviewed decades later for a local historical society project, recalled Whitfield rarely discussing the patent lawsuit directly, mentioning it mostly through brief, matter-of-fact asides[14]. [15]The historical society\'s project, along with the rediscovered court records, has since prompted renewed interest in properly documenting Whitfield\'s actual contribution to an invention that quietly transformed an entire industry[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Despite this legal victory, Whitfield\'s name largely disappeared from subsequent accounts of the bobbin-winding mechanism\'s development, which tended to credit the manufacturers who had licensed and mass-produced her design]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Despite this legal victory, Whitfield\'s name largely disappeared from subsequent accounts of the bobbin-winding mechanisms development, which tended to credit the manufacturers who had licensed and mass-produced her design',
      'Despite this legal victory, Whitfield\'s name largely disappeared from subsequent accounts of the bobbin-winding mechanism\'s development, that tended to credit the manufacturers who had licensed and mass-produced her design',
      'Despite this legal victory, Whitfields name largely disappeared from subsequent accounts of the bobbin-winding mechanism\'s development, which tended to credit the manufacturers who had licensed and mass-produced her design',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "mechanism\'s" correctly shows singular possession, "Whitfield\'s" correctly shows singular possession, and "which" correctly introduces a nonrestrictive clause set off by a comma, appropriately adding extra information about the accounts. The second option omits the necessary apostrophe in "mechanisms," failing to show possession. The third option incorrectly uses "that" instead of "which" for a nonrestrictive clause already set off by a comma; "which" is the standard choice for nonrestrictive clauses in formal written English. The fourth option omits the necessary apostrophe in "Whitfields," failing to show possession of the name. ACT Tip: Nonrestrictive clauses, which add extra, nonessential information and are set off by commas, are conventionally introduced with "which" rather than "that" in formal written English.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-kol-026',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Knowledge of Language',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In 1887, a machinist named Eleanor Whitfield filed a patent for an improved bobbin-winding mechanism that would later become standard equipment in textile mills across New England, though her name would remain largely absent from the histories written about that transformation. [1]Whitfield had worked in a Providence textile mill since the age of sixteen, gradually earning a reputation among floor supervisors for solving mechanical problems that stumped more senior workers[1]. [2]Her bobbin-winding design reduced thread breakage by nearly forty percent, a improvement that mill owners across the region quickly recognized as commercially significant[2]. [3]Within two years of filing her patent, Whitfield had licensed the design to three separate mill equipment manufacturers[3]. [4]A fourth manufacturer, rather than licensing the design legitimately, began producing a nearly identical mechanism without Whitfield\'s permission[4]. [5]Whitfield sued for patent infringement in 1891, a decision that required her to travel to Boston repeatedly over the following two years for court proceedings[5]. [6]The case dragged on considerably longer than Whitfield had anticipated, she later wrote to a cousin that the legal costs nearly exceeded what she had earned from her legitimate licensing agreements[6]. [7]Court records from the case, rediscovered by a historian researching women inventors in the 1990s, reveal that the presiding judge initially expressed skepticism that a woman without formal engineering training could have designed the mechanism herself[7]. [8]Whitfield\'s testimony, preserved in the surviving court transcripts, describes in careful technical detail exactly how she had arrived at her design through years of hands-on observation[8]. [9]She ultimately won her infringement case in 1893, receiving a settlement that, while smaller than she had hoped, still validated her original claim[9]. [10]Despite this legal victory, Whitfield\'s name largely disappeared from subsequent accounts of the bobbin-winding mechanism\'s development, which tended to credit the manufacturers who had licensed and mass-produced her design[10]. [11]The historian who rediscovered her court records has since argued that Whitfield\'s case reflects a broader, common pattern, women inventors of the era winning legal recognition while still losing lasting historical credit[11]. [12]Whitfield herself continued working as a machinist until her retirement in 1911, filing two additional, less commercially significant patents during that time[12]. [13]Neither of these later patents generated the kind of income or recognition her original bobbin-winding design had[13]. [14]Her granddaughter, interviewed decades later for a local historical society project, recalled Whitfield rarely discussing the patent lawsuit directly, mentioning it mostly through brief, matter-of-fact asides[14]. [15]The historical society\'s project, along with the rediscovered court records, has since prompted renewed interest in properly documenting Whitfield\'s actual contribution to an invention that quietly transformed an entire industry[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: The historian who rediscovered her court records has since argued that Whitfield\'s case reflects a broader, common pattern, women inventors of the era winning legal recognition while still losing lasting historical credit]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'The historian who rediscovered her court records has since argued that Whitfield\'s case reflects a broader, common pattern: women inventors of the era winning legal recognition while still losing lasting historical credit',
      'The historian who rediscovered her court records has since argued that Whitfield\'s case reflects a broader, common pattern; women inventors of the era winning legal recognition while still losing lasting historical credit',
      'The historian who rediscovered her court records has since argued that Whitfield\'s case reflects a broader, common pattern women inventors of the era winning legal recognition while still losing lasting historical credit',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'A colon correctly introduces the specific explanation of what the "broader, common pattern" consists of, connecting the general claim to its specific description. NO CHANGE creates a comma splice-like fusion, using only a comma where a colon is needed to introduce the explanatory phrase. Using a semicolon, as in the third option, is incorrect because "women inventors of the era winning legal recognition while still losing lasting historical credit" is not an independent clause. Removing all punctuation, as in the fourth option, creates a run-on sentence, fusing the general claim and its explanation without any separating punctuation. ACT Tip: A colon is often the most precise punctuation choice when a general claim, such as a "pattern" here, is immediately followed by a phrase explaining exactly what that pattern consists of.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-pow-027',
