sappeared from subsequent accounts of the bobbin-winding mechanism\'s development, which tended to credit the manufacturers who had licensed and mass-produced her design[10]. [11]The historian who rediscovered her court records has since argued that Whitfield\'s case reflects a broader, common pattern, women inventors of the era winning legal recognition while still losing lasting historical credit[11]. [12]Whitfield herself continued working as a machinist until her retirement in 1911, filing two additional, less commercially significant patents during that time[12]. [13]Neither of these later patents generated the kind of income or recognition her original bobbin-winding design had[13]. [14]Her granddaughter, interviewed decades later for a local historical society project, recalled Whitfield rarely discussing the patent lawsuit directly, mentioning it mostly through brief, matter-of-fact asides[14]. [15]The historical society\'s project, along with the rediscovered court records, has since prompted renewed interest in properly documenting Whitfield\'s actual contribution to an invention that quietly transformed an entire industry[15].',
