    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "asides" precisely describes brief, incidental remarks made in passing, matching the passage\'s description of Whitfield mentioning the lawsuit only occasionally and without dwelling on it. "Remarks" is a plausible near-synonym but is more general, lacking the specific connotation of a brief, incidental, tangential comment that "asides" carries. "Interjections" typically implies a more abrupt, interrupting type of speech, a less precise fit for calm, matter-of-fact mentions woven into ordinary conversation. "Outbursts" implies a sudden, often emotional eruption of speech, which directly contradicts the passage\'s description of Whitfield\'s mentions as "matter-of-fact," a calm and understated tone. ACT Tip: When a vocabulary-in-context question involves describing how someone speaks, check whether the surrounding description, such as "matter-of-fact" here, rules out near-synonyms with a mismatched emotional connotation.',
