  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-cse-031',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Along a stretch of coastline in the Gulf region, a decade-long mangrove restoration project has slowly transformed what was once a barren stretch of eroding shoreline into a dense, tangled forest of red and black mangroves. [1]Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, believe the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted[1]. [2]The coastline had lost nearly ninety percent of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century, largely due to development, dredging, and repeated storm damage[2]. [3]Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots, monitoring survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort[3]. [4]Early survival rates disappointed the team, fewer than thirty percent of the initial seedlings survived their first year[4]. [5]Coley attributes this early failure primarily to inadequate attention to local tidal patterns, which the team had not accounted for closely enough in selecting initial planting sites[5]. [6]After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to its preferred tidal zone, survival rates climbed to over seventy percent[6]. [7]The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that use the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators[7]. [8]Local shrimp fishers, who initially expressed skepticism about the restoration project, have since reported noticeably improved catches in waters adjacent to the restored areas[8]. [9]Coley\'s team has also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with their extensive root networks[9]. [10]This erosion reduction has proven particularly valuable during recent hurricane seasons, when the restored mangroves appear to have measurably reduced storm surge damage to nearby coastal properties[10]. [11]Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals, however, certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated[11]. [12]Coley remains cautiously optimistic that these bird populations will eventually rebound as the restored forest continues maturing over the coming decade[12]. [13]The project has attracted attention from coastal restoration teams in other regions facing similar erosion and habitat loss challenges[13]. [14]Coley has begun consulting with several of these teams, sharing both the project\'s successes and its early planting failures as practical lessons[14]. [15]He emphasizes that the project\'s early struggles, as much as its eventual successes, offer valuable guidance for anyone attempting similar restoration work elsewhere[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, believe the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, believes the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted',
      'Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, believing the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted',
      'Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, have believed the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The singular subject "Deshawn Coley" requires the singular verb "believes," not the plural "believe." NO CHANGE incorrectly uses a plural verb form. "Believing," in the third option, removes the finite verb, creating a sentence fragment. "Have believed," in the fourth option, incorrectly uses a plural auxiliary verb with the singular subject and unnecessarily shifts into present perfect tense. ACT Tip: A relative clause like "who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages," set off by commas, does not change the number of the subject the main verb must agree with.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-pow-032',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Along a stretch of coastline in the Gulf region, a decade-long mangrove restoration project has slowly transformed what was once a barren stretch of eroding shoreline into a dense, tangled forest of red and black mangroves. [1]Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, believe the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted[1]. [2]The coastline had lost nearly ninety percent of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century, largely due to development, dredging, and repeated storm damage[2]. [3]Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots, monitoring survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort[3]. [4]Early survival rates disappointed the team, fewer than thirty percent of the initial seedlings survived their first year[4]. [5]Coley attributes this early failure primarily to inadequate attention to local tidal patterns, which the team had not accounted for closely enough in selecting initial planting sites[5]. [6]After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to its preferred tidal zone, survival rates climbed to over seventy percent[6]. [7]The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that use the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators[7]. [8]Local shrimp fishers, who initially expressed skepticism about the restoration project, have since reported noticeably improved catches in waters adjacent to the restored areas[8]. [9]Coley\'s team has also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with their extensive root networks[9]. [10]This erosion reduction has proven particularly valuable during recent hurricane seasons, when the restored mangroves appear to have measurably reduced storm surge damage to nearby coastal properties[10]. [11]Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals, however, certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated[11]. [12]Coley remains cautiously optimistic that these bird populations will eventually rebound as the restored forest continues maturing over the coming decade[12]. [13]The project has attracted attention from coastal restoration teams in other regions facing similar erosion and habitat loss challenges[13]. [14]Coley has begun consulting with several of these teams, sharing both the project\'s successes and its early planting failures as practical lessons[14]. [15]He emphasizes that the project\'s early struggles, as much as its eventual successes, offer valuable guidance for anyone attempting similar restoration work elsewhere[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: The coastline had lost nearly ninety percent of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century, largely due to development, dredging, and repeated storm damage]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'The coastline had lost nearly ninety percent of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century',
      'The coastline had lost quite a lot of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century, largely due to development, dredging, and repeated storm damage, unfortunately',
      'The coastline had lost nearly ninety percent of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century, largely due to development, dredging, and repeated storm damage, which was a shame',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the sentence specifies both the precise scale of the loss, nearly ninety percent, and its specific causes, which together establish the scale of the challenge Coley\'s restoration project needed to address. Cutting the causal explanation, as in the second option, removes meaningful context for understanding the restoration\'s difficulty. The third option replaces the precise "nearly ninety percent" with the vague "quite a lot" and adds the unnecessary editorializing word "unfortunately." The fourth option adds the vague, unnecessary closing phrase "which was a shame," restating something the sentence\'s description of loss already conveys. ACT Tip: A specific, quantified detail that establishes the scale of a problem a passage goes on to address should generally be preserved rather than vaguened or cut.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-cse-033',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Along a stretch of coastline in the Gulf region, a decade-long mangrove restoration project has slowly transformed what was once a barren stretch of eroding shoreline into a dense, tangled forest of red and black mangroves. [1]Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, believe the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted[1]. [2]The coastline had lost nearly ninety percent of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century, largely due to development, dredging, and repeated storm damage[2]. [3]Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots, monitoring survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort[3]. [4]Early survival rates disappointed the team, fewer than thirty percent of the initial seedlings survived their first year[4]. [5]Coley attributes this early failure primarily to inadequate attention to local tidal patterns, which the team had not accounted for closely enough in selecting initial planting sites[5]. [6]After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to its preferred tidal zone, survival rates climbed to over seventy percent[6]. [7]The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that use the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators[7]. [8]Local shrimp fishers, who initially expressed skepticism about the restoration project, have since reported noticeably improved catches in waters adjacent to the restored areas[8]. [9]Coley\'s team has also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with their extensive root networks[9]. [10]This erosion reduction has proven particularly valuable during recent hurricane seasons, when the restored mangroves appear to have measurably reduced storm surge damage to nearby coastal properties[10]. [11]Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals, however, certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated[11]. [12]Coley remains cautiously optimistic that these bird populations will eventually rebound as the restored forest continues maturing over the coming decade[12]. [13]The project has attracted attention from coastal restoration teams in other regions facing similar erosion and habitat loss challenges[13]. [14]Coley has begun consulting with several of these teams, sharing both the project\'s successes and its early planting failures as practical lessons[14]. [15]He emphasizes that the project\'s early struggles, as much as its eventual successes, offer valuable guidance for anyone attempting similar restoration work elsewhere[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots, monitoring survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots; monitoring survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort',
      'Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots monitoring survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort',
      'Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots, monitoring, survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the comma correctly sets off the participial phrase "monitoring survival rates...," which describes an action the team undertook alongside the planting. Using a semicolon, as in the second option, is incorrect because the participial phrase is not an independent clause. Removing the comma entirely, as in the third option, fuses the modifying phrase onto the main clause without the needed pause. Adding a comma after "monitoring," as in the fourth option, incorrectly separates the participle from its own object, "survival rates." ACT Tip: A participial phrase describing an accompanying action is typically set off from the main clause by a single comma, not a semicolon.',
  ),
