      'Early survival rates disappointed the team: fewer than thirty percent of the initial seedlings survived their first year',
      'Early survival rates disappointed the team fewer than thirty percent of the initial seedlings survived their first year',
      'Early survival rates disappointed the team, and fewer than thirty percent, of the initial seedlings survived their first year',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'A colon correctly introduces the specific evidence explaining why the survival rates were disappointing, connecting the general claim to the specific statistic that follows. NO CHANGE creates a comma splice by joining two independent clauses with only a comma. Removing all punctuation, as in the third option, creates a run-on sentence. The fourth option fixes the splice with "and" but adds an unnecessary comma after "percent," incorrectly separating the subject phrase from its verb. ACT Tip: A colon is often the most precise punctuation choice when a general claim is immediately followed by the specific data supporting it.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-pow-035',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Along a stretch of coastline in the Gulf region, a decade-long mangrove restoration project has slowly transformed what was once a barren stretch of eroding shoreline into a dense, tangled forest of red and black mangroves. [1]Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, believe the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted[1]. [2]The coastline had lost nearly ninety percent of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century, largely due to development, dredging, and repeated storm damage[2]. [3]Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots, monitoring survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort[3]. [4]Early survival rates disappointed the team, fewer than thirty percent of the initial seedlings survived their first year[4]. [5]Coley attributes this early failure primarily to inadequate attention to local tidal patterns, which the team had not accounted for closely enough in selecting initial planting sites[5]. [6]After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to its preferred tidal zone, survival rates climbed to over seventy percent[6]. [7]The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that use the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators[7]. [8]Local shrimp fishers, who initially expressed skepticism about the restoration project, have since reported noticeably improved catches in waters adjacent to the restored areas[8]. [9]Coley\'s team has also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with their extensive root networks[9]. [10]This erosion reduction has proven particularly valuable during recent hurricane seasons, when the restored mangroves appear to have