    questionText: '[underlined: Coley attributes this early failure primarily to inadequate attention to local tidal patterns, which the team had not accounted for closely enough in selecting initial planting sites]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Coley attributes this early failure primarily to inadequate attention to local tidal patterns',
      'Coley attributes this early failure to a bunch of different factors, primarily inadequate attention to local tidal patterns, which the team had not accounted for closely enough in selecting initial planting sites',
      'This early failure is attributed by Coley primarily to inadequate attention to local tidal patterns, which the team had not accounted for closely enough in selecting initial planting sites',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the full sentence both identifies the specific cause of the early failure and explains precisely what went wrong in the site-selection process, directly setting up the following sentence\'s description of how the team corrected this specific problem. Cutting the explanatory clause, as in the second option, removes the specific detail about tidal patterns not being accounted for, weakening the connection to the following sentence\'s solution. The third option adds the vague, unnecessary filler phrase "a bunch of different factors," which contradicts the sentence\'s own focus on a single primary cause. The fourth option shifts to a less direct, weaker passive construction, "is attributed by Coley," compared to the more direct and effective active voice of the original. ACT Tip: When a sentence\'s specific explanation directly sets up the solution described in a following sentence, cutting that explanation typically weakens the passage\'s logical connection between cause and resolution.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-cse-036',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Along a stretch of coastline in the Gulf region, a decade-long mangrove restoration project has slowly transformed what was once a barren stretch of eroding shoreline into a dense, tangled forest of red and black mangroves. [1]Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, believe the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted[1]. [2]The coastline had lost nearly ninety percent of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century, largely due to development, dredging, and repeated storm damage[2]. [3]Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots, monitoring survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort[3]. [4]Early survival rates disappointed the team, fewer than thirty percent of the initial seedlings survived their first year[4]. [5]Coley attributes this early failure primarily to inadequate attention to local tidal patterns, which the team had not accounted for closely enough in selecting initial planting sites[5]. [6]After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to its preferred tidal zone, survival rates climbed to over seventy percent[6]. [7]The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that use the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators[7]. [8]Local shrimp fishers, who initially expressed skepticism about the restoration project, have since reported noticeably improved catches in waters adjacent to the restored areas[8]. [9]Coley\'s team has also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with their extensive root networks[9]. [10]This erosion reduction has proven particularly valuable during recent hurricane seasons, when the restored mangroves appear to have measurably reduced storm surge damage to nearby coastal properties[10]. [11]Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals, however, certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated[11]. [12]Coley remains cautiously optimistic that these bird populations will eventually rebound as the restored forest continues maturing over the coming decade[12]. [13]The project has attracted attention from coastal restoration teams in other regions facing similar erosion and habitat loss challenges[13]. [14]Coley has begun consulting with several of these teams, sharing both the project\'s successes and its early planting failures as practical lessons[14]. [15]He emphasizes that the project\'s early struggles, as much as its eventual successes, offer valuable guidance for anyone attempting similar restoration work elsewhere[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to its preferred tidal zone, survival rates climbed to over seventy percent]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to its preferred tidal zone survival rates climbed to over seventy percent',
      'After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to its preferred tidal zone, survival rates climbing to over seventy percent',
      'After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to it\'s preferred tidal zone, survival rates climbed to over seventy percent',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the comma after the introductory participial phrase "After adjusting...tidal zone" correctly separates it from the main clause, and "its" correctly functions as the possessive pronoun referring to each seedling species. Removing the comma, as in the second option, fuses the introductory phrase onto the main clause without the needed pause. "Climbing," in the third option, removes the finite verb, creating a sentence fragment. "It\'s," in the fourth option, incorrectly uses the contraction meaning "it is" where the possessive "its" is required. ACT Tip: An introductory phrase describing an action taken before the main clause\'s result is typically set off by a comma, and possessive "its" never takes an apostrophe.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-kol-037',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Knowledge of Language',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Along a stretch of coastline in the Gulf region, a decade-long mangrove restoration project has slowly transformed what was once a barren stretch of eroding shoreline into a dense, tangled forest of red and black mangroves. [1]Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, believe the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted[1]. [2]The coastline had lost nearly ninety percent of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century, largely due to development, dredging, and repeated storm damage[2]. [3]Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots, monitoring survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort[3]. [4]Early survival rates disappointed the team, fewer than thirty percent of the initial seedlings survived their first year[4]. [5]Coley attributes this early failure primarily to inadequate attention to local tidal patterns, which the team had not accounted for closely enough in selecting initial planting sites[5]. [6]After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to its preferred tidal zone, survival rates climbed to over seventy percent[6]. [7]The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that use the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators[7]. [8]Local shrimp fishers, who initially expressed skepticism about the restoration project, have since reported noticeably improved catches in waters adjacent to the restored areas[8]. [9]Coley\'s team has also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with their extensive root networks[9]. [10]This erosion reduction has proven particularly valuable during recent hurricane seasons, when the restored mangroves appear to have measurably reduced storm surge damage to nearby coastal properties[10]. [11]Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals, however, certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated[11]. [12]Coley remains cautiously optimistic that these bird populations will eventually rebound as the restored forest continues maturing over the coming decade[12]. [13]The project has attracted attention from coastal restoration teams in other regions facing similar erosion and habitat loss challenges[13]. [14]Coley has begun consulting with several of these teams, sharing both the project\'s successes and its early planting failures as practical lessons[14]. [15]He emphasizes that the project\'s early struggles, as much as its eventual successes, offer valuable guidance for anyone attempting similar restoration work elsewhere[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that use the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that utilize the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators',
      'The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that employ the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators',
      'The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that exploit the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "use" is the plain, direct, and most natural word for describing how fish and shellfish rely on the root systems for shelter, matching the passage\'s accessible, clear tone. "Utilize" is a more formal, often unnecessarily inflated synonym for "use" that the ACT typically treats as needlessly wordy when a simpler word conveys the same meaning. "Employ," in this context describing animal behavior rather than deliberate human strategy, is an awkward and less natural fit than the direct "use." "Exploit" carries a negative connotation of taking unfair advantage, an inappropriate and inaccurate fit for describing a natural, mutually beneficial ecological relationship. ACT Tip: When a simpler, more direct word conveys the same meaning as a more formal or unusual synonym, the ACT typically favors the simpler, more natural word choice.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
