    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Along a stretch of coastline in the Gulf region, a decade-long mangrove restoration project has slowly transformed what was once a barren stretch of eroding shoreline into a dense, tangled forest of red and black mangroves. [1]Environmental scientist Deshawn Coley, who has overseen the project since its earliest planning stages, believe the restoration offers a rare example of ecological recovery unfolding faster than initial models predicted[1]. [2]The coastline had lost nearly ninety percent of its original mangrove cover over the preceding half-century, largely due to development, dredging, and repeated storm damage[2]. [3]Coley\'s team began by planting mangrove seedlings in carefully selected test plots, monitoring survival rates before committing to a larger-scale planting effort[3]. [4]Early survival rates disappointed the team, fewer than thirty percent of the initial seedlings survived their first year[4]. [5]Coley attributes this early failure primarily to inadequate attention to local tidal patterns, which the team had not accounted for closely enough in selecting initial planting sites[5]. [6]After adjusting their site-selection criteria to better match each seedling species to its preferred tidal zone, survival rates climbed to over seventy percent[6]. [7]The restored mangroves now provide nursery habitat for numerous commercially important fish and shellfish species that use the tangled root systems as shelter from larger predators[7]. [8]Local shrimp fishers, who initially expressed skepticism about the restoration project, have since reported noticeably improved catches in waters adjacent to the restored areas[8]. [9]Coley\'s team has also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with their extensive root networks[9]. [10]This erosion reduction has proven particularly valuable during recent hurricane seasons, when the restored mangroves appear to have measurably reduced storm surge damage to nearby coastal properties[10]. [11]Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals, however, certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated[11]. [12]Coley remains cautiously optimistic that these bird populations will eventually rebound as the restored forest continues maturing over the coming decade[12]. [13]The project has attracted attention from coastal restoration teams in other regions facing similar erosion and habitat loss challenges[13]. [14]Coley has begun consulting with several of these teams, sharing both the project\'s successes and its early planting failures as practical lessons[14]. [15]He emphasizes that the project\'s early struggles, as much as its eventual successes, offer valuable guidance for anyone attempting similar restoration work elsewhere[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Coley\'s team has also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with their extensive root networks]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Coley\'s team have also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with their extensive root networks',
      'Coley\'s team has also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with it\'s extensive root networks',
      'Coley\'s team has also documented a measurable reduction in shoreline erosion rates since the mangroves matured sufficiently to stabilize sediment with there extensive root networks',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the collective noun "team," treated as singular here, correctly takes the singular verb "has," and "their" correctly functions as the possessive pronoun referring to the plural mangroves and their collective root networks. "Have," in the second option, incorrectly pairs a plural verb with the singular collective noun "team" as used consistently elsewhere in the passage. "It\'s," in the third option, incorrectly uses the contraction meaning "it is" where a possessive pronoun referring to the plural mangroves is required. "There," in the fourth option, incorrectly uses the locational adverb where the possessive pronoun "their" is required. ACT Tip: Distinguish carefully among "their" (possessive), "there" (location), and "they\'re" (contraction of "they are"), since these homophones are frequently confused but serve entirely different grammatical functions.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
