 measurably reduced storm surge damage to nearby coastal properties[10]. [11]Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals, however, certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated[11]. [12]Coley remains cautiously optimistic that these bird populations will eventually rebound as the restored forest continues maturing over the coming decade[12]. [13]The project has attracted attention from coastal restoration teams in other regions facing similar erosion and habitat loss challenges[13]. [14]Coley has begun consulting with several of these teams, sharing both the project\'s successes and its early planting failures as practical lessons[14]. [15]He emphasizes that the project\'s early struggles, as much as its eventual successes, offer valuable guidance for anyone attempting similar restoration work elsewhere[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals, however, certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals; however, certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated',
      'Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals however certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated',
      'Not every outcome has matched the project\'s original goals, however certain bird species researchers hoped would return to the area have been slower to reestablish breeding populations than anticipated',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'A semicolon before the conjunctive adverb "however" and a comma after it correctly join the two independent clauses. NO CHANGE creates a comma splice by using only a comma before "however." Removing all punctuation, as in the third option, creates a run-on sentence. Keeping only a comma before "however" without the semicolon, as in the fourth option, repeats the same comma splice error while also failing to set off "however" with a following comma. ACT Tip: Conjunctive adverbs like "however," "therefore," and "nevertheless" require a semicolon before them and a comma after when joining two independent clauses.',
