  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-cse-046',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Five years ago, a regional symphony orchestra began sending small ensembles of musicians into state correctional facilities to perform and, eventually, to teach basic music instruction to incarcerated participants who volunteered for the program. [1]Conductor Yolanda Briggs, who proposed the program to the orchestra\'s board, have described it as one of the most demanding and rewarding projects of her career[1]. [2]Early performances inside the facilities were met with a mixture of curiosity and open skepticism from both incarcerated participants and correctional staff[2]. [3]Briggs recalls one early performance where several attendees sat with arms crossed throughout the entire first piece, visibly unconvinced this program would offer anything meaningful[3]. [4]By the second and third pieces, however, the mood in the room shifted noticeably, applause growing louder and questions afterward growing more genuinely curious[4]. [5]Within a year, the program expanded beyond simple performances to include actual instruction, teaching participants to play donated instruments themselves[5]. [6]Correctional staff initially raised understandable security concerns about bringing musical instruments, some of which include small metal parts, into facilities[6]. [7]Briggs worked closely with facility administrators to develop instrument-tracking protocols that satisfied security requirements without unduly restricting the instruction itself[7]. [8]Participants in the instruction program now range from complete beginners to individuals who had played instruments seriously before their incarceration[8]. [9]One participant, who had played trumpet in his high school band years before entering the correctional system, described picking up an instrument again as the first time in years he had felt genuinely like himself[9]. [10]Briggs is careful not to overstate the program\'s effects, she avoids claiming the program directly reduces recidivism, since rigorous long-term outcome data remains limited[10]. [11]What she will say confidently is that participants consistently report the program provides a rare sense of structure, accomplishment, and creative expression within an environment that often offers little of any of these things[11]. [12]The orchestra now runs the program in three separate facilities, with a growing waitlist of participants hoping to join at each location[12]. [13]Funding for the program comes primarily from private donors specifically interested in arts-based rehabilitation initiatives, rather than from the orchestra\'s general operating budget[13]. [14]Briggs hopes to eventually expand the program to additional facilities, assuming continued donor support makes such expansion financially feasible[14]. [15]For now, she says, the program\'s modest scale allows for the kind of close, individualized instruction that a larger, more rushed expansion might inevitably sacrifice[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Conductor Yolanda Briggs, who proposed the program to the orchestra\'s board, have described it as one of the most demanding and rewarding projects of her career]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Conductor Yolanda Briggs, who proposed the program to the orchestra\'s board, has described it as one of the most demanding and rewarding projects of her career',
      'Conductor Yolanda Briggs, who proposed the program to the orchestra\'s board, describing it as one of the most demanding and rewarding projects of her career',
      'Conductor Yolanda Briggs, who proposed the program to the orchestra\'s board, having described it as one of the most demanding and rewarding projects of her career',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The singular subject "Yolanda Briggs" requires the singular verb "has," not the plural "have." NO CHANGE incorrectly uses a plural verb form. "Describing," in the third option, removes the finite verb, creating a sentence fragment. "Having described," in the fourth option, similarly removes the finite verb needed to complete the sentence, creating a fragment. ACT Tip: A relative clause like "who proposed the program to the orchestra\'s board," set off by commas, does not change the number of the subject the main verb must agree with.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
