      'Briggs recalls one early performance where several attendees sat with arms crossed throughout the entire first piece visibly unconvinced this program would offer anything meaningful',
      'Briggs recalls one early performance where several attendees sat with arms crossed throughout the entire first piece, visibly unconvinced, this program would offer anything meaningful',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the comma correctly sets off the participial phrase "visibly unconvinced this program would offer anything meaningful," which describes the attendees\' apparent state of mind. Using a semicolon, as in the second option, is incorrect because the participial phrase is not an independent clause. Removing the comma entirely, as in the third option, fuses the modifying phrase onto the main clause without the needed pause. Adding a comma after "unconvinced," as in the fourth option, incorrectly separates the adjective from the clause it needs to be grammatically complete. ACT Tip: A participial or adjectival phrase describing a subject\'s apparent state or attitude is typically set off from the main clause by a single comma.',
