  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-cse-061',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'For the past four years, sports scientist Dr. Felix Marchetti has used underwater motion-capture cameras to study exactly what separates elite competitive swimmers from merely very good ones. [1]Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, record swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle[1]. [2]Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors[2]. [3]The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap[3]. [4]One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water[4]. [5]Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface[5]. [6]Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began[6]. [7]Marchetti\'s team has also studied body rotation, the degree to which a swimmer\'s hips and shoulders rotate together during freestyle stroke cycles[7]. [8]Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can waste energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles[8]. [9]Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style[9]. [10]This individual variation has complicated efforts to develop a single, universal technique template applicable to every swimmer regardless of body type[10]. [11]Instead, Marchetti\'s team increasingly works with individual swimmers and their coaches, identifying each swimmer\'s specific technical inefficiencies through personalized motion-capture analysis[11]. [12]One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations[12]. [13]Marchetti cautions that such dramatic individual improvements are not guaranteed for every swimmer who undergoes similar analysis[13]. [14]He emphasizes that technique refinement works alongside, rather than as a replacement for, the physical conditioning that remains fundamental to elite competitive performance[14]. [15]Marchetti hopes his lab\'s ongoing research will eventually help coaches identify promising technical adjustments earlier in a swimmer\'s development, well before less efficient movement patterns become deeply ingrained habits[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, record swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, records swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle',
      'Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, recording swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle',
      'Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, have recorded swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The singular subject "lab" requires the singular verb "records," not the plural "record." NO CHANGE incorrectly uses a plural verb form. "Recording," in the third option, removes the finite verb, creating a sentence fragment. "Have recorded," in the fourth option, incorrectly uses a plural auxiliary verb with the singular subject and unnecessarily shifts into present perfect tense. ACT Tip: A parenthetical phrase describing the subject, such as "housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool," does not change whether that subject is singular or plural for verb agreement.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-pow-062',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past four years, sports scientist Dr. Felix Marchetti has used underwater motion-capture cameras to study exactly what separates elite competitive swimmers from merely very good ones. [1]Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, record swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle[1]. [2]Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors[2]. [3]The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap[3]. [4]One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water[4]. [5]Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface[5]. [6]Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began[6]. [7]Marchetti\'s team has also studied body rotation, the degree to which a swimmer\'s hips and shoulders rotate together during freestyle stroke cycles[7]. [8]Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can waste energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles[8]. [9]Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style[9]. [10]This individual variation has complicated efforts to develop a single, universal technique template applicable to every swimmer regardless of body type[10]. [11]Instead, Marchetti\'s team increasingly works with individual swimmers and their coaches, identifying each swimmer\'s specific technical inefficiencies through personalized motion-capture analysis[11]. [12]One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations[12]. [13]Marchetti cautions that such dramatic individual improvements are not guaranteed for every swimmer who undergoes similar analysis[13]. [14]He emphasizes that technique refinement works alongside, rather than as a replacement for, the physical conditioning that remains fundamental to elite competitive performance[14]. [15]Marchetti hopes his lab\'s ongoing research will eventually help coaches identify promising technical adjustments earlier in a swimmer\'s development, well before less efficient movement patterns become deeply ingrained habits[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors',
      'Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors, which turned out to be wrong',
      'Early on, Marchetti had some expectations about physical power being important, generally speaking',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the sentence establishes Marchetti\'s initial expectation, which the following sentence directly complicates by revealing that "the motion-capture data told a more complicated story," creating an effective setup-and-reveal structure. Cutting "Early in his research," as in the second option, loses the meaningful timeframe context that clarifies this was an initial, since-revised expectation. The third option adds the vague, unnecessary closing phrase "which turned out to be wrong," prematurely stating the following sentence\'s point and reducing that sentence\'s own rhetorical effectiveness. The fourth option is vague and imprecise, replacing the specific expectation with the generic "had some expectations...generally speaking," losing the sentence\'s precise claim. ACT Tip: When a sentence sets up an expectation that a following sentence directly complicates or reveals to be wrong, cutting or prematurely revealing that setup typically weakens the passage\'s rhetorical structure.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
