    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past four years, sports scientist Dr. Felix Marchetti has used underwater motion-capture cameras to study exactly what separates elite competitive swimmers from merely very good ones. [1]Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, record swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle[1]. [2]Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors[2]. [3]The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap[3]. [4]One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water[4]. [5]Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface[5]. [6]Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began[6]. [7]Marchetti\'s team has also studied body rotation, the degree to which a swimmer\'s hips and shoulders rotate together during freestyle stroke cycles[7]. [8]Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can waste energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles[8]. [9]Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style[9]. [10]This individual variation has complicated efforts to develop a single, universal technique template applicable to every swimmer regardless of body type[10]. [11]Instead, Marchetti\'s team increasingly works with individual swimmers and their coaches, identifying each swimmer\'s specific technical inefficiencies through personalized motion-capture analysis[11]. [12]One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations[12]. [13]Marchetti cautions that such dramatic individual improvements are not guaranteed for every swimmer who undergoes similar analysis[13]. [14]He emphasizes that technique refinement works alongside, rather than as a replacement for, the physical conditioning that remains fundamental to elite competitive performance[14]. [15]Marchetti hopes his lab\'s ongoing research will eventually help coaches identify promising technical adjustments earlier in a swimmer\'s development, well before less efficient movement patterns become deeply ingrained habits[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'The motion-capture data told a more complicated story: technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap',
      'The motion-capture data told a more complicated story technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap',
      'The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, and technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted, for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'A colon correctly introduces the specific explanation of what the "more complicated story" actually consisted of, connecting the general claim to the specific finding that follows. NO CHANGE creates a comma splice by joining two independent clauses with only a comma. Removing all punctuation, as in the third option, creates a run-on sentence. The fourth option fixes the splice with "and" but adds an unnecessary comma after "accounted," incorrectly separating the verb from the prepositional phrase it needs to be grammatically complete. ACT Tip: A colon is often the most precise punctuation choice when a general claim, such as a "story" here, is immediately followed by the specific explanation of what that story consists of.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-kol-064',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Knowledge of Language',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'For the past four years, sports scientist Dr. Felix Marchetti has used underwater motion-capture cameras to study exactly what separates elite competitive swimmers from merely very good ones. [1]Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, record swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle[1]. [2]Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors[2]. [3]The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap[3]. [4]One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water[4]. [5]Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface[5]. [6]Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began[6]. [7]Marchetti\'s team has also studied body rotation, the degree to which a swimmer\'s hips and shoulders rotate together during freestyle stroke cycles[7]. [8]Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can waste energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles[8]. [9]Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style[9]. [10]This individual variation has complicated efforts to develop a single, universal technique template applicable to every swimmer regardless of body type[10]. [11]Instead, Marchetti\'s team increasingly works with individual swimmers and their coaches, identifying each swimmer\'s specific technical inefficiencies through personalized motion-capture analysis[11]. [12]One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations[12]. [13]Marchetti cautions that such dramatic individual improvements are not guaranteed for every swimmer who undergoes similar analysis[13]. [14]He emphasizes that technique refinement works alongside, rather than as a replacement for, the physical conditioning that remains fundamental to elite competitive performance[14]. [15]Marchetti hopes his lab\'s ongoing research will eventually help coaches identify promising technical adjustments earlier in a swimmer\'s development, well before less efficient movement patterns become deeply ingrained habits[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke, when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water',
      'One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water',
      'One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase; the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the single comma correctly sets off the appositive phrase "the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water," which renames and defines the "catch" phase. Adding a second comma after "stroke," as in the second option, incorrectly splits the appositive phrase, separating its own necessary "when" clause from the noun it modifies. Removing the comma entirely, as in the third option, fuses the appositive onto the sentence without the needed pause. Using a semicolon, as in the fourth option, is incorrect because the appositive phrase is not an independent clause. ACT Tip: An appositive phrase that renames and defines a preceding term should be set off by a single comma, with no additional internal comma splitting the appositive\'s own necessary modifying clause.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-pow-065',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past four years, sports scientist Dr. Felix Marchetti has used underwater motion-capture cameras to study exactly what separates elite competitive swimmers from merely very good ones. [1]Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, record swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle[1]. [2]Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors[2]. [3]The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap[3]. [4]One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water[4]. [5]Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface[5]. [6]Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began[6]. [7]Marchetti\'s team has also studied body rotation, the degree to which a swimmer\'s hips and shoulders rotate together during freestyle stroke cycles[7]. [8]Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can waste energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles[8]. [9]Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style[9]. [10]This individual variation has complicated efforts to develop a single, universal technique template applicable to every swimmer regardless of body type[10]. [11]Instead, Marchetti\'s team increasingly works with individual swimmers and their coaches, identifying each swimmer\'s specific technical inefficiencies through personalized motion-capture analysis[11]. [12]One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations[12]. [13]Marchetti cautions that such dramatic individual improvements are not guaranteed for every swimmer who undergoes similar analysis[13]. [14]He emphasizes that technique refinement works alongside, rather than as a replacement for, the physical conditioning that remains fundamental to elite competitive performance[14]. [15]Marchetti hopes his lab\'s ongoing research will eventually help coaches identify promising technical adjustments earlier in a swimmer\'s development, well before less efficient movement patterns become deeply ingrained habits[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase',
      'Elite swimmers positioned their hand and forearm in certain ways to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface, apparently',
      'Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface, interestingly enough',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the closing clause explains the technical mechanism behind the positioning, using the forearm as a propulsive surface extension, which directly supports the following sentence\'s contrasting description of slower swimmers wasting this same propulsive potential. Cutting this explanatory clause, as in the second option, removes the technical mechanism the following sentence\'s contrast directly depends on. The third option adds the vague filler "in certain ways" and the unnecessary hedging word "apparently," which undermines the sentence\'s otherwise confident, specific technical claim. The fourth option adds the unnecessary editorializing phrase "interestingly enough," which disrupts the passage\'s measured, technical tone. ACT Tip: When a sentence\'s technical explanation directly sets up a following sentence\'s contrast or comparison, cutting that explanation typically weakens the passage\'s logical connection between the two.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-cse-066',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Conventions of Standard English',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past four years, sports scientist Dr. Felix Marchetti has used underwater motion-capture cameras to study exactly what separates elite competitive swimmers from merely very good ones. [1]Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, record swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle[1]. [2]Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors[2]. [3]The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap[3]. [4]One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water[4]. [5]Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface[5]. [6]Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began[6]. [7]Marchetti\'s team has also studied body rotation, the degree to which a swimmer\'s hips and shoulders rotate together during freestyle stroke cycles[7]. [8]Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can waste energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles[8]. [9]Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style[9]. [10]This individual variation has complicated efforts to develop a single, universal technique template applicable to every swimmer regardless of body type[10]. [11]Instead, Marchetti\'s team increasingly works with individual swimmers and their coaches, identifying each swimmer\'s specific technical inefficiencies through personalized motion-capture analysis[11]. [12]One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations[12]. [13]Marchetti cautions that such dramatic individual improvements are not guaranteed for every swimmer who undergoes similar analysis[13]. [14]He emphasizes that technique refinement works alongside, rather than as a replacement for, the physical conditioning that remains fundamental to elite competitive performance[14]. [15]Marchetti hopes his lab\'s ongoing research will eventually help coaches identify promising technical adjustments earlier in a swimmer\'s development, well before less efficient movement patterns become deeply ingrained habits[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly begins',
      'Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often lets their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began',
      'Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slicing through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the plural subject "swimmers" correctly takes the plural verb "let," and the past tense "began" correctly matches the passage\'s consistent past-tense description of Marchetti\'s research findings. "Begins," in the second option, incorrectly shifts to present tense, inconsistent with the surrounding past-tense narration of the research findings. "Lets," in the third option, incorrectly uses a singular verb form for the plural subject "swimmers." "Slicing," in the fourth option, is ungrammatical after "let," which requires the base form of the verb, "slice," not the "-ing" form. ACT Tip: The verb "let" is followed by the base form of the following verb, not the "-ing" form, as in "let their hand slice," not "let their hand slicing."',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-kol-067',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Knowledge of Language',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past four years, sports scientist Dr. Felix Marchetti has used underwater motion-capture cameras to study exactly what separates elite competitive swimmers from merely very good ones. [1]Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, record swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle[1]. [2]Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors[2]. [3]The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap[3]. [4]One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water[4]. [5]Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface[5]. [6]Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began[6]. [7]Marchetti\'s team has also studied body rotation, the degree to which a swimmer\'s hips and shoulders rotate together during freestyle stroke cycles[7]. [8]Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can waste energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles[8]. [9]Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style[9]. [10]This individual variation has complicated efforts to develop a single, universal technique template applicable to every swimmer regardless of body type[10]. [11]Instead, Marchetti\'s team increasingly works with individual swimmers and their coaches, identifying each swimmer\'s specific technical inefficiencies through personalized motion-capture analysis[11]. [12]One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations[12]. [13]Marchetti cautions that such dramatic individual improvements are not guaranteed for every swimmer who undergoes similar analysis[13]. [14]He emphasizes that technique refinement works alongside, rather than as a replacement for, the physical conditioning that remains fundamental to elite competitive performance[14]. [15]Marchetti hopes his lab\'s ongoing research will eventually help coaches identify promising technical adjustments earlier in a swimmer\'s development, well before less efficient movement patterns become deeply ingrained habits[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can waste energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can squander energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles',
      'Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can spend energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles',
      'Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can consume energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because "waste" is the most natural, precise word for describing energy used inefficiently without proportional benefit, matching the passage\'s clear, accessible tone. "Squander" is a plausible near-synonym but carries a stronger connotation of careless or reckless loss, a slightly more dramatic fit than the passage\'s measured, technical description warrants. "Spend" is too neutral and does not capture the specific sense of inefficient or unproductive use that "waste" conveys; energy can be "spent" productively as well as unproductively. "Consume" similarly is too neutral, describing energy use generally without capturing the specific inefficiency or lack of proportional benefit that "waste" precisely conveys. ACT Tip: When a sentence describes an outcome as specifically inefficient or disproportionate to its cost, favor a near-synonym like "waste" that captures this specific inefficiency, rather than a more neutral word describing use in general.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-pow-068',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past four years, sports scientist Dr. Felix Marchetti has used underwater motion-capture cameras to study exactly what separates elite competitive swimmers from merely very good ones. [1]Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, record swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle[1]. [2]Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors[2]. [3]The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap[3]. [4]One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water[4]. [5]Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface[5]. [6]Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began[6]. [7]Marchetti\'s team has also studied body rotation, the degree to which a swimmer\'s hips and shoulders rotate together during freestyle stroke cycles[7]. [8]Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can waste energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles[8]. [9]Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style[9]. [10]This individual variation has complicated efforts to develop a single, universal technique template applicable to every swimmer regardless of body type[10]. [11]Instead, Marchetti\'s team increasingly works with individual swimmers and their coaches, identifying each swimmer\'s specific technical inefficiencies through personalized motion-capture analysis[11]. [12]One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations[12]. [13]Marchetti cautions that such dramatic individual improvements are not guaranteed for every swimmer who undergoes similar analysis[13]. [14]He emphasizes that technique refinement works alongside, rather than as a replacement for, the physical conditioning that remains fundamental to elite competitive performance[14]. [15]Marchetti hopes his lab\'s ongoing research will eventually help coaches identify promising technical adjustments earlier in a swimmer\'s development, well before less efficient movement patterns become deeply ingrained habits[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'Finding this optimal rotation range appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style',
      'This optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style',
      'Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style, apparently',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'Removing "Finding" corrects a logical error in the original sentence: it is the optimal rotation range itself, not the act of finding it, that varies by body type and stroke style, so the subject should be "This optimal rotation range," not "Finding this optimal rotation range." NO CHANGE illogically states that the act of finding something varies by body type, when the passage clearly means the range itself varies. The second option removes the necessary attribution "Marchetti notes," losing the direct connection to Marchetti\'s own research conclusions established throughout the passage. The fourth option retains the original logical error while also adding the unnecessary, redundant word "apparently" alongside "appears," which already conveys a similar sense of tentative observation. ACT Tip: Check that a sentence\'s subject logically matches what its verb describes; a subject naming an action, like "finding," cannot logically be the thing that itself "varies" if the intended meaning is that a separate underlying quantity varies.',
  ),
