  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-english-pow-071',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.english,
    skillArea: 'Production of Writing',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'For the past four years, sports scientist Dr. Felix Marchetti has used underwater motion-capture cameras to study exactly what separates elite competitive swimmers from merely very good ones. [1]Marchetti\'s lab, housed at a university with an Olympic-sized research pool, record swimmers from multiple angles simultaneously, tracking dozens of specific body positions throughout each stroke cycle[1]. [2]Early in his research, Marchetti expected that raw physical power would prove to be the dominant factor separating elite swimmers from their slightly slower competitors[2]. [3]The motion-capture data told a more complicated story, technique differences, often measured in fractions of a second per stroke phase, accounted for a surprisingly large share of the performance gap[3]. [4]One particularly consistent finding involved what Marchetti calls the "catch" phase, the brief moment early in each arm stroke when the swimmer\'s hand first begins pulling against the water[4]. [5]Elite swimmers consistently positioned their hand and forearm to maximize water resistance during this catch phase, effectively using their forearm as an extension of their propulsive surface[5]. [6]Slower swimmers, by contrast, more often let their hand slice through the water at the start of the stroke, wasting valuable propulsive potential before the pull even truly began[6]. [7]Marchetti\'s team has also studied body rotation, the degree to which a swimmer\'s hips and shoulders rotate together during freestyle stroke cycles[7]. [8]Excessive rotation, the data suggests, can waste energy without providing proportional propulsive benefit, while insufficient rotation limits how effectively a swimmer can engage their strongest muscles[8]. [9]Finding this optimal rotation range, Marchetti notes, appears to vary somewhat by individual body type and stroke style[9]. [10]This individual variation has complicated efforts to develop a single, universal technique template applicable to every swimmer regardless of body type[10]. [11]Instead, Marchetti\'s team increasingly works with individual swimmers and their coaches, identifying each swimmer\'s specific technical inefficiencies through personalized motion-capture analysis[11]. [12]One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations[12]. [13]Marchetti cautions that such dramatic individual improvements are not guaranteed for every swimmer who undergoes similar analysis[13]. [14]He emphasizes that technique refinement works alongside, rather than as a replacement for, the physical conditioning that remains fundamental to elite competitive performance[14]. [15]Marchetti hopes his lab\'s ongoing research will eventually help coaches identify promising technical adjustments earlier in a swimmer\'s development, well before less efficient movement patterns become deeply ingrained habits[15].',
    questionText: '[underlined: One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations]',
    options: [
      'NO CHANGE',
      'One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time',
      'A swimmer worked with the lab and had good results, reportedly, after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations',
      'One competitive swimmer who worked with Marchetti\'s lab reported shaving nearly half a second off her hundred-meter freestyle time after adjusting her catch-phase hand positioning based on the lab\'s specific recommendations, which is impressive',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'NO CHANGE is correct because the full sentence connects the specific measured improvement directly to the specific technical adjustment, catch-phase hand positioning, tying this concrete example back to the passage\'s earlier detailed discussion of the catch phase\'s importance. Cutting the causal explanation, as in the second option, loses this direct connection to the passage\'s earlier technical discussion. The third option is vague and imprecise, replacing the specific measured result with the generic "had good results, reportedly," losing the concrete, persuasive detail. The fourth option adds the vague, unnecessary closing phrase "which is impressive," restating an unsupported editorial judgment not needed given the sentence\'s already concrete, self-evidently impressive detail. ACT Tip: When a specific example directly echoes and confirms an earlier technical point in the passage, preserving that specific connection typically strengthens the passage\'s persuasive and logical coherence.',
