    questionText: 'According to the passage, what is meant by the term "intervention saturation"?',
    options: [
      'A phenomenon in which a technique\'s effectiveness gradually diminishes as its novelty and rarity decline over time',
      'A phenomenon in which voters become physically unable to receive any further campaign contact',
      'A statistical method used to verify voter turnout through public records',
      'A term describing the total number of voters contacted by a single campaign in one election cycle',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The passage directly defines the term: "a phenomenon researchers sometimes call intervention saturation, in which a technique\'s effectiveness gradually diminishes as its novelty and rarity decline." The passage does not describe any physical limitation on voters receiving contact; the term concerns psychological or behavioral responsiveness declining, not a physical capacity limit. Verification of turnout through public records is described earlier in the passage as a separate methodological detail, unrelated to the definition of "intervention saturation." The passage does not define this term as a measure of total voters contacted; it specifically describes a decline in a technique\'s effectiveness over time, not a raw contact count. ACT Tip: When a passage introduces a specialized term, locate the sentence that explicitly defines it rather than guessing based on the term\'s individual words or a superficially related detail elsewhere in the passage.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-reading-cas-014',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Craft and Structure',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Political scientists studying voter turnout have long sought to identify which types of get-out-the-vote interventions actually succeed in persuading registered but historically infrequent voters to cast a ballot, and which interventions, despite seeming intuitively promising, produce little measurable effect once tested rigorously. Beginning in the early 2000s, a series of large-scale field experiments began transforming this area of research from one dominated largely by survey-based speculation into one grounded in randomized, controlled comparisons conducted on actual voter populations.\n\nOne of the most influential early studies randomly assigned tens of thousands of registered voters in a single state to receive one of several different get-out-the-vote interventions, or to a control group receiving no contact at all, then compared actual turnout rates, verified through public voting records rather than self-reported survey responses, across all groups. The results proved surprising to many campaign strategists at the time. Impersonal contact methods, such as pre-recorded phone calls and standard mailers, produced only marginal increases in turnout, often not statistically distinguishable from zero once the substantial cost of each contact attempt was factored into a cost-effectiveness comparison. Live phone calls from trained callers performed somewhat better, though the effect remained modest. The intervention that produced by far the largest measurable increase in turnout was old-fashioned door-to-door canvassing, in which a canvasser had a brief, direct, personal conversation with the voter on their own doorstep.\n\nDr. Priya Nandakumar, a political scientist who has conducted several subsequent replications of this original study design across different states and election types, explains that the canvassing effect appears to depend heavily on the genuinely personal, individualized nature of the interaction. "It\'s not simply that someo