    explanation: 'The passage directly connects the smaller replicated effects, attributed to differing electoral contexts and possible intervention saturation, to Nandakumar\'s later acknowledgment that "the size of this advantage appears considerably more context-dependent than early popular accounts of the original research sometimes suggested," tying the specific replication findings directly to this broader conclusion. The passage does not state canvassing has no effect whatsoever; Nandakumar explicitly remains "cautiously confident" that canvassing retains a "meaningful, if likely somewhat diminished, advantage." The passage does not claim live phone calls have been proven more effective than canvassing in every context; it describes canvassing\'s effect as diminished in some contexts, not reversed relative to phone calls. The passage\'s concluding paragraph explicitly praises the shift toward randomized field experimentation as a "lasting and valuable contribution," directly contradicting a claim that this method should be abandoned. ACT Tip: When a passage presents complicating follow-up findings, look for how those findings connect to a more nuanced conclusion stated later in the passage, rather than assuming they simply negate the original finding entirely.',
