mpetitive a given race appears to be and how frequently voters in a particular area have already been contacted by other campaigns, seems to meaningfully affect how large a canvassing effect any individual study is likely to detect.\n\nBeyond simply measuring which interventions increase turnout, researchers in this area have increasingly focused on a related but distinct question: whether observed turnout increases represent voters who would have eventually voted anyway, simply nudged to vote slightly earlier or with slightly less friction, or whether these interventions genuinely bring entirely new voters into the electorate who would otherwise not have voted at all in that particular election. This distinction matters considerably for how campaigns and civic organizations should think about the ultimate value of these interventions, since an intervention that merely shifts existing likely voters earlier in the process offers meaningfully different civic value than one that expands genuine electoral participation among voters who would otherwise have remained entirely disengaged from that specific election.\n\nDespite these ongoing complications and open questions, the broader methodological shift toward randomized field experimentation, rather than relying primarily on survey-based speculation about voter psychology, is now widely regarded within the discipline as a lasting and valuable contribution, one that has meaningfully improved how campaigns, civic organizations, and researchers alike think about designing and rigorously evaluating future turnout interventions.',
    questionText: 'The passage\'s discussion of the distinction between "nudging" existing voters versus "genuinely" expanding the electorate (paragraph 6) primarily functions to:',
    options: [
      'prove that all get-out-the-vote interventions are equally worthless regardless of this distinction',
      'introduce a further layer of nuance to how the value of turnout interventions should be evaluated, beyond simply measuring raw turnout increases',
      'argue that campaigns should stop measuring voter turnout altogether',
      'demonstrate that door-to-door canvassing exclusively nudges existing voters and never expands the electorate',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The paragraph explicitly frames this as "a related but distinct question" that "matters considerably for how campaigns and civic organizations should think about the ultimate value of these interventions," adding a further evaluative dimension beyond the raw turnout-increase measurements discussed earlier in the passage. The passage does not suggest interventions are worthless regardless of this distinction; it argues the distinction affects how their value should be interpreted, not that all interventions lack value. The passage does not argue that campaigns should stop measuring turnout; it argues for a more nuanced, additional layer of measurement and interpretation, not abandoning measurement entirely. The passage does not make any specific claim that canvassing exclusively nudges existing voters; it raises this distinction as an open, unresolved question applicable to interventions generally, not a settled claim about canvassing specifically. ACT Tip: When a passage introduces a "related but distinct" question late in its discussion, expect it to add nuance or complexity to the passage\'s evaluation of its subject, not to introduce a contradictory or dismissive new claim.',
  ),
