    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'In context, the passage describes "periods of suspicion in the Soviet Union" where an internationalist movement "periodically drew official scrutiny despite occasional periods of tolerance," fitting the sense of close, often suspicious official attention or examination directed at the movement. "Enthusiastic celebration" is essentially the opposite of the intended meaning, since the surrounding context describes suspicion and persecution, not celebration. "Complete indifference" contradicts the sentence\'s description of active official attention and suspicion; indifference would imply no attention at all. "Financial investment" does not fit the sentence\'s context, which concerns political suspicion and monitoring, not any financial relationship. ACT Tip: When a vocabulary word appears alongside words like "suspicion" and "persecution" in the surrounding context, use that context to confirm a negative, watchful connotation rather than a neutral or positive one.',
