      'argue that Zamenhof\'s original humanitarian vision was fundamentally naive and doomed from the start',
      'provide one specific, historically grounded explanation for Esperanto\'s limited global adoption, complementing the practical and linguistic explanations discussed in the previous paragraph',
      'prove that all Esperanto speakers were closely affiliated with Zamenhof\'s own political views',
      'suggest that Esperanto is no longer spoken by anyone today',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The paragraph explicitly frames these historical setbacks as adding to the practical inertia and Eurocentrism explanations discussed in the previous paragraph, stating that "the twentieth century\'s political upheavals dealt the movement further setbacks," directly building on and complementing the earlier discussion of why adoption remained limited. The passage does not characterize Zamenhof\'s vision as "naive and doomed"; it treats his humanitarian ambitions with a measured, factual tone throughout, without passing this kind of harsh judgment. The passage does not claim all Esperanto speakers shared Zamenhof\'s specific political views; the persecution described targeted the movement generally due to its internationalist character, not because every individual speaker held identical views to its founder. The passage\'s final paragraph explicitly states Esperanto "has persisted as a living language into the present day," directly contradicting a claim that no one speaks it today. ACT Tip: When a passage presents a historical complication following an earlier discussion of related causes, look for how the new information adds to or builds on the earlier explanation, rather than assuming it introduces an unrelated or contradictory claim.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-reading-kid-028',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.reading,
    skillArea: 'Key Ideas and Details',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'In 1887, a young ophthalmologist in Warsaw named L. L. Zamenhof published, under a modest pseudonym, a slim book proposing an entirely new language designed to be learned easily by speakers of any native tongue and adopted as a universal second language for international communication. Zamenhof called his creation Esperanto, meaning "one who hopes," a name that captured both his linguistic ambitions and a deeper, more personal conviction that shared language might help dissolve the ethnic and religious tensions he had witnessed firsthand growing up in a multilingual, often fractious corner of the Russian Empire.\n\nZamenhof\'s linguistic design choices reflected a careful, almost engineering-minded approach to eliminating the irregularities that make many natural languages notoriously difficult for adult learners to master. Esperanto\'s grammar contains no irregular verbs whatsoever, a stark contrast to the exception-riddled conjugation systems of languages like English or French. Every noun ends in a predictable vowel, every adjective agrees with its noun in a simple, consistent pattern, and word roots can be combined and modified using a small set of standardized prefixes and suffixes to generate new vocabulary systematically, rather than requiring learners to memorize enormous, often arbitrary vocabularies word by word. Zamenhof drew his vocabulary primarily from Romance and Germanic language roots already familiar to many European learners, a choice that made the language considerably easier for Europeans to acquire quickly, though later critics would note this same choice made Esperanto less immediately accessible to speakers of non-European language families.\n\nBeyond its purely linguistic architecture, Zamenhof envisioned Esperanto as serving an explicitly humanitarian purpose, a conviction he articulated with increasing directness throughout his life. He believed that a shared, culturally neutral second language, one belonging to no single nation or ethnic group and therefore conferring no inherent political advantage on any of its native speakers, since Esperanto has no native speakers by design, could serve as a practical tool for reducing the kind of nationalist and ethnic tension that would erupt catastrophically in the following century. This aspiration, sometimes called "internal idea" within the Esperanto movement itself, distinguished Zamenhof\'s project from other, more narrowly linguistic constructed-language efforts of the era, which focused primarily on efficiency or logical elegance without Zamenhof\'s explicit humanitarian and pacifist ambitions.\n\nThe language attracted a modest but genuinely international following within Zamenhof\'s own lifetime, with Esperanto clubs and publications emerging across Europe and, somewhat more sparsely, in other parts of the world. The first World Esperanto Congress, held in France in 1905, drew participants from numerous countries who conversed with each other, often for the first time, entirely in the constructed language, providing an early practical demonstration that Esperanto could function as genuine spoken communication, not merely a theoretical linguistic exercise confined to written correspondence.\n\nDespite this early enthusiasm, Esperanto never achieved anything close to Zamenhof\'s hoped-for status as a genuinely universal international auxiliary language. Critics have offered several overlapping explanations for this limited adoption. Some point to simple practical inertia: national governments and international institutions had little incentive to adopt a politically neutral constructed language when existing major languages, backed by powerful nations with established diplomatic, commercial, and cultural infrastructure, already served most practical international communication needs reasonably well. Others note that Esperanto\'s Eurocentric vocabulary and grammar, while easing acquisition for European learners, may have limited its appeal as a genuinely neutral choice for speakers from non-European linguistic backgrounds, somewhat undermining the very neutrality Zamenhof had hoped the language would embody.\n\nThe twentieth century\'s political upheavals dealt the movement further setbacks. Esperanto speakers faced persecution under multiple authoritarian regimes during the twentieth century, including explicit condemnation in Nazi Germany, where the language\'s Jewish creator and its internationalist ideals were viewed with particular hostility, and periods of suspicion in the Soviet Union as well, where any strongly internationalist movement operating somewhat outside state control periodically drew official scrutiny despite occasional periods of tolerance.\n\nDespite never achieving Zamenhof\'s original ambitions for universal adoption, Esperanto has persisted as a living language into the present day, with an estimated hundreds of thousands to low millions of speakers worldwide, an active body of original and translated literature, and a small population of speakers who have raised children as native Esperanto speakers from birth, an unusual status for a constructed language originally designed explicitly to belong to no one natively. Contemporary linguists studying Esperanto note that, whatever its ultimate real-world political impact, the language remains a genuinely useful case study in constructed language design, offering insight into which linguistic features most reliably ease adult second-language acquisition, a question with genuine relevance well beyond the specific historical and political ambitions that originally motivated Zamenhof\'s nineteenth-century creation.',
    questionText: 'According to the passage, what was Zamenhof\'s profession?',
    options: [
      'Linguist',
      'Ophthalmologist',
      'Diplomat',
      'Journalist',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The passage states directly that Zamenhof was "a young ophthalmologist in Warsaw." The passage never identifies Zamenhof as a professional linguist by trade; his linguistic work on Esperanto is described as a personal project alongside his medical profession, not his stated occupation. The passage does not describe Zamenhof as a diplomat. The passage does not describe Zamenhof as a journalist. ACT Tip: For a direct factual question about a person\'s stated profession, locate the exact sentence identifying that profession rather than assuming a role suggested by their most famous accomplishment.',
  ),
