peal as a genuinely neutral choice for speakers from non-European linguistic backgrounds, somewhat undermining the very neutrality Zamenhof had hoped the language would embody.\n\nThe twentieth century\'s political upheavals dealt the movement further setbacks. Esperanto speakers faced persecution under multiple authoritarian regimes during the twentieth century, including explicit condemnation in Nazi Germany, where the language\'s Jewish creator and its internationalist ideals were viewed with particular hostility, and periods of suspicion in the Soviet Union as well, where any strongly internationalist movement operating somewhat outside state control periodically drew official scrutiny despite occasional periods of tolerance.\n\nDespite never achieving Zamenhof\'s original ambitions for universal adoption, Esperanto has persisted as a living language into the present day, with an estimated hundreds of thousands to low millions of speakers worldwide, an active body of original and translated literature, and a small population of speakers who have raised children as native Esperanto speakers from birth, an unusual status for a constructed language originally designed explicitly to belong to no one natively. Contemporary linguists studying Esperanto note that, whatever its ultimate real-world political impact, the language remains a genuinely useful case study in constructed language design, offering insight into which linguistic features most reliably ease adult second-language acquisition, a question with genuine relevance well beyond the specific historical and political ambitions that originally motivated Zamenhof\'s nineteenth-century creation.',
    questionText: 'Based on the passage, what distinguishes Zamenhof\'s Esperanto project from other constructed-language efforts of the same era, according to the passage?',
    options: [
      'Esperanto was the only constructed language ever created before the twentieth century',
      'Esperanto\'s explicit humanitarian and pacifist ambitions distinguished it from other constructed-language efforts that focused primarily on linguistic efficiency or logical elegance',
      'Esperanto was the only constructed language that included any grammatical rules at all',
      'Esperanto was created collaboratively by an international committee rather than by a single individual',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The passage explicitly states that the "internal idea" "distinguished Zamenhof\'s project from other, more narrowly linguistic constructed-language efforts of the era, which focused primarily on efficiency or logical elegance without Zamenhof\'s explicit humanitarian and pacifist ambitions." The passage does not claim Esperanto was the only constructed language ever created before the twentieth century; it explicitly references "other...constructed-language efforts of the era," acknowledging Esperanto was not unique in this general sense. The passage does not make any claim about Esperanto being the only constructed language with grammatical rules; all constructed languages by definition require some grammatical structure, and the passage does not compare this specific feature across different projects. The passage attributes Esperanto\'s creation to Zamenhof individually, describing him publishing his proposal under a pseudonym, directly contradicting a claim of collaborative committee creation. ACT Tip: When a question asks what distinguishes one specific effort from similar contemporaneous efforts, locate the passage\'s own explicit comparative statement rather than assuming an unstated basis for comparison.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
