    questionText: 'The passage\'s comparison between octopus color change and hormonal color change in other animals (paragraph 1) primarily serves to:',
    options: [
      'prove that hormonal color change is a more effective camouflage strategy than neural control',
      'highlight the speed advantage of the octopus\'s neural control mechanism, setting up the passage\'s detailed explanation of how chromatophores achieve this rapid change',
      'argue that only octopuses are capable of any form of color change in the animal kingdom',
      'explain why octopuses are unable to perceive color themselves',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The passage explicitly contrasts the "comparatively slow hormonal signals that can take minutes or hours" in other animals with the octopus\'s neural control achieving change "within a fraction of a second," directly setting up the following paragraph\'s detailed technical explanation of exactly how chromatophores achieve this speed. The passage does not claim hormonal color change is more effective; it explicitly emphasizes the octopus\'s neural mechanism as notably faster, without making a broader claim about overall camouflage effectiveness between the two mechanisms. The passage does not claim octopuses are the only animals capable of color change; it explicitly acknowledges other animals achieve color change too, just through a different, slower mechanism. The octopus\'s inability to perceive color is discussed much later in the passage as a separate, unrelated puzzle, not explained by this early comparison to hormonal color change mechanisms. ACT Tip: When a passage opens with a comparison to a related but different phenomenon, that comparison typically functions to highlight a specific, notable feature of the passage\'s main subject, setting up more detailed explanation to follow.',
  ),
