  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-emi-008',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'City environmental officials placed air quality monitors at five locations with differing traffic and industrial exposure, recording the average daily concentration of fine particulate matter (PM2.5, measured in micrograms per cubic meter) over one month at each site. Table 1 shows the results.\n\nTable 1: Average Daily PM2.5 Concentration by Monitoring Site\n\nSite 1 (residential, low traffic): 8.2 micrograms/cubic meter\nSite 2 (residential, near highway): 15.6 micrograms/cubic meter\nSite 3 (downtown commercial district): 18.9 micrograms/cubic meter\nSite 4 (industrial zone): 24.3 micrograms/cubic meter\nSite 5 (city park, away from roads): 6.1 micrograms/cubic meter\n\nOfficials noted that the national air quality standard sets a recommended annual average limit of 12 micrograms per cubic meter for PM2.5, and that concentrations above this level are associated with increased respiratory health risks with long-term exposure.',
    questionText: 'Based on Table 1, a city council member proposing to reduce citywide PM2.5 exposure would most likely prioritize interventions targeting which site first, based purely on the data shown?',
    options: [
      'Site 1',
      'Site 4',
      'Site 5',
      'None of the sites require intervention',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Site 4, the industrial zone, has the highest PM2.5 concentration of all five sites at 24.3 micrograms/cubic meter, more than double the national standard of 12 micrograms/cubic meter, making it the site with the greatest measured need for intervention based purely on the data. Site 1\'s concentration of 8.2 micrograms/cubic meter already falls below the national standard, making it a lower priority than sites exceeding the standard. Site 5\'s concentration of 6.1 micrograms/cubic meter is the lowest of all five sites and well below the national standard, making it the least likely priority for intervention. Three of the five sites, including Site 4, exceed the national standard, directly contradicting a claim that no sites require intervention based on the stated standard. ACT Tip: When asked to prioritize an intervention based on data, identify the specific data point that most exceeds a relevant stated threshold or standard, rather than assuming all sites are equally in need of attention.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-iod-009',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'A city public works department tracked monthly electricity consumption (in megawatt-hours, MWh) for its streetlight network as it gradually converted fixtures from older sodium-vapor bulbs to LED bulbs over a two-year period. Figure 1 shows total monthly streetlight electricity consumption alongside the cumulative percentage of fixtures converted to LED at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Streetlight Electricity Consumption and Percent LED Conversion\n\nMonth 0: 0% converted, 420 MWh\nMonth 4: 20% converted, 378 MWh\nMonth 8: 40% converted, 336 MWh\nMonth 12: 60% converted, 294 MWh\nMonth 16: 80% converted, 252 MWh\nMonth 20: 100% converted, 210 MWh\n\nThe department noted that LED bulbs consume significantly less electricity than sodium-vapor bulbs while producing comparable or better light output, and that the conversion project\'s total cost is expected to be fully recovered through electricity savings within six years of full completion.',
    questionText: 'According to Figure 1, what was the total monthly streetlight electricity consumption at Month 12?',
    options: [
      '336 MWh',
      '294 MWh',
      '252 MWh',
      '210 MWh',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Figure 1 lists Month 12 (60% converted) with a consumption of 294 MWh. The value 336 MWh corresponds to Month 8, not Month 12. The value 252 MWh corresponds to Month 16, not Month 12. The value 210 MWh corresponds to Month 20, not Month 12. ACT Tip: When several measurement points have closely spaced values, trace the exact month stated in the question to the matching data point before selecting an answer.',
  ),
