  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-emi-013',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'A city public works department tracked monthly electricity consumption (in megawatt-hours, MWh) for its streetlight network as it gradually converted fixtures from older sodium-vapor bulbs to LED bulbs over a two-year period. Figure 1 shows total monthly streetlight electricity consumption alongside the cumulative percentage of fixtures converted to LED at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Streetlight Electricity Consumption and Percent LED Conversion\n\nMonth 0: 0% converted, 420 MWh\nMonth 4: 20% converted, 378 MWh\nMonth 8: 40% converted, 336 MWh\nMonth 12: 60% converted, 294 MWh\nMonth 16: 80% converted, 252 MWh\nMonth 20: 100% converted, 210 MWh\n\nThe department noted that LED bulbs consume significantly less electricity than sodium-vapor bulbs while producing comparable or better light output, and that the conversion project\'s total cost is expected to be fully recovered through electricity savings within six years of full completion.',
    questionText: 'Based on the pattern shown in Figure 1, which of the following best describes the relationship between percent LED conversion and electricity consumption reduction across the six measurement points?',
    options: [
      'Each 20 percentage-point increase in conversion corresponds to a consistent reduction of approximately 42 MWh in consumption',
      'Each 20 percentage-point increase in conversion corresponds to a steadily increasing amount of consumption reduction',
      'Each 20 percentage-point increase in conversion corresponds to a steadily decreasing amount of consumption reduction',
      'There is no consistent relationship between conversion percentage and consumption reduction',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'Calculating the reduction between each consecutive pair of measurement points: 420 to 378 is 42 MWh, 378 to 336 is 42 MWh, 336 to 294 is 42 MWh, 294 to 252 is 42 MWh, and 252 to 210 is 42 MWh, showing a remarkably consistent reduction of 42 MWh for each 20 percentage-point increase in conversion. The reductions do not increase across intervals; they remain consistently at 42 MWh throughout, directly contradicting a claim of a steadily increasing reduction amount. The reductions do not decrease across intervals either; they remain consistently at 42 MWh throughout, directly contradicting a claim of a steadily decreasing reduction amount. The data shows a highly consistent, linear relationship between conversion percentage and consumption reduction, directly contradicting a claim of no consistent relationship. ACT Tip: To characterize the relationship between two variables across multiple data points, calculate the change at each interval and compare those changes to determine whether the relationship is linear, accelerating, decelerating, or inconsistent.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-iod-014',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'A city public works department tracked monthly electricity consumption (in megawatt-hours, MWh) for its streetlight network as it gradually converted fixtures from older sodium-vapor bulbs to LED bulbs over a two-year period. Figure 1 shows total monthly streetlight electricity consumption alongside the cumulative percentage of fixtures converted to LED at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Streetlight Electricity Consumption and Percent LED Conversion\n\nMonth 0: 0% converted, 420 MWh\nMonth 4: 20% converted, 378 MWh\nMonth 8: 40% converted, 336 MWh\nMonth 12: 60% converted, 294 MWh\nMonth 16: 80% converted, 252 MWh\nMonth 20: 100% converted, 210 MWh\n\nThe department noted that LED bulbs consume significantly less electricity than sodium-vapor bulbs while producing comparable or better light output, and that the conversion project\'s total cost is expected to be fully recovered through electricity savings within six years of full completion.',
    questionText: 'According to Figure 1, at what percent conversion did consumption first drop below 300 MWh?',
    options: [
      '20%',
      '40%',
      '60%',
      '80%',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'At 40% conversion, consumption was 336 MWh, still above 300 MWh. At 60% conversion, consumption dropped to 294 MWh, the first measurement below 300 MWh. At 20% conversion, consumption was 378 MWh, well above 300 MWh. At 80% conversion, consumption was 252 MWh, also below 300 MWh, but this is not the first measurement point at which consumption dropped below 300 MWh, since that occurred earlier at 60% conversion. ACT Tip: When asked for the first time a threshold is crossed, check each measurement point in sequence rather than selecting a later point where the value is also below the threshold.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-emi-015',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'A city public works department tracked monthly electricity consumption (in megawatt-hours, MWh) for its streetlight network as it gradually converted fixtures from older sodium-vapor bulbs to LED bulbs over a two-year period. Figure 1 shows total monthly streetlight electricity consumption alongside the cumulative percentage of fixtures converted to LED at six measurement points.\n\nFigure 1: Streetlight Electricity Consumption and Percent LED Conversion\n\nMonth 0: 0% converted, 420 MWh\nMonth 4: 20% converted, 378 MWh\nMonth 8: 40% converted, 336 MWh\nMonth 12: 60% converted, 294 MWh\nMonth 16: 80% converted, 252 MWh\nMonth 20: 100% converted, 210 MWh\n\nThe department noted that LED bulbs consume significantly less electricity than sodium-vapor bulbs while producing comparable or better light output, and that the conversion project\'s total cost is expected to be fully recovered through electricity savings within six years of full completion.',
    questionText: 'Based on the consistent linear pattern shown in Figure 1, if the city had only converted 50% of its fixtures rather than reaching 100%, electricity consumption would most likely be closest to which of the following?',
    options: [
      '273 MWh',
      '294 MWh',
      '315 MWh',
      '336 MWh',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'Given the consistent linear relationship in which each 20 percentage-point increase corresponds to a 42 MWh reduction, the reduction per percentage point is 42/20 = 2.1 MWh per percentage point. At 50% conversion, the reduction from the starting 420 MWh would be 50 x 2.1 = 105 MWh, giving an estimated consumption of 420 - 105 = 315 MWh. A value of 273 MWh would correspond to a higher conversion percentage than 50%, overshooting the expected value at this specific conversion level. A value of 294 MWh matches the actual measured value at 60% conversion, not 50%, making it inconsistent with the lower conversion percentage specified. A value of 336 MWh matches the actual measured value at 40% conversion, undershooting the expected value for the higher 50% conversion level specified. ACT Tip: When a data set shows a consistent linear rate of change, calculate the rate per unit of the independent variable, then apply that rate to estimate a value at a specific point not directly listed in the data.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-sci-016',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Researchers conducted three experiments to study how caffeine consumption affects reaction time and alertness in adult volunteers, measuring reaction time in milliseconds using a standardized computer-based test in which participants pressed a button as quickly as possible after a visual signal appeared.\n\nExperiment 1\nResearchers gave volunteers one of four caffeine doses, dissolved in an identical-tasting beverage to prevent participants from identifying their dose, and measured average reaction time 45 minutes later.\n0 mg (placebo): 312 ms\n50 mg: 289 ms\n100 mg: 271 ms\n200 mg: 268 ms\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four doses, researchers also measured self-reported alertness on a 1-to-10 scale 45 minutes after consumption.\n0 mg (placebo): 4.2\n50 mg: 5.8\n100 mg: 7.1\n200 mg: 7.9\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers gave a new group of volunteers a single 100 mg dose and measured reaction time at several points after consumption to determine how the effect changed over time.\n15 minutes after consumption: 298 ms\n45 minutes after consumption: 271 ms\n90 minutes after consumption: 275 ms\n180 minutes after consumption: 301 ms\n360 minutes after consumption: 315 ms\n\nResearchers noted that all volunteers reported abstaining from caffeine for at least 24 hours before each testing session, and that each dose group in Experiments 1 and 2 included 25 participants whose results were averaged.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 1, which caffeine dose resulted in the fastest average reaction time?',
    options: [
      '0 mg (placebo)',
      '50 mg',
      '100 mg',
      '200 mg',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'Experiment 1 reports the 200 mg dose resulted in an average reaction time of 268 ms, the fastest (lowest) value among the four doses (312, 289, 271, and 268 ms). The 0 mg placebo produced the slowest reaction time of all four conditions, 312 ms. The 50 mg dose produced 289 ms, faster than placebo but slower than the higher doses. The 100 mg dose produced 271 ms, faster than 50 mg but still slightly slower than 200 mg. ACT Tip: For reaction time data, remember that a lower number in milliseconds represents a faster response, so "fastest" corresponds to the smallest value, not the largest.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-sci-017',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Researchers conducted three experiments to study how caffeine consumption affects reaction time and alertness in adult volunteers, measuring reaction time in milliseconds using a standardized computer-based test in which participants pressed a button as quickly as possible after a visual signal appeared.\n\nExperiment 1\nResearchers gave volunteers one of four caffeine doses, dissolved in an identical-tasting beverage to prevent participants from identifying their dose, and measured average reaction time 45 minutes later.\n0 mg (placebo): 312 ms\n50 mg: 289 ms\n100 mg: 271 ms\n200 mg: 268 ms\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four doses, researchers also measured self-reported alertness on a 1-to-10 scale 45 minutes after consumption.\n0 mg (placebo): 4.2\n50 mg: 5.8\n100 mg: 7.1\n200 mg: 7.9\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers gave a new group of volunteers a single 100 mg dose and measured reaction time at several points after consumption to determine how the effect changed over time.\n15 minutes after consumption: 298 ms\n45 minutes after consumption: 271 ms\n90 minutes after consumption: 275 ms\n180 minutes after consumption: 301 ms\n360 minutes after consumption: 315 ms\n\nResearchers noted that all volunteers reported abstaining from caffeine for at least 24 hours before each testing session, and that each dose group in Experiments 1 and 2 included 25 participants whose results were averaged.',
    questionText: 'What was the purpose of dissolving each caffeine dose in an identical-tasting beverage in Experiment 1?',
    options: [
      'To prevent participants from identifying their dose, reducing the chance that expectations about the dose would influence their reaction time results',
      'To make the caffeine taste better to the participants',
      'To ensure the caffeine would dissolve completely and not settle at the bottom of the container',
      'To measure how quickly caffeine dissolves in different types of liquid',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The passage explicitly states the beverage was "identical-tasting...to prevent participants from identifying their dose," a standard research practice designed to prevent participants\' expectations about receiving a high or low dose from influencing their actual measured performance, a potential confounding variable. The passage does not state that improving taste was the goal; the stated purpose is specifically about preventing dose identification, not general palatability. The passage does not discuss dissolution completeness or settling as a concern; this is not mentioned anywhere in the passage. The passage does not describe measuring dissolution rates in different liquids; this is not a stated purpose or measured outcome in the passage. ACT Tip: When a passage describes a specific experimental control, such as disguising a treatment\'s identity, its purpose is typically to prevent a specific confounding variable, like participant expectation, from affecting the measured outcome.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-sci-018',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Researchers conducted three experiments to study how caffeine consumption affects reaction time and alertness in adult volunteers, measuring reaction time in milliseconds using a standardized computer-based test in which participants pressed a button as quickly as possible after a visual signal appeared.\n\nExperiment 1\nResearchers gave volunteers one of four caffeine doses, dissolved in an identical-tasting beverage to prevent participants from identifying their dose, and measured average reaction time 45 minutes later.\n0 mg (placebo): 312 ms\n50 mg: 289 ms\n100 mg: 271 ms\n200 mg: 268 ms\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four doses, researchers also measured self-reported alertness on a 1-to-10 scale 45 minutes after consumption.\n0 mg (placebo): 4.2\n50 mg: 5.8\n100 mg: 7.1\n200 mg: 7.9\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers gave a new group of volunteers a single 100 mg dose and measured reaction time at several points after consumption to determine how the effect changed over time.\n15 minutes after consumption: 298 ms\n45 minutes after consumption: 271 ms\n90 minutes after consumption: 275 ms\n180 minutes after consumption: 301 ms\n360 minutes after consumption: 315 ms\n\nResearchers noted that all volunteers reported abstaining from caffeine for at least 24 hours before each testing session, and that each dose group in Experiments 1 and 2 included 25 participants whose results were averaged.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 2, which caffeine dose resulted in the highest average self-reported alertness score?',
    options: [
      '0 mg (placebo)',
      '50 mg',
      '100 mg',
      '200 mg',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'D',
    explanation: 'Experiment 2 reports the 200 mg dose produced an average alertness score of 7.9, the highest value among the four doses (4.2, 5.8, 7.1, and 7.9). The 0 mg placebo produced the lowest alertness score of all four conditions, 4.2. The 50 mg dose produced 5.8, higher than placebo but lower than the higher doses. The 100 mg dose produced 7.1, higher than 50 mg but still lower than 200 mg. ACT Tip: Even when two experiments measure different variables, such as reaction time and alertness, check each experiment\'s data independently rather than assuming the same condition will rank highest in both, though in this case both do point toward the same dose.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-emi-019',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Researchers conducted three experiments to study how caffeine consumption affects reaction time and alertness in adult volunteers, measuring reaction time in milliseconds using a standardized computer-based test in which participants pressed a button as quickly as possible after a visual signal appeared.\n\nExperiment 1\nResearchers gave volunteers one of four caffeine doses, dissolved in an identical-tasting beverage to prevent participants from identifying their dose, and measured average reaction time 45 minutes later.\n0 mg (placebo): 312 ms\n50 mg: 289 ms\n100 mg: 271 ms\n200 mg: 268 ms\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four doses, researchers also measured self-reported alertness on a 1-to-10 scale 45 minutes after consumption.\n0 mg (placebo): 4.2\n50 mg: 5.8\n100 mg: 7.1\n200 mg: 7.9\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers gave a new group of volunteers a single 100 mg dose and measured reaction time at several points after consumption to determine how the effect changed over time.\n15 minutes after consumption: 298 ms\n45 minutes after consumption: 271 ms\n90 minutes after consumption: 275 ms\n180 minutes after consumption: 301 ms\n360 minutes after consumption: 315 ms\n\nResearchers noted that all volunteers reported abstaining from caffeine for at least 24 hours before each testing session, and that each dose group in Experiments 1 and 2 included 25 participants whose results were averaged.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiments 1 and 2 together, a researcher concludes that doubling the caffeine dose always produces a proportionally doubled improvement in both reaction time and alertness. Is this conclusion well supported by the data?',
    options: [
      'Yes, because both reaction time and alertness improved at every dose increase tested',
      'No, because while both measures generally improved with increasing dose, the size of improvement was not consistently proportional; for example, the improvement from 100 mg to 200 mg was much smaller than the improvement from 0 mg to 100 mg in both measures',
      'Yes, because the 200 mg dose produced exactly double the reaction time improvement of the 100 mg dose compared to placebo',
      'No, because reaction time and alertness both worsened as caffeine dose increased',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'From 0 mg to 100 mg, reaction time improved by 312 - 271 = 41 ms, while from 100 mg to 200 mg, it improved by only 271 - 268 = 3 ms; similarly, alertness improved by 7.1 - 4.2 = 2.9 points from 0 to 100 mg but only by 7.9 - 7.1 = 0.8 points from 100 to 200 mg, showing the improvement diminishes substantially rather than remaining proportional as dose increases. Both measures do generally improve with each dose increase, but a general improvement at every step is not the same as a claim of consistent, doubled proportionality, which the diminishing improvement sizes directly contradict. The improvement from 100 mg to 200 mg (3 ms) is dramatically smaller, not double, the improvement from 0 mg to 100 mg (41 ms), directly contradicting a claim of exact doubling. Both reaction time and alertness clearly improved, not worsened, as dose increased across all four conditions in both experiments, directly contradicting this option. ACT Tip: A claim of "proportional" or "doubled" effect requires checking not just whether a general improving trend exists, but whether the specific magnitude of change remains consistent as the underlying variable increases.',
  ),
