    options: [
      '12 hours',
      '24 hours',
      '48 hours',
      '72 hours',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The passage states directly that "all volunteers reported abstaining from caffeine for at least 24 hours before each testing session." The other listed durations are not mentioned anywhere in the passage. ACT Tip: For direct factual questions about experimental setup details, locate the exact sentence describing the procedure rather than estimating from the results.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-emi-022',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Researchers conducted three experiments to study how caffeine consumption affects reaction time and alertness in adult volunteers, measuring reaction time in milliseconds using a standardized computer-based test in which participants pressed a button as quickly as possible after a visual signal appeared.\n\nExperiment 1\nResearchers gave volunteers one of four caffeine doses, dissolved in an identical-tasting beverage to prevent participants from identifying their dose, and measured average reaction time 45 minutes later.\n0 mg (placebo): 312 ms\n50 mg: 289 ms\n100 mg: 271 ms\n200 mg: 268 ms\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four doses, researchers also measured self-reported alertness on a 1-to-10 scale 45 minutes after consumption.\n0 mg (placebo): 4.2\n50 mg: 5.8\n100 mg: 7.1\n200 mg: 7.9\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers gave a new group of volunteers a single 100 mg dose and measured reaction time at several points after consumption to determine how the effect changed over time.\n15 minutes after consumption: 298 ms\n45 minutes after consumption: 271 ms\n90 minutes after consumption: 275 ms\n180 minutes after consumption: 301 ms\n360 minutes after consumption: 315 ms\n\nResearchers noted that all volunteers reported abstaining from caffeine for at least 24 hours before each testing session, and that each dose group in Experiments 1 and 2 included 25 participants whose results were averaged.',
    questionText: 'What was the purpose of requiring volunteers to abstain from caffeine for at least 24 hours before each testing session?',
    options: [
      'To ensure that any measured effects in the experiment could be attributed to the specific test dose, without residual caffeine from prior consumption confounding the results',
      'To determine the maximum safe caffeine dose for human consumption',
      'To measure how quickly caffeine is eliminated from the body',
      'To make the experiment take longer to complete',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'By requiring abstinence before each session, researchers ensured that any measured reaction time or alertness effects could be attributed specifically to the caffeine dose given during the test, rather than being confounded by residual caffeine from a participant\'s prior, uncontrolled consumption. The passage does not describe any measurement of maximum safe dosage; the doses tested (0 to 200 mg) are well within commonly consumed ranges, and safety thresholds are not discussed. The passage does not describe measuring caffeine elimination rates from the body; this is a different type of study not described in the passage. The abstinence requirement is a scientific control measure, not a stated method for extending the experiment\'s duration, and the passage gives no indication this was the purpose. ACT Tip: When a variable like prior substance use is described as being controlled through participant instructions, its purpose is almost always to prevent that specific past behavior from confounding the measured results of the actual experimental treatment being tested.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-sci-023',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Researchers conducted three experiments to study how caffeine consumption affects reaction time and alertness in adult volunteers, measuring reaction time in milliseconds using a standardized computer-based test in which participants pressed a button as quickly as possible after a visual signal appeared.\n\nExperiment 1\nResearchers gave volunteers one of four caffeine doses, dissolved in an identical-tasting beverage to prevent participants from identifying their dose, and measured average reaction time 45 minutes later.\n0 mg (placebo): 312 ms\n50 mg: 289 ms\n100 mg: 271 ms\n200 mg: 268 ms\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four doses, researchers also measured self-reported alertness on a 1-to-10 scale 45 minutes after consumption.\n0 mg (placebo): 4.2\n50 mg: 5.8\n100 mg: 7.1\n200 mg: 7.9\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers gave a new group of volunteers a single 100 mg dose and measured reaction time at several points after consumption to determine how the effect changed over time.\n15 minutes after consumption: 298 ms\n45 minutes after consumption: 271 ms\n90 minutes after consumption: 275 ms\n180 minutes after consumption: 301 ms\n360 minutes after consumption: 315 ms\n\nResearchers noted that all volunteers reported abstaining from caffeine for at least 24 hours before each testing session, and that each dose group in Experiments 1 and 2 included 25 participants whose results were averaged.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiment 3, approximately how many minutes after consumption did reaction time return to being slower than the original 15-minute measurement of 298 ms?',
    options: [
      'Between 15 and 45 minutes',
      'Between 45 and 90 minutes',
      'Between 90 and 180 minutes',
      'Reaction time never became slower than the 15-minute measurement',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'At 90 minutes, reaction time was 275 ms, still faster than the 15-minute value of 298 ms. At 180 minutes, reaction time was 301 ms, which is slower than 298 ms. This means reaction time crossed back above the original 15-minute measurement sometime between the 90-minute and 180-minute measurements. Between 15 and 45 minutes, reaction time was improving (getting faster), not returning to a slower level. Between 45 and 90 minutes, reaction time remained faster than the 15-minute baseline, staying at 275 ms by 90 minutes. The data clearly shows reaction time becoming slower than the 15-minute measurement by the 180-minute mark, directly contradicting a claim that this never occurred. ACT Tip: To find when a value crosses back past an earlier reference point, identify the two consecutive measurements between which that reference value falls, rather than the measurement point closest to the reference value.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-emi-024',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Researchers conducted three experiments to study how caffeine consumption affects reaction time and alertness in adult volunteers, measuring reaction time in milliseconds using a standardized computer-based test in which participants pressed a button as quickly as possible after a visual signal appeared.\n\nExperiment 1\nResearchers gave volunteers one of four caffeine doses, dissolved in an identical-tasting beverage to prevent participants from identifying their dose, and measured average reaction time 45 minutes later.\n0 mg (placebo): 312 ms\n50 mg: 289 ms\n100 mg: 271 ms\n200 mg: 268 ms\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing the same four doses, researchers also measured self-reported alertness on a 1-to-10 scale 45 minutes after consumption.\n0 mg (placebo): 4.2\n50 mg: 5.8\n100 mg: 7.1\n200 mg: 7.9\n\nExperiment 3\nResearchers gave a new group of volunteers a single 100 mg dose and measured reaction time at several points after consumption to determine how the effect changed over time.\n15 minutes after consumption: 298 ms\n45 minutes after consumption: 271 ms\n90 minutes after consumption: 275 ms\n180 minutes after consumption: 301 ms\n360 minutes after consumption: 315 ms\n\nResearchers noted that all volunteers reported abstaining from caffeine for at least 24 hours before each testing session, and that each dose group in Experiments 1 and 2 included 25 participants whose results were averaged.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiment 3, which of the following best describes the overall time course of caffeine\'s effect on reaction time after a single 100 mg dose?',
    options: [
      'Reaction time improves quickly, reaches a peak improvement around 45 minutes, remains fairly stable through about 90 minutes, and then gradually returns toward, and eventually past, baseline levels by 360 minutes',
      'Reaction time improves continuously and indefinitely for the entire 360-minute period with no decline',
      'Reaction time shows no measurable change at any point during the 360-minute period',
      'Reaction time is fastest immediately at 15 minutes and then declines steadily for the rest of the period',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The data shows reaction time improving from 298 ms at 15 minutes to a peak of 271 ms at 45 minutes, remaining relatively stable at 275 ms at 90 minutes, then gradually worsening to 301 ms at 180 minutes and 315 ms at 360 minutes, which is slower than even the original 15-minute measurement, matching this full time-course description. Reaction time does not continue improving indefinitely; it worsens substantially after 90 minutes, directly contradicting a claim of continuous, indefinite improvement. The data shows clear, substantial changes in reaction time across the five time points, directly contradicting a claim of no measurable change. Reaction time is not fastest at 15 minutes; it continues improving after 15 minutes, reaching its fastest point at 45 minutes before later declining, directly contradicting this option\'s description of immediate peak followed by steady decline. ACT Tip: When describing a time-course pattern with multiple data points, trace the value at every single time point in sequence to identify the complete shape of the pattern, including any peak, plateau, and eventual decline, rather than describing only the beginning and end points.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-sci-025',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Biochemistry students conducted two experiments to study how pH affects the activity of an enzyme that breaks down starch into simpler sugars, measuring enzyme activity as the amount of sugar produced (in micromoles) from a fixed starch sample over a 10-minute reaction period.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested the enzyme\'s activity at six different pH levels, holding temperature constant at 37 C. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\npH 3: 8 micromoles\npH 5: 34 micromoles\npH 7: 62 micromoles\npH 8: 58 micromoles\npH 9: 29 micromoles\npH 11: 6 micromoles\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only pH 7, the optimal level identified in Experiment 1, students varied the reaction temperature to determine its effect on enzyme activity. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\n20 C: 41 micromoles\n30 C: 55 micromoles\n37 C: 62 micromoles\n45 C: 48 micromoles\n55 C: 12 micromoles\n\nStudents noted that at 55 C, microscopic examination of the enzyme sample showed signs of structural breakdown, a process called denaturation, in which the enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape becomes distorted and its function is impaired.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 1, at which pH level was enzyme activity highest?',
    options: [
      'pH 5',
      'pH 7',
      'pH 8',
      'pH 9',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Experiment 1 reports 62 micromoles of sugar produced at pH 7, the highest value among the six pH levels tested (8, 34, 62, 58, 29, and 6 micromoles). At pH 5, 34 micromoles were produced, lower than at pH 7. At pH 8, 58 micromoles were produced, close to but still lower than the peak at pH 7. At pH 9, only 29 micromoles were produced, notably lower than the peak. ACT Tip: When a data set shows values rising and then falling across a range, such as pH here, identify the single peak value rather than assuming the highest value occurs at either extreme of the tested range.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-sci-026',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Biochemistry students conducted two experiments to study how pH affects the activity of an enzyme that breaks down starch into simpler sugars, measuring enzyme activity as the amount of sugar produced (in micromoles) from a fixed starch sample over a 10-minute reaction period.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested the enzyme\'s activity at six different pH levels, holding temperature constant at 37 C. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\npH 3: 8 micromoles\npH 5: 34 micromoles\npH 7: 62 micromoles\npH 8: 58 micromoles\npH 9: 29 micromoles\npH 11: 6 micromoles\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only pH 7, the optimal level identified in Experiment 1, students varied the reaction temperature to determine its effect on enzyme activity. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\n20 C: 41 micromoles\n30 C: 55 micromoles\n37 C: 62 micromoles\n45 C: 48 micromoles\n55 C: 12 micromoles\n\nStudents noted that at 55 C, microscopic examination of the enzyme sample showed signs of structural breakdown, a process called denaturation, in which the enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape becomes distorted and its function is impaired.',
    questionText: 'Why did students hold temperature constant at 37 C throughout Experiment 1?',
    options: [
      'To isolate the effect of pH on enzyme activity without the added variable of differing temperatures',
      'Because 37 C is the temperature at which all enzymes become completely inactive',
      'Because the laboratory equipment could not be set to any other temperature',
      'To measure how quickly the starch sample would spoil at room temperature',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'By holding temperature constant while varying only pH, students ensured that any differences in sugar production could be attributed specifically to pH\'s effect on the enzyme, rather than to differences in temperature, which is a separate variable investigated later in Experiment 2. The passage does not state that 37 C causes enzymes to become completely inactive; in fact, Experiment 2 shows this temperature produces the highest activity level of all temperatures tested at pH 7, directly contradicting this claim. The passage gives no information suggesting the laboratory equipment was limited to a single temperature setting; Experiment 2 explicitly varies temperature across five different values. The passage does not describe any measurement of starch spoilage; the experiments measure enzyme-driven sugar production, not spoilage. ACT Tip: When a variable is described as being held constant across an experiment\'s trials, its purpose is almost always to isolate the effect of the variable that is deliberately changed, which in this case is investigated separately in a later experiment.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-sci-027',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Biochemistry students conducted two experiments to study how pH affects the activity of an enzyme that breaks down starch into simpler sugars, measuring enzyme activity as the amount of sugar produced (in micromoles) from a fixed starch sample over a 10-minute reaction period.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested the enzyme\'s activity at six different pH levels, holding temperature constant at 37 C. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\npH 3: 8 micromoles\npH 5: 34 micromoles\npH 7: 62 micromoles\npH 8: 58 micromoles\npH 9: 29 micromoles\npH 11: 6 micromoles\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only pH 7, the optimal level identified in Experiment 1, students varied the reaction temperature to determine its effect on enzyme activity. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\n20 C: 41 micromoles\n30 C: 55 micromoles\n37 C: 62 micromoles\n45 C: 48 micromoles\n55 C: 12 micromoles\n\nStudents noted that at 55 C, microscopic examination of the enzyme sample showed signs of structural breakdown, a process called denaturation, in which the enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape becomes distorted and its function is impaired.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 2, at which temperature was enzyme activity highest?',
    options: [
      '20 C',
      '30 C',
      '37 C',
      '45 C',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'Experiment 2 reports 62 micromoles of sugar produced at 37 C, the highest value among the five temperatures tested (41, 55, 62, 48, and 12 micromoles). At 20 C, 41 micromoles were produced, lower than at 37 C. At 30 C, 55 micromoles were produced, higher than at 20 C but still lower than at 37 C. At 45 C, only 48 micromoles were produced, notably lower than the peak at 37 C. ACT Tip: When comparing multiple conditions in a single experiment, scan the complete list of results before identifying the highest or lowest value.',
