  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-emi-028',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Biochemistry students conducted two experiments to study how pH affects the activity of an enzyme that breaks down starch into simpler sugars, measuring enzyme activity as the amount of sugar produced (in micromoles) from a fixed starch sample over a 10-minute reaction period.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested the enzyme\'s activity at six different pH levels, holding temperature constant at 37 C. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\npH 3: 8 micromoles\npH 5: 34 micromoles\npH 7: 62 micromoles\npH 8: 58 micromoles\npH 9: 29 micromoles\npH 11: 6 micromoles\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only pH 7, the optimal level identified in Experiment 1, students varied the reaction temperature to determine its effect on enzyme activity. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\n20 C: 41 micromoles\n30 C: 55 micromoles\n37 C: 62 micromoles\n45 C: 48 micromoles\n55 C: 12 micromoles\n\nStudents noted that at 55 C, microscopic examination of the enzyme sample showed signs of structural breakdown, a process called denaturation, in which the enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape becomes distorted and its function is impaired.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiment 2 and the students\' note about denaturation at 55 C, which of the following best explains why enzyme activity declined sharply between 45 C and 55 C?',
    options: [
      'The starch sample completely dissolved at 55 C, leaving nothing for the enzyme to act upon',
      'The enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape became distorted through denaturation at the higher temperature, impairing its ability to function properly',
      'The measurement device malfunctioned specifically at 55 C',
      'The reaction time was shortened specifically for the 55 C trial',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The passage explicitly states that at 55 C, "microscopic examination of the enzyme sample showed signs of structural breakdown, a process called denaturation, in which the enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape becomes distorted and its function is impaired," directly explaining the sharp decline in measured activity at this temperature. The passage does not mention the starch sample dissolving; the students\' note specifically attributes the decline to structural changes in the enzyme itself, not to any change in the starch substrate. The passage does not mention any measurement device malfunction; the explanation given is specifically about the enzyme\'s physical structure, not a technical measurement problem. The passage explicitly states the same 10-minute reaction period was used for all trials in Experiment 2, directly contradicting a claim that the 55 C trial used a shortened reaction time. ACT Tip: When a passage includes a researcher\'s own explanatory note about an unexpected result, use that note as the primary basis for interpreting the data pattern rather than proposing an unrelated explanation the passage does not support.',
  ),
