  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-iod-030',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Interpretation of Data',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Biochemistry students conducted two experiments to study how pH affects the activity of an enzyme that breaks down starch into simpler sugars, measuring enzyme activity as the amount of sugar produced (in micromoles) from a fixed starch sample over a 10-minute reaction period.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested the enzyme\'s activity at six different pH levels, holding temperature constant at 37 C. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\npH 3: 8 micromoles\npH 5: 34 micromoles\npH 7: 62 micromoles\npH 8: 58 micromoles\npH 9: 29 micromoles\npH 11: 6 micromoles\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only pH 7, the optimal level identified in Experiment 1, students varied the reaction temperature to determine its effect on enzyme activity. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\n20 C: 41 micromoles\n30 C: 55 micromoles\n37 C: 62 micromoles\n45 C: 48 micromoles\n55 C: 12 micromoles\n\nStudents noted that at 55 C, microscopic examination of the enzyme sample showed signs of structural breakdown, a process called denaturation, in which the enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape becomes distorted and its function is impaired.',
    questionText: 'According to Experiment 1, what was the difference in sugar production between pH 3 and pH 7?',
    options: [
      '44 micromoles',
      '50 micromoles',
      '54 micromoles',
      '58 micromoles',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'C',
    explanation: 'At pH 3, 8 micromoles of sugar were produced, and at pH 7, 62 micromoles were produced. The difference is 62 - 8 = 54 micromoles. ACT Tip: When subtracting two data values to find a difference, write out the subtraction explicitly rather than estimating, since a small arithmetic slip can lead to selecting an incorrect but plausible answer choice.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-emi-031',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Biochemistry students conducted two experiments to study how pH affects the activity of an enzyme that breaks down starch into simpler sugars, measuring enzyme activity as the amount of sugar produced (in micromoles) from a fixed starch sample over a 10-minute reaction period.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested the enzyme\'s activity at six different pH levels, holding temperature constant at 37 C. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\npH 3: 8 micromoles\npH 5: 34 micromoles\npH 7: 62 micromoles\npH 8: 58 micromoles\npH 9: 29 micromoles\npH 11: 6 micromoles\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only pH 7, the optimal level identified in Experiment 1, students varied the reaction temperature to determine its effect on enzyme activity. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\n20 C: 41 micromoles\n30 C: 55 micromoles\n37 C: 62 micromoles\n45 C: 48 micromoles\n55 C: 12 micromoles\n\nStudents noted that at 55 C, microscopic examination of the enzyme sample showed signs of structural breakdown, a process called denaturation, in which the enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape becomes distorted and its function is impaired.',
    questionText: 'Based on Experiments 1 and 2 together, which of the following statements is best supported by the data?',
    options: [
      'The enzyme functions best under highly acidic conditions regardless of temperature',
      'The enzyme shows an optimal range for both pH and temperature, with activity declining when either factor moves too far from its respective optimal point in either direction',
      'Temperature has no measurable effect on enzyme activity once pH is held constant',
      'The enzyme\'s activity increases indefinitely as temperature rises, with no upper limit',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'Experiment 1 shows activity peaking at pH 7 and declining at both more acidic (pH 3, 5) and more basic (pH 8, 9, 11) levels, while Experiment 2 shows activity peaking at 37 C and declining at both lower (20 C, 30 C) and higher (45 C, 55 C) temperatures, directly supporting this combined statement about optimal ranges for both variables. Experiment 1 shows very low activity at the most acidic pH tested, pH 3 (8 micromoles), directly contradicting a claim that the enzyme functions best under highly acidic conditions. Experiment 2 explicitly shows substantial, measurable changes in activity across different temperatures, directly contradicting a claim that temperature has no measurable effect. Experiment 2 shows activity declining sharply after 37 C, directly contradicting a claim that activity increases indefinitely with no upper limit. ACT Tip: When a question asks about a claim supported by two experiments together, verify that each part of the combined statement is separately supported by its corresponding experiment\'s specific data pattern.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-sci-032',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Scientific Investigation',
    difficulty: Difficulty.medium,
    passageText: 'Biochemistry students conducted two experiments to study how pH affects the activity of an enzyme that breaks down starch into simpler sugars, measuring enzyme activity as the amount of sugar produced (in micromoles) from a fixed starch sample over a 10-minute reaction period.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested the enzyme\'s activity at six different pH levels, holding temperature constant at 37 C. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\npH 3: 8 micromoles\npH 5: 34 micromoles\npH 7: 62 micromoles\npH 8: 58 micromoles\npH 9: 29 micromoles\npH 11: 6 micromoles\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only pH 7, the optimal level identified in Experiment 1, students varied the reaction temperature to determine its effect on enzyme activity. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\n20 C: 41 micromoles\n30 C: 55 micromoles\n37 C: 62 micromoles\n45 C: 48 micromoles\n55 C: 12 micromoles\n\nStudents noted that at 55 C, microscopic examination of the enzyme sample showed signs of structural breakdown, a process called denaturation, in which the enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape becomes distorted and its function is impaired.',
    questionText: 'Why did students use pH 7 specifically, rather than any other pH level, when conducting Experiment 2?',
    options: [
      'Because pH 7 was identified in Experiment 1 as the optimal pH level for maximum enzyme activity',
      'Because pH 7 represents the pH of pure water and was chosen arbitrarily',
      'Because pH 7 was the only pH level available in the laboratory during Experiment 2',
      'Because pH 7 causes the enzyme to denature immediately upon contact',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'A',
    explanation: 'The passage explicitly states that Experiment 2 used "only pH 7, the optimal level identified in Experiment 1," directly explaining why this specific pH was selected for the temperature experiment. While it is true that pH 7 is the pH of pure water, the passage does not state this was the reason for its selection; it explicitly attributes the choice to the results of Experiment 1, not to this incidental chemical fact. The passage gives no indication that pH 7 was the only pH level available; Experiment 1 explicitly tested six different pH levels using the same laboratory setup. The passage does not state that pH 7 causes denaturation; denaturation is instead described as occurring due to high temperature in Experiment 2, unrelated to the pH level itself. ACT Tip: When an experiment uses a specific condition identified as "optimal" from a previous experiment, that connection is typically the explicit, stated reason for the choice, rather than an incidental or unrelated property of that condition.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-emi-033',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.hard,
    passageText: 'Biochemistry students conducted two experiments to study how pH affects the activity of an enzyme that breaks down starch into simpler sugars, measuring enzyme activity as the amount of sugar produced (in micromoles) from a fixed starch sample over a 10-minute reaction period.\n\nExperiment 1\nStudents tested the enzyme\'s activity at six different pH levels, holding temperature constant at 37 C. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\npH 3: 8 micromoles\npH 5: 34 micromoles\npH 7: 62 micromoles\npH 8: 58 micromoles\npH 9: 29 micromoles\npH 11: 6 micromoles\n\nExperiment 2\nUsing only pH 7, the optimal level identified in Experiment 1, students varied the reaction temperature to determine its effect on enzyme activity. Sugar produced after 10 minutes:\n20 C: 41 micromoles\n30 C: 55 micromoles\n37 C: 62 micromoles\n45 C: 48 micromoles\n55 C: 12 micromoles\n\nStudents noted that at 55 C, microscopic examination of the enzyme sample showed signs of structural breakdown, a process called denaturation, in which the enzyme\'s three-dimensional shape becomes distorted and its function is impaired.',
    questionText: 'A student proposes that enzyme activity increases continuously as pH increases from acidic to basic, with no decline at any point. Do the results of Experiment 1 support this proposal?',
    options: [
      'Yes, because sugar production increased at every pH level tested from pH 3 to pH 11',
      'No, because sugar production increased from pH 3 up to pH 7 but then decreased steadily from pH 7 through pH 11, indicating an optimal point rather than a continuous increase',
      'Yes, because pH 11, the most basic level tested, also produced the highest sugar production',
      'No, because pH was not actually varied in Experiment 1',
    ],
    correctAnswer: 'B',
    explanation: 'The data in Experiment 1 shows sugar production rising from 8 micromoles at pH 3 to a peak of 62 micromoles at pH 7, but then falling steadily to 58, 29, and finally 6 micromoles at pH 8, 9, and 11 respectively, directly contradicting a claim of continuous increase and instead showing a clear optimal point followed by decline. Sugar production did not increase at every pH level tested; it clearly decreased from pH 7 onward, making the first option factually incorrect based on the data. pH 11, the most basic level tested, produced only 6 micromoles, the lowest value in the entire data set, directly contradicting the claim that it produced the highest sugar production. pH was explicitly varied across six distinct levels in Experiment 1, directly contradicting the claim that it was not varied. ACT Tip: To evaluate a claim of a continuous or indefinite trend across a data set, check the data across its full range, including the highest and lowest tested values, rather than assuming an early increasing pattern continues unchanged throughout.',
  ),
  ActQuestion(
    id: 's03-science-emi-034',
    setNumber: 3,
    section: ActSection.science,
    skillArea: 'Evaluation of Models, Inferences, and Experimental Results',
    difficulty: Difficulty.easy,
    passageText: 'Beginning around 50,000 to 10,000 years ago, a large number of large-bodied mammal species, often called megafauna, went extinct across multiple continents, including mammoths, giant ground sloths, saber-toothed cats, and many others. Two scientists offer differing explanations for the primary cause of this widespread wave of extinctions.\n\nScientist 1: Human Overhunting\nThe primary driver of megafauna extinction was overhunting by expanding populations of anatomically modern humans as they migrated into new continents and regions previously unoccupied by an efficient, technologically sophisticated human predator. Large-bodied mammals in these newly colonized regions had evolved without exposure to human hunting pressure and consequently lacked appropriate behavioral wariness toward this new predator, making them disproportionately vulnerable compared to smaller, faster-reproducing species better able to withstand additional predation pressure. This hypothesis is strongly supported by the close temporal correlation observed on multiple continents between the arrival of humans, as established through archaeological dating, and the subsequent extinction of local megafauna populations, a correlation that holds with notable consistency across Australia, the Americas, and various islands, each colonized by humans at different times, with megafauna extinctions following human arrival in each region within a roughly similar timeframe after colonization. Additionally, direct archaeological evidence, including butchered megafauna bones found alongside human tools and hunting sites, confirms that humans did actively hunt at least some of these now-extinct large species, even if the full scale of hunting\'s population-level impact remains harder to establish with certainty from the archaeological record alone.\n\nScientist 2: Climate Change\nWhile human hunting undoubtedly occurred and affected some individual megafauna populations, it cannot fully account for the overall pattern and timing of extinctions observed globally. The primary cause was instead the dramatic climate instability characterizing the transition out of the last glacial period, during which rapid, repeated shifts in temperature and precipitation patterns transformed vast stretches of the specific grassland and steppe habitats that many megafauna species had specifically adapted to depend upon, replacing these habitats with forests or different vegetation types poorly suited to supporting the same large grazing and browsing populations these species required. This explanation is supported by the observation that several megafauna extinctions, particularly in regions like Eurasia, began before well-documented, substantial human population expansion into those specific regions, suggesting climate-driven habitat transformation alone was sufficient to drive at least some of these extinctions independent of significant human hunting pressure. Additionally, the fossil record indicates that many megafauna species had already survived multiple previous glacial-interglacial climate cycles over hundreds of thousands of years without going extinct, but proponents of the climate hypothesis argue this most recent transition was climatologically distinct in its speed and severity compared to earlier cycles, a distinction that may help explain why this particular climate transition, unlike earlier ones these species had previously survived, coincided with such widespread extinction.',
    questionText: 'According to Scientist 1, what specific archaeological evidence supports the human overhunting hypothesis?',
    options: [
      'Fossil pollen records showing vegetation change',
      'Butchered megafauna bones found alongside human tools and hunting sites',
